399be801-2626-470b-8fed-892493a02a14	06d73554-562d-4cd9-8562-a2dd2a852b60	352d7e21-5155-4ff8-8169-284bf5ece3e7	0.3	0	3	approved	\N	\N	\N	\N	777775a349605948956764d0ccc14f375a17ebd95014f7f9e7979dadf78762d5	1. Purpose\nThis SOP defines the COC process.\n2. Scope\nAll manufacturing units.\n3. Procedure\n3.1 Initial steps	\N	Added purpose and scope sections via COC module	\N	4ccf68ad-485c-495a-935b-10d74b173a52	2026-03-18 06:03:33.823877+00	\N	\N	4ccf68ad-485c-495a-935b-10d74b173a52	2026-03-18 05:32:33.905095+00	platform	\N
1dbe4284-2ebd-4407-a2c6-60ae24b4f3fc	df81fe2e-d06d-44db-b8ea-1554a2fafc41	352d7e21-5155-4ff8-8169-284bf5ece3e7	1.0	1	0	approved	\N	\N	\N	\N	6a1c295ea4d8558d950107f5e22d396a293080bf6a11b79d3520d76b1cc96262	Complete rewrite of sterile manufacturing SOP per new regulations.	\N	Major revision per EU GMP Annex 1 update	regulatory_update	\N	\N	\N	\N	4ccf68ad-485c-495a-935b-10d74b173a52	2026-03-02 15:55:47.479611+00	platform	\N
83d9e338-521b-4358-9e59-4ee7acbd51f2	09693b1c-d31d-4784-82c9-8ef69a1c3331	352d7e21-5155-4ff8-8169-284bf5ece3e7	0.1	0	1	approved	FileAttachmentService-Forma.docx	352d7e21-5155-4ff8-8169-284bf5ece3e7/documents/2026/03/420c7f2c-037b-4fdf-b875-046f942635f0_fileattachmentservice-forma.docx	1158013	application/vnd.openxmlformats-officedocument.wordprocessingml.document	4d72bf55d5e825ad06606ac236f3594ce525363f27d5edfe5568727dfb2ad3ac	SERVICE SPECIFICATION\n\nFile Attachment Services\n\n\n\nQAssure Multi-Tenant SaaS Platform\n\nVersion 1.0   ·   February 2026\n\n\n\n1. Feature of Service\n\nSERVICE NAME: FILE ATTACHMENT SERVICES\n\nCentralised, tenant-isolated, binary file lifecycle engine that governs how files (documents, images, reports, specifications, audit evidence, training materials) are uploaded, stored, versioned, downloaded, previewed, shared, and deleted across multi-tenant regulated environments. All binary file operations across the QAssure platform and module applications are routed through this single service.\n\n\n\nCore capabilities:\n\n\n\n1. File Upload & Storage --- Single and multi-file upload (up to 10 files per request) with multer memory storage, 100MB max file size enforcement, blocked dangerous extensions (.exe, .bat, .cmd, .sh, .ps1, .msi, .dll, .com, .scr, .vbs, .js), SHA-256 checksum calculation via crypto.createHash('sha256'), and automatic tenant-scoped folder hierarchy: {tenant_id}/{entity_type}/{YYYY}/{MM}/{uuid}_{sanitized_name}.ext. Files are linked to any platform entity via entity_type + entity_id (e.g., entity_type='documents', entity_id=document_uuid). Source module is auto-derived from authentication context: module code for combined JWT + API Key mode (e.g., 'coc', 'dhf') where module identity is captured from the API key even though user identity comes from JWT; 'platform' for JWT-only users; moduleCode for API key-only modules (e.g., 'aice', 'dhf'); or 'api_key' as fallback if an API key has no linked module. Priority order: req.module.code (combined mode) → req.user.moduleCode (API key mode) → 'platform' (JWT mode). Duplicate detection prevents re-upload of identical files to the same entity (enforced by checksum + entity match on active records).\n\n2. File Versioning --- Each file supports immutable version history. Uploading a new version via POST /:id/versions marks the previous version as is_current_version=false and creates a new record with incremented version number, linked via previous_version_id. Version history is retrieved via recursive CTE (Common Table Expression) that traverses the chain in both directions, returning complete lineage. Only the current version is returned in standard queries.\n\n3. Download & Preview --- Download endpoint (GET /:id/download) serves files with Content-Disposition: attachment header for browser download prompt. Preview endpoint (GET /:id/preview) serves with Content-Disposition: inline for in-browser rendering. Both use storageProvider.getFullPath() with path.resolve() for absolute path resolution. File access is logged in file_access_logs table with user_id, IP address, user agent, and action type.\n\n4. File Sharing with Token-Based Access --- Create time-limited, download-limited share links via POST /:id/share. Generates 64-character hex token via crypto.randomBytes(32). Supports configurable parameters: expires_at (absolute timestamp), expires_in_hours (convenience --- auto-converted to expires_at), max_downloads (download cap), share_type ('link' or 'user'), shared_with_user_id, shared_with_email, and permissions ('view' or 'download'). Access via GET /shared/:token is a PUBLIC route (no authentication required). The service validates token existence, checks expiry, enforces max_downloads limit, increments current_downloads counter, and serves the file. Share revocation via DELETE /shares/:shareId deactivates the share.\n\n5. Storage Quota Management --- Per-tenant storage quotas tracked in storage_quotas table: max_storage_bytes (default 10GB), used_storage_bytes, file_count, max_file_size_bytes (default 100MB), allowed_mime_types, blocked_extensions. Quota auto-creates on first upload if not present. PostgreSQL trigger (trg_update_storage_usage) automatically increments/decrements used_storage_bytes and file_count on INSERT, DELETE, and UPDATE (soft-delete via SET deleted_at). Quota is checked before every upload: individual file size vs max_file_size_bytes, and total used + new file vs max_storage_bytes. Admin-only PUT /quota endpoint allows updating quota limits (requires files:admin permission).\n\n6. Soft Delete with Hard Delete Option --- Default DELETE /:id performs soft delete (sets deleted_at timestamp). PostgreSQL trigger automatically decrements quota on soft delete. Hard delete via DELETE /:id?hard=true permanently removes the database record AND the physical file from storage via storageProvider.delete(). Orphaned file cleanup: if database INSERT fails after file is saved to disk, the file is automatically cleaned up in the catch block.\n\n7. Storage Provider Abstraction (S3-Ready) --- All file I/O goes through LocalStorageProvider with 4 methods: save(relativePath, buffer), delete(relativePath), exists(relativePath), getFullPath(relativePath). No controller or service code touches the filesystem directly. Migrating to S3 requires implementing an S3StorageProvider with the same interface --- zero code changes in controllers or service logic.\n\n8. Three Authentication Modes --- Every endpoint (except public share access) supports three authentication modes via authenticate middleware: (a) JWT only --- QAssure UI users authenticate via Authorization: Bearer header; tenant derived from JWT claims; user permissions enforced via authorize(); (b) JWT + API Key combined --- module applications acting on behalf of a logged-in user send both Authorization: Bearer (JWT from verify-launch-token) and X-API-Key headers; tenant derived from JWT (X-Tenant-ID not needed); user permissions enforced (not module bypass); source_module set from module identity (e.g., 'coc', 'dhf') for accurate audit attribution; (c) API Key + X-Tenant-ID --- module system/cron operations with no user context send X-API-Key and X-Tenant-ID headers; module gets full access bypass on authorize(). This enables QAssure UI users, module applications acting on behalf of users (with real user identity in audit trails), and module background processes to consume the file attachment service. Routes use authorize() for permission checks: files:read for GET endpoints, files:write for POST/PUT/DELETE endpoints, files:admin for quota management.\n\n9. Multi-Tenant Data Isolation --- All queries are tenant-scoped. Every file, share, quota, and access log belongs to a tenant_id. API Key middleware validates module-tenant assignment via the tenant_module_access table (enabled = true) before granting access. Missing X-Tenant-ID returns error code MISSING_TENANT. Unassigned tenant returns MODULE_NOT_ASSIGNED. In combined mode (JWT + API Key), if the JWT's tenant does not match the API key's assigned tenant, error code TENANT_MISMATCH is returned; if the module is deactivated for the tenant, error code MODULE_NOT_ACTIVE is returned. Users cannot see or access files belonging to other tenants under any circumstance.\n\n10. Security Hardening --- UUID validation middleware on all :id route parameters returns 400 (INVALID_ID_FORMAT) for malformed UUIDs instead of leaking PostgreSQL errors. Error sanitization function (sanitizeErrorResponse) on all 17 controller catch blocks classifies errors into client-safe categories (QUOTA_ERROR, NOT_FOUND, VALIDATION_ERROR, etc.) and returns generic 'An internal error occurred' for unexpected failures --- never exposing constraint names, table structures, or query details. Blocked file extensions prevent executable uploads. Path traversal is impossible because stored names use UUID-based naming, not user-controlled paths.\n\n11. Observability --- Redis caching with graceful degradation (file cache 300s TTL, quota 60s, share 3600s, entity files 120s). Elasticsearch indexing for file searchability. File access logging with IP/user-agent tracking. Storage statistics with per-entity-type and per-module breakdowns. All infrastructure dependencies (Redis, Elasticsearch) fail gracefully --- the service continues operating without caching or indexing if either is unavailable.\n\n\n\n2. Scope\n\nIN SCOPE (Phase 1):\n\nFull file CRUD: upload single file (with auto-generated tenant-scoped storage path, checksum, source_module derivation), upload multiple files (up to 10, parallel via Promise.all), read (single by ID with Redis cache, paginated list with filtering, by entity with pagination), update metadata (description, tags, category, metadata JSONB), soft delete (with quota trigger), hard delete (with physical file removal).\n\nFile versioning: upload new version (marks previous as not current, increments version number, links via previous_version_id), retrieve version history (recursive CTE traversal in both directions).\n\nDownload and preview with correct Content-Disposition headers (attachment for download, inline for preview), file access logging, and path resolution via storageProvider abstraction.\n\nFile sharing: create share link with configurable expiry (expires_at or expires_in_hours), max_downloads cap, share_type (link/user), permissions (view/download). Public access via token (no auth required). Share revocation. Expired/exhausted shares return 404.\n\nStorage quota management: auto-create on first upload, per-tenant limits (max_storage_bytes, max_file_size_bytes, allowed_mime_types, blocked_extensions), quota enforcement on every upload, admin-only quota configuration updates (files:admin permission), PostgreSQL trigger for automatic used_storage_bytes/file_count tracking on INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE.\n\nThree authentication modes (JWT only, JWT + API Key combined, API Key + X-Tenant-ID) on all 16 authenticated endpoints via authenticate middleware (1 public route excluded: GET /shared/:token). Combined mode derives tenant from JWT (X-Tenant-ID not needed), enforces user permissions, and sets source_module from module identity for accurate audit attribution.\n\nMulti-tenant isolation on all read and write operations.\n\nAPI Key middleware validates module-tenant access via tenant_module_access table.\n\nUUID validation middleware on all :id parameters --- returns 400 for invalid UUIDs.\n\nError sanitization on all 17 catch blocks --- never leaks database internals.\n\nFile access audit trail via file_access_logs with user_id, IP address, user agent, action.\n\nStorage statistics with quota summary, file type counts, by_entity and by_module breakdowns.\n\nPagination via limit and offset query parameters (default limit 50). Response is a JSON array of file objects.\n\nFilter support on list: source_module, mime_type, entity_type, category, search (ILIKE on original_name), sort (created_at, file_size, original_name), date range (created_after, created_before).\n\nOrphaned file cleanup: if DB INSERT fails after file save, file is automatically removed from storage.\n\nRedis caching with graceful degradation (operates without Redis).\n\nElasticsearch indexing with graceful degradation (operates without ES).\n\n\n\nOUT OF SCOPE (Phase 1 --- File Attachment Service boundary):\n\nS3/MinIO object storage migration --- LocalStorageProvider is production-ready; S3StorageProvider is a config swap planned for Phase 2. Storage provider abstraction is already in place.\n\nVirus/malware scanning --- scan_status and scanned_at columns exist in file_attachments table but no scanning service is integrated.\n\nImage thumbnail generation --- has_thumbnail, thumbnail_path columns exist but no thumbnail generation pipeline is implemented.\n\nFull-text content extraction from uploaded files (PDF text extraction, OCR).\n\nVector embedding of file content for semantic search via Qdrant.\n\nReal-time file upload progress (WebSocket-based progress tracking).\n\nClient-side chunked upload for files larger than 100MB.\n\nCDN integration for cached file delivery.\n\nFile format conversion (PDF to image, document to PDF, etc.).\n\nWatermarking or digital stamping of downloaded files.\n\nBulk import/export of files.\n\nFile-level access control lists (ACLs) --- access is controlled at entity level via entity_type permissions, not per-file.\n\nRetention policy enforcement --- soft-deleted files remain in database indefinitely until hard-deleted.\n\nEmail/SMS notifications on file events (handled by Notification Service separately).\n\nDocument lifecycle management (SOPs, versioning, review/approval) --- handled by separate Document Service (document.service.js).\n\n\n\n3. Clear Responsibility Split\n\n3.1 SaaS Platform (Owned by Platform Team):\n\nProvide and maintain the File Attachment Service as a centralised, tenant-isolated binary file management engine.\n\nEnforce file upload constraints: 100MB max file size (multer + quota), blocked dangerous extensions, per-tenant storage quotas with auto-creation and trigger-based tracking.\n\nGenerate and manage tenant-scoped storage paths: {tenant_id}/{entity_type}/{YYYY}/{MM}/{uuid}_{sanitized_name}.ext.\n\nCalculate SHA-256 checksums on every upload for integrity verification.\n\nAuto-derive source_module from authentication context with priority: req.module.code for combined JWT + API Key mode (e.g., 'coc', 'dhf'), req.user.moduleCode for API key-only modules, 'platform' for JWT-only users, 'api_key' fallback for API keys without linked modules. This ensures files uploaded by modules on behalf of users are correctly attributed to the originating module in storage statistics.\n\nManage file versioning: version number increment, previous_version_id linking, is_current_version flagging, recursive CTE version history retrieval.\n\nMaintain storage provider abstraction (LocalStorageProvider) with save, delete, exists, getFullPath methods for future S3 migration.\n\nEnforce tenant isolation on all queries via tenant_id filtering. No cross-tenant file access is possible.\n\nProvide three authentication modes on all 16 authenticated endpoints via authenticate middleware (1 public route excluded: GET /shared/:token): (a) JWT only for QAssure UI users, (b) JWT + API Key combined for module applications acting on behalf of logged-in users (tenant from JWT, user permissions enforced, source_module from module identity), (c) API Key + X-Tenant-ID for module system/cron operations.\n\nValidate module-tenant access via tenant_module_access table (enabled = true check). Return MISSING_TENANT or MODULE_NOT_ASSIGNED errors for invalid API Key requests. In combined mode, return TENANT_MISMATCH if JWT tenant differs from API key's assigned tenant, and MODULE_NOT_ACTIVE if the module is deactivated for the tenant.\n\nProvide UUID validation middleware for all route parameters. Return 400 INVALID_ID_FORMAT for malformed UUIDs.\n\nSanitise all error responses to prevent database internal leakage.\n\nProvide file sharing infrastructure: token generation, expiry enforcement, max_downloads enforcement, public access route.\n\nProvide storage statistics with per-entity-type and per-module breakdowns.\n\nLog all file access events (download, preview, checkout, checkin) in file_access_logs.\n\nProvide Redis caching and Elasticsearch indexing with graceful degradation.\n\nExpose admin-only quota management endpoint (files:admin permission).\n\n\n\n3.2 Module Applications (Residing on Platform; Owned by App Teams --- LTTS):\n\nUse the File Attachment Service API with the appropriate authentication mode:\n\nFor user-context operations (user uploads a file via module UI): Send both Authorization: Bearer (JWT obtained from verify-launch-token) and X-API-Key headers. X-Tenant-ID is NOT needed --- tenant is derived from JWT. User permissions (files:read, files:write) are enforced. uploaded_by and file_access_logs.user_id record the real user identity. source_module records the module identity (e.g., 'coc').\n\nFor system/cron operations (no user context): Send X-API-Key + X-Tenant-ID headers. Module gets full access bypass. source_module records the module identity from API key.\n\nSpecify entity_type and entity_id when uploading files to link them to the correct module entity (e.g., entity_type='documents', entity_id=<document_uuid> for Document Service; entity_type='capa', entity_id=<capa_uuid> for CAPA module).\n\nProvide required fields on upload: file (multipart), entity_type (string), entity_id (UUID). Optional fields: description, category, tags, metadata (JSONB).\n\nUse the version upload endpoint (POST /:id/versions) for updating files rather than deleting and re-uploading. This preserves version history and audit trail.\n\nUse the share endpoint to generate time-limited, download-limited links for external stakeholders (e.g., auditors, suppliers) without granting platform login access.\n\nDo NOT implement custom file storage logic --- always delegate to platform's file attachment service.\n\nDo NOT store file metadata in the module's own database --- use platform as the system of record for all file attachments. Use the entity link (entity_type + entity_id) to associate files with module records.\n\nDo NOT construct file paths manually or access the uploads directory directly --- always use service API endpoints for file access.\n\nUse SSO launch token flow to obtain both tenant context and user session JWT dynamically in production: call POST /modules/verify-launch-token with X-API-Key and the launch token to receive a JWT (24h expiry), user details (including department, designation, user category), tenant details, and permissions. Use this JWT in Authorization: Bearer header alongside X-API-Key for all user-context platform calls. Use .env TENANT_ID + X-Tenant-ID header for development and system/cron operations only.\n\n\n\n4. API List (Refer to Excel Sheet shared)\n\n\n\n#\n\nMethod\n\nEndpoint\n\nAuth\n\nDescription\n\n1\n\nPOST\n\n/api/v1/files/upload\n\nauthenticate + files:write\n\nUpload single file (multipart)\n\n2\n\nPOST\n\n/api/v1/files/upload/multiple\n\nauthenticate + files:write\n\nUpload multiple files (up to 10)\n\n3\n\nGET\n\n/api/v1/files\n\nauthenticate + files:read\n\nList files (paginated, filterable)\n\n4\n\nGET\n\n/api/v1/files/:id\n\nauthenticate + files:read + validateUUID\n\nGet single file by ID\n\n5\n\nPUT\n\n/api/v1/files/:id\n\nauthenticate + files:write + validateUUID\n\nUpdate file metadata\n\n6\n\nDELETE\n\n/api/v1/files/:id\n\nauthenticate + files:write + validateUUID\n\nDelete file (soft; ?hard=true for permanent)\n\n7\n\nGET\n\n/api/v1/files/:id/download\n\nauthenticate + files:read + validateUUID\n\nDownload file (Content-Disposition: attachment)\n\n8\n\nGET\n\n/api/v1/files/:id/preview\n\nauthenticate + files:read + validateUUID\n\nPreview file (Content-Disposition: inline)\n\n9\n\nPOST\n\n/api/v1/files/:id/versions\n\nauthenticate + files:write + validateUUID\n\nUpload new version of existing file\n\n10\n\nGET\n\n/api/v1/files/:id/versions\n\nauthenticate + files:read + validateUUID\n\nGet version history (recursive CTE)\n\n11\n\nPOST\n\n/api/v1/files/:id/share\n\nauthenticate + files:write + validateUUID\n\nCreate share link (token, expiry, max_downloads)\n\n12\n\nDELETE\n\n/api/v1/files/shares/:shareId\n\nauthenticate + files:write + validateUUID\n\nRevoke share link\n\n13\n\nGET\n\n/api/v1/files/shared/:token\n\nPUBLIC (no auth)\n\nAccess shared file via token\n\n14\n\nGET\n\n/api/v1/files/entity/:entity_type/:entity_id\n\nauthenticate + files:read + validateUUID\n\nGet files by entity (paginated)\n\n15\n\nGET\n\n/api/v1/files/stats\n\nauthenticate + files:read\n\nGet storage statistics (by entity, by module)\n\n16\n\nGET\n\n/api/v1/files/quota\n\nauthenticate + files:read\n\nGet tenant storage quota\n\n17\n\nPUT\n\n/api/v1/files/quota\n\nauthenticate + files:admin\n\nUpdate tenant storage quota (admin only)\n\n\n\nRoute ordering note: /stats, /quota, /entity/:type/:id, /shared/:token are registered BEFORE /:id to prevent Express matching static segments as UUID parameters.\n\n\n\n5. Typical Workflow\n\n5.1 Write Path (File Upload & Management):\n\n1. Upload Single File: Tenant user or module calls POST /api/v1/files/upload with multipart form data: file (required, binary), entity_type (required, e.g., 'documents'), entity_id (required, UUID of parent entity), and optional: description, category, tags (comma-separated string or JSON array), metadata (JSONB). The API validates: file presence (returns VALIDATION_ERROR 'No file uploaded'), entity_type and entity_id presence (returns VALIDATION_ERROR 'entity_type and entity_id are required'), file extension not in blocked list (.exe, .bat, .cmd, .sh, .ps1, .msi, .dll, .com, .scr, .vbs, .js --- returns error 'File type .exe is not allowed'), file size under 100MB (returns FILE_TOO_LARGE 'File size exceeds 100MB limit'), tenant quota not exceeded (returns QUOTA_ERROR for both individual file and total storage).\n\nPlatform generates: stored_name as {uuid}_{sanitized_original_name}.ext, storage path as {tenant_id}/{entity_type}/{YYYY}/{MM}/{stored_name}, SHA-256 checksum via crypto.createHash('sha256').update(buffer).digest('hex'), source_module derived from auth context with priority: req.module.code for combined mode (e.g., 'coc', 'dhf'), 'platform' for JWT-only users, req.user.moduleCode for API key-only modules (e.g., 'aice', 'dhf'), or 'api_key' fallback if API key has no linked module. File is saved to storage via storageProvider.save(relativePath, buffer), then database record is inserted into file_attachments. If DB INSERT fails, file is automatically cleaned up from storage (orphan prevention). Redis cache is invalidated for entity files list. Elasticsearch index is updated via IndexingHooks. Returns full file object with 201 status.\n\n2. Upload Multiple Files: POST /api/v1/files/upload/multiple accepts up to 10 files via multer array ('files' field, max 10). All files are uploaded in parallel via Promise.all, sharing the same entity_type and entity_id. Returns array of results with per-file success/error status. Quota is checked per file individually.\n\n3. Update Metadata: PUT /api/v1/files/:id updates allowed fields: description (text), category (varchar), tags (cast as text[]), metadata (cast as JSONB). File content changes go through versioning (POST /:id/versions), not metadata update. Sets updated_at timestamp. Returns 'No valid fields to update' error if no recognized fields provided. Returns full file object with 200 status.\n\n4. Soft Delete: DELETE /api/v1/files/:id sets deleted_at = NOW() timestamp. PostgreSQL trigger (trg_update_storage_usage) automatically decrements used_storage_bytes and file_count in storage_quotas using GREATEST(..., 0) to prevent negative values. File remains on disk for potential recovery. Redis cache invalidated. Elasticsearch index updated. Returns { success: true, message: 'File deleted' }.\n\n5. Hard Delete: DELETE /api/v1/files/:id?hard=true permanently removes: database record (DELETE FROM file_attachments WHERE id = $1 AND tenant_id = $2), physical file from storage via storageProvider.delete(file_path), thumbnail if exists via storageProvider.delete(thumbnail_path). Returns 404 on subsequent GET. Irreversible. Quota adjusts via DELETE trigger.\n\n\n\n5.2 Versioning:\n\nMinor Versioning: Default behavior. Every POST /api/v1/files/:id/versions with new file (multipart) performs: fetches current record by ID and tenant_id, marks it as is_current_version=false via UPDATE, inserts new record with version = previous_version + 1, is_current_version=true, previous_version_id = old record ID. SHA-256 checksum is calculated for the new file. Both old and new records remain in database --- version history is immutable and never deleted.\n\nVersion History Retrieval: GET /api/v1/files/:id/versions uses a recursive CTE (Common Table Expression) that traverses the previous_version_id chain in both directions (forward via self-join on previous_version_id, backward via matching previous_version_id = id). Returns complete version lineage with: id, version, file_size, checksum, original_name, uploaded_by_email (resolved via LEFT JOIN users), created_at for each version. Ordered by version number ascending.\n\nVersion Chain Structure: file_attachments.previous_version_id creates a self-referencing linked list. Version 1 has previous_version_id=null. Version 2 points to Version 1. Version 3 points to Version 2. Only the latest version has is_current_version=true. Standard queries (list, get by entity) only return current versions (WHERE is_current_version = true AND deleted_at IS NULL).\n\n\n\n5.3 Read/Search Path:\n\nGet by ID: GET /api/v1/files/:id --- Redis cached (300s TTL). Returns full file object with uploaded_by_email resolved via LEFT JOIN users. Filters by tenant_id for isolation.\n\nList Files: GET /api/v1/files?limit=50&offset=0 --- returns a JSON array of file objects. Supports limit and offset query parameters for pagination. Supports filters: source_module, mime_type, entity_type, category, search (ILIKE on original_name), sort_by (created_at|file_size|original_name), sort_order (ASC|DESC), created_after, created_before. Only returns active files (deleted_at IS NULL, is_current_version = true).\n\nGet by Entity: GET /api/v1/files/entity/:entity_type/:entity_id --- returns a JSON array of all files linked to a specific entity. Used by Document Service, CAPA module, Training module, etc. to retrieve their linked files.\n\nStorage Stats: GET /api/v1/files/stats --- returns four sections: quota (max_storage, used_storage, available, usage_percent), files (total, images, pdfs, documents counts), by_entity (count + size per entity_type), and by_module (count + size per source_module e.g., 'platform', 'coc').\n\nStorage Quota: GET /api/v1/files/quota --- returns current quota status: max_storage_bytes, used_storage_bytes, file_count, max_file_size_bytes, allowed_mime_types, blocked_extensions, usage_percentage.\n\n\n\n5.4 File Sharing Path:\n\nCreate Share: POST /api/v1/files/:id/share with body: share_type ('link'|'user'), permissions ('view'|'download'), expires_at (ISO timestamp) or expires_in_hours (number, auto-converted), max_downloads (integer), shared_with_user_id, shared_with_email. Service generates 64-char hex token via crypto.randomBytes(32). Returns share object with share_token, share_url, expires_at, max_downloads. Redis cached (3600s TTL).\n\nAccess Shared File: GET /api/v1/files/shared/:token --- PUBLIC route (no authentication). Service: looks up share by token (Redis cached), validates expiry (expires_at > NOW()), validates download count (current_downloads < max_downloads), increments current_downloads, resolves file path via storageProvider.getFullPath(), serves file with correct Content-Type and Content-Disposition headers. Returns 404 if share not found, expired, or download limit reached.\n\nRevoke Share: DELETE /api/v1/files/shares/:shareId --- sets is_active=false. Invalidates Redis cache for the share token.\n\n\n\n5.5 Retention/Deletion:\n\nPhase 1: No automated retention policy enforcement. Soft-deleted files remain in database with deleted_at timestamp set. Hard delete permanently removes file and database record.\n\nQuota Recovery: Soft delete triggers automatic quota decrement via PostgreSQL trigger. Restoring a soft-deleted file (clearing deleted_at) would trigger quota increment.\n\nPlanned Phase 2: Configurable retention periods per entity_type. Scheduled cleanup job to hard-delete files past retention period. Archived storage tier for long-term retention at reduced cost.\n\n\n\n5.6 Audit Trail:\n\nfile_access_logs records (inserted on download, preview, and any file access): file_id, tenant_id, user_id, action ('download'|'preview'|'access'), ip_address (from req.ip), user_agent (from req.headers), created_at.\n\nStorage quota changes tracked via PostgreSQL trigger --- every INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE on file_attachments automatically adjusts used_storage_bytes and file_count.\n\nShare access tracked via current_downloads counter and last_accessed_at timestamp on file_shares.\n\nAuthentication mode impacts audit attribution: In combined JWT + API Key mode, uploaded_by and file_access_logs.user_id record the real user identity (from JWT), while source_module records the originating module (from API key). This provides GxP-compliant traceability --- auditors can see both who performed the action and which module facilitated it. In API Key-only mode, uploaded_by records the module service account and source_module records the module code. In JWT-only mode, uploaded_by records the platform user and source_module is set to 'platform'.\n\n\n\n6. PostgreSQL DB --- MODEL\n\nThe File Attachment Service uses 4 PostgreSQL tables: file_attachments (master record with binary file metadata, entity linking, versioning, and storage path), storage_quotas (per-tenant storage limits with trigger-maintained usage counters), file_shares (token-based file sharing with expiry and download limits), and file_access_logs (audit trail for file access events). All tables enforce multi-tenant isolation via tenant_id foreign key with ON DELETE CASCADE. The file_attachments table serves as the central entity. A PostgreSQL trigger function (update_storage_usage) automatically maintains used_storage_bytes and file_count in storage_quotas on INSERT, UPDATE (soft-delete/restore), and DELETE operations.\n\n6.1 ER Diagram:\n\n\n\nFigure 6.1: Entity Relationship Diagram --- File Attachment Service\n\nfile_attachments.tenant_id → tenants.id (ON DELETE CASCADE)\n\nfile_attachments.uploaded_by → users.id\n\nfile_attachments.previous_version_id → file_attachments.id (self-referencing version chain)\n\nstorage_quotas.tenant_id → tenants.id (UNIQUE --- one quota per tenant)\n\nfile_shares.tenant_id → tenants.id, file_shares.file_id → file_attachments.id\n\nfile_access_logs.file_id → file_attachments.id, file_access_logs.user_id → users.id\n\n6.2 Entities & Attributes:\n\n6.2.1 Entity: file_attachments (32 columns)\n\n\n\nAttribute\n\nData Type\n\nConstraint / FK\n\nDescription\n\nExample\n\nid\n\nUUID\n\nPK, DEFAULT gen_random_uuid()\n\nUnique file identifier\n\ndaf3c8ad-...\n\ntenant_id\n\nUUID\n\nNOT NULL, FK → tenants(id) ON DELETE CASCADE, INDEX\n\nTenant isolation\n\nc0000000-...\n\noriginal_name\n\nVARCHAR(500)\n\nNOT NULL\n\nUser-uploaded filename\n\ntest-upload.txt\n\nstored_name\n\nVARCHAR(500)\n\nNOT NULL\n\nUUID-based stored name\n\ndaf3c8ad_test-upload.txt\n\nfile_path\n\nVARCHAR(1000)\n\nNOT NULL\n\nRelative storage path\n\nc000.../documents/2026/02/daf3...txt\n\nmime_type\n\nVARCHAR(255)\n\nNOT NULL\n\nMIME type\n\ntext/plain\n\nfile_size\n\nBIGINT\n\nNOT NULL\n\nFile size in bytes\n\n18\n\nchecksum\n\nVARCHAR(128)\n\nNullable\n\nSHA-256 hash\n\na591a6d40...\n\nstorage_provider\n\nVARCHAR(50)\n\nDEFAULT 'local'\n\nStorage backend\n\nlocal\n\nstorage_bucket\n\nVARCHAR(255)\n\nNullable\n\nS3 bucket (Phase 2)\n\nnull\n\nstorage_region\n\nVARCHAR(100)\n\nNullable\n\nS3 region (Phase 2)\n\nnull\n\nentity_type\n\nVARCHAR(100)\n\nNOT NULL, INDEX\n\nParent entity type\n\ndocuments\n\nentity_id\n\nUUID\n\nNOT NULL, INDEX\n\nParent entity ID\n\nUUID\n\ncategory\n\nVARCHAR(100)\n\nNullable\n\nFile category\n\nspecification\n\ndescription\n\nTEXT\n\nNullable\n\nFile description\n\nTest file upload\n\ntags\n\nTEXT[]\n\nNullable\n\nPostgreSQL text array\n\n{sop,quality}\n\nversion\n\nINTEGER\n\nDEFAULT 1\n\nVersion number\n\n1\n\nis_current_version\n\nBOOLEAN\n\nDEFAULT true, INDEX (partial)\n\nActive version flag\n\ntrue\n\nprevious_version_id\n\nUUID\n\nFK → file_attachments(id), Nullable\n\nVersion chain link\n\nUUID or null\n\nis_public\n\nBOOLEAN\n\nDEFAULT false\n\nPublic access flag\n\nfalse\n\naccess_level\n\nVARCHAR(50)\n\nDEFAULT 'private'\n\nAccess level\n\nprivate\n\ndownload_count\n\nINTEGER\n\nDEFAULT 0\n\nTotal downloads\n\n5\n\nhas_thumbnail\n\nBOOLEAN\n\nDEFAULT false\n\nThumbnail exists\n\nfalse\n\nthumbnail_path\n\nVARCHAR(1000)\n\nNullable\n\nThumbnail storage path\n\nnull\n\nmetadata\n\nJSONB\n\nDEFAULT '{}'\n\nExtensible metadata\n\n{}\n\nscan_status\n\nVARCHAR(50)\n\nNullable\n\nVirus scan status (Phase 2)\n\nnull\n\nscanned_at\n\nTIMESTAMPTZ\n\nNullable\n\nScan timestamp (Phase 2)\n\nnull\n\nuploaded_by\n\nUUID\n\nFK → users(id), Nullable\n\nUploader user ID\n\nUUID\n\nsource_module\n\nVARCHAR(100)\n\nDEFAULT 'platform', INDEX\n\nOrigin module\n\nplatform\n\ncreated_at\n\nTIMESTAMPTZ\n\nDEFAULT NOW()\n\nCreated timestamp\n\nISO datetime\n\nupdated_at\n\nTIMESTAMPTZ\n\nDEFAULT NOW()\n\nUpdated timestamp\n\nISO datetime\n\ndeleted_at\n\nTIMESTAMPTZ\n\nNullable, INDEX\n\nSoft delete timestamp\n\nnull\n\n\n\nNOTES: tags is TEXT[] not JSONB. file_size is BIGINT (returns as string from node-pg, parsed to integer in _formatAttachment). is_current_version partial index filters WHERE deleted_at IS NULL AND is_current_version = TRUE. previous_version_id creates a self-referencing linked list for version chains. source_module is auto-derived from auth context, never user-supplied.\n\n\n\n6.2.2 Entity: storage_quotas (10 columns)\n\n\n\nAttribute\n\nData Type\n\nConstraint / FK\n\nDescription\n\nExample\n\nid\n\nUUID\n\nPK, DEFAULT gen_random_uuid()\n\nQuota record ID\n\nUUID\n\ntenant_id\n\nUUID\n\nNOT NULL, FK → tenants(id), UNIQUE\n\nTenant isolation\n\nc0000000-...\n\nmax_storage_bytes\n\nBIGINT\n\nDEFAULT 10737418240 (10GB)\n\nMax total storage\n\n10737418240\n\nused_storage_bytes\n\nBIGINT\n\nDEFAULT 0\n\nCurrent usage (trigger-maintained)\n\n1536\n\nfile_count\n\nINTEGER\n\nDEFAULT 0\n\nActive file count (trigger-maintained)\n\n15\n\nmax_file_size_bytes\n\nBIGINT\n\nDEFAULT 104857600 (100MB)\n\nMax single file size\n\n104857600\n\nallowed_mime_types\n\nTEXT[]\n\nNullable\n\nWhitelist (null = all allowed)\n\nnull\n\nblocked_extensions\n\nTEXT[]\n\nNullable\n\nBlacklist\n\n{.exe,.bat}\n\ncreated_at\n\nTIMESTAMPTZ\n\nDEFAULT NOW()\n\nCreated timestamp\n\nISO datetime\n\nupdated_at\n\nTIMESTAMPTZ\n\nDEFAULT NOW()\n\nUpdated timestamp\n\nISO datetime\n\n\n\nNOTES: max_storage_bytes and used_storage_bytes are BIGINT. PostgreSQL trigger update_storage_usage() maintains used_storage_bytes and file_count automatically on file_attachments INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE. The trigger uses GREATEST(..., 0) to prevent negative values. Quota auto-creates via service when first upload occurs for a tenant.\n\n\n\nPostgreSQL Trigger Function:\n\nCREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION update_storage_usage() --- Handles INSERT (increment), DELETE (decrement), UPDATE with NEW.deleted_at IS NOT NULL AND OLD.deleted_at IS NULL (soft-delete decrement), UPDATE with NEW.deleted_at IS NULL AND OLD.deleted_at IS NOT NULL (restore increment). Uses GREATEST(used_storage_bytes - file_size, 0) and GREATEST(file_count - 1, 0) to prevent negative values.\n\n6.2.3 Entity: file_shares (15 columns)\n\n\n\nAttribute\n\nData Type\n\nConstraint / FK\n\nDescription\n\nExample\n\nid\n\nUUID\n\nPK, DEFAULT gen_random_uuid()\n\nShare record ID\n\nUUID\n\ntenant_id\n\nUUID\n\nNOT NULL, FK → tenants(id) ON DELETE CASCADE\n\nTenant isolation\n\nc0000000-...\n\nfile_id\n\nUUID\n\nNOT NULL, FK → file_attachments(id) ON DELETE CASCADE\n\nShared file\n\nUUID\n\nshare_token\n\nVARCHAR(255)\n\nNOT NULL, UNIQUE\n\n64-char hex token\n\na3f8c2d1...\n\nshare_type\n\nVARCHAR(50)\n\nDEFAULT 'link'\n\nShare type\n\nlink\n\nshared_with_user_id\n\nUUID\n\nFK → users(id), Nullable\n\nTarget user (for 'user' type)\n\nnull\n\nshared_with_email\n\nVARCHAR(255)\n\nNullable\n\nTarget email\n\nnull\n\npermissions\n\nVARCHAR(50)\n\nDEFAULT 'view'\n\nAccess level\n\nview/download\n\nexpires_at\n\nTIMESTAMPTZ\n\nNullable\n\nExpiry timestamp\n\nISO datetime\n\nmax_downloads\n\nINTEGER\n\nNullable\n\nDownload cap (null = unlimited)\n\n5\n\ncurrent_downloads\n\nINTEGER\n\nDEFAULT 0\n\nDownloads consumed\n\n2\n\nis_active\n\nBOOLEAN\n\nDEFAULT true\n\nActive flag (false = revoked)\n\ntrue\n\nlast_accessed_at\n\nTIMESTAMPTZ\n\nNullable\n\nLast access time\n\nISO datetime\n\ncreated_by\n\nUUID\n\nFK → users(id), Nullable\n\nShare creator\n\nUUID\n\ncreated_at\n\nTIMESTAMPTZ\n\nDEFAULT NOW()\n\nCreated timestamp\n\nISO datetime\n\n\n\nNOTES: share_token is UNIQUE. expires_at can be set directly or auto-calculated from expires_in_hours (service converts: new Date(Date.now() + hours * 3600000).toISOString()). max_downloads null means unlimited. current_downloads is incremented atomically on each access. is_active=false means share has been revoked.\n\n\n\n6.2.4 Entity: file_access_logs (8 columns)\n\n\n\nAttribute\n\nData Type\n\nConstraint / FK\n\nDescription\n\nExample\n\nid\n\nUUID\n\nPK, DEFAULT gen_random_uuid()\n\nLog record ID\n\nUUID\n\ntenant_id\n\nUUID\n\nNOT NULL, FK → tenants(id) ON DELETE CASCADE\n\nTenant isolation\n\nUUID\n\nfile_id\n\nUUID\n\nNOT NULL, FK → file_attachments(id) ON DELETE CASCADE\n\nFile accessed\n\nUUID\n\nuser_id\n\nUUID\n\nNOT NULL, FK → users(id)\n\nWho accessed\n\nUUID\n\naction\n\nVARCHAR(50)\n\nNOT NULL\n\nAction type\n\ndownload/preview/access\n\nip_address\n\nINET\n\nNullable\n\nClient IP\n\n192.168.1.1\n\nuser_agent\n\nTEXT\n\nNullable\n\nBrowser UA string\n\nMozilla/5.0...\n\ncreated_at\n\nTIMESTAMPTZ\n\nDEFAULT NOW()\n\nWhen accessed\n\nISO datetime\n\n\n\nNOTES: Records are insert-only (append-only audit log). ip_address and user_agent are captured from Express request object when available. action values include 'download', 'preview', 'access' for share access.\n\n\n\n7. Qdrant (Vector DB)\n\nPhase 1 Status: NOT USED by File Attachment Service.\n\nBinary file content is stored on the filesystem via LocalStorageProvider. File metadata is stored in PostgreSQL (file_attachments table). No vector embeddings of file content are generated in Phase 1.\n\n\n\nPlanned for Phase 2:\n\nCollection Name: file_content_vec_v1\n\nVector Dimension: 1536 (OpenAI text-embedding-3-small) or 768 (sentence-transformers)\n\nPayload Fields: file_id (string), tenant_id (string --- mandatory filter), original_name (string), entity_type (string), entity_id (string), mime_type (string), category (string), source_module (string), created_at (ISO string)\n\nPayload Indexes: tenant_id, entity_type, mime_type, source_module, created_at\n\nUse Case: Semantic search across extracted file content (PDF text, document text), similar file discovery, AI-powered content recommendations, duplicate detection via vector similarity rather than checksum matching.\n\nPrerequisite: Content extraction pipeline (PDF text extraction, OCR for images, document parsing) must be implemented first to generate text from binary files before vector embedding.\n\n\n\n8. Object Storage\n\nFolder Structure Hierarchy:\n\nRoot: UPLOAD_DIR (default: ./uploads/, configurable via environment variable)\n\nPattern: {UPLOAD_DIR}/{tenant_id}/{entity_type}/{YYYY}/{MM}/{uuid}_{sanitized_name}.ext\n\n\n\nExample hierarchy:\n\nuploads/\n\n  c0000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001/   (Tenant: Pharma Corp)\n\n    documents/   (Entity type)\n\n      2026/   (Year)\n\n        02/   (Month)\n\n          daf3c8ad-3660-4c13-8b57-0daf3cc9aaf0_test-upload.txt\n\n          d7cbcb77-a8f1-4e22-b901-3dc77f8e22a1_report-v2.pdf\n\n    capa/\n\n      2026/\n\n        02/\n\n          f1a2b3c4-..._corrective-action-photo.jpg\n\n    training/\n\n      2026/\n\n        01/\n\n          a9b8c7d6-..._sop-training-material.pdf\n\n  d0000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000002/   (Tenant: Another Org)\n\n    documents/\n\n    suppliers/\n\n    temp/   (Temporary storage)\n\n    thumbnails/   (Thumbnail storage)\n\n\n\nStorage Provider Abstraction:\n\nLocalStorageProvider (Phase 1 --- current):\n\nsave(relativePath, buffer) --- Creates directory tree via fs.mkdirSync({recursive: true}), writes buffer via fs.writeFileSync.\n\ndelete(relativePath) --- Removes file via fs.unlinkSync. Silently ignores if file doesn't exist.\n\nexists(relativePath) --- Checks via fs.existsSync.\n\ngetFullPath(relativePath) --- Joins UPLOAD_DIR + relativePath. Controller uses path.resolve() for absolute path.\n\nS3 Migration Path (Phase 2):\n\nImplement S3StorageProvider with identical interface (save, delete, exists, getFullPath). S3 key structure maps 1:1 to local folder hierarchy: s3://{bucket}/{tenant_id}/{entity_type}/{YYYY}/{MM}/{uuid}_{name}.ext. No changes to service or controller code required --- swap provider via configuration.\n\n\n\nSECTION 10 --- REDIS CACHE DETAILS\n\nFile Attachment Service uses 4 Redis cache key patterns with graceful degradation. The service operates normally if Redis is unavailable --- all cache operations are wrapped in try/catch and failures are logged but never block the main operation.\n\n\n\nCache Key Pattern\n\nPurpose\n\nTTL\n\nInvalidation\n\nfile:{tenant_id}:{file_id}\n\nSingle file metadata cache\n\n300s (5 min)\n\nOn update, delete\n\nquota:{tenant_id}\n\nTenant storage quota\n\n60s (1 min)\n\nOn upload, delete, quota update\n\nshare:{share_token}\n\nShare token lookup\n\n3600s (1 hr)\n\nOn revoke\n\nentity_files:{entity_type}:{entity_id}\n\nFiles by entity list\n\n120s (2 min)\n\nOn upload, delete to entity\n\n\n\nRedis Service Integration:\n\nFile: src/infrastructure/cache/redis.service.js --- singleton instance exported as module.exports = new RedisService().\n\nMethods used: get(key, parseJson=true), set(key, value, ttlSeconds), delete(key). Note: The Redis service uses delete() method, NOT del().\n\nCache invalidation method: _invalidateCache(tenantId, fileId, entityType, entityId) --- deletes all relevant cache keys after any write operation (upload, update, delete, share revoke).\n\nLazy initialization: getRedis() function returns singleton Redis instance on first call, returns null if Redis is unavailable. Every cache operation checks for null before proceeding.\n\n\n\nPlatform-level Redis usage that benefits File Attachment Service indirectly:\n\nSession caching: JWT session data cached by AuthService for fast auth validation on every request.\n\nPermission caching: User permissions cached by PermissionService, used by authorization middleware for files:read/files:write/files:admin checks.\n\n\n\nPlanned Phase 2 enhancements:\n\nFile content cache: Large frequently-accessed files cached in Redis for sub-millisecond retrieval.\n\nShare token bloom filter: Probabilistic check before DB lookup for invalid/revoked tokens.\n\nUpload rate limiting: Per-tenant upload rate tracking via Redis INCR + EXPIRE.	<p>SERVICE SPECIFICATION</p><p><strong>File Attachment Services</strong></p><p>QAssure Multi-Tenant SaaS Platform</p><p>Version 1.0   ·   February 2026</p><h1>1. Feature of Service</h1><h3>SERVICE NAME: FILE ATTACHMENT SERVICES</h3><p>Centralised, tenant-isolated, binary file lifecycle engine that governs how files (documents, images, reports, specifications, audit evidence, training materials) are uploaded, stored, versioned, downloaded, previewed, shared, and deleted across multi-tenant regulated environments. All binary file operations across the QAssure platform and module applications are routed through this single service.</p><p><strong>Core capabilities:</strong></p><p><strong>1. File Upload &amp; Storage</strong> --- Single and multi-file upload (up to 10 files per request) with multer memory storage, 100MB max file size enforcement, blocked dangerous extensions (.exe, .bat, .cmd, .sh, .ps1, .msi, .dll, .com, .scr, .vbs, .js), SHA-256 checksum calculation via crypto.createHash('sha256'), and automatic tenant-scoped folder hierarchy: {tenant_id}/{entity_type}/{YYYY}/{MM}/{uuid}_{sanitized_name}.ext. Files are linked to any platform entity via entity_type + entity_id (e.g., entity_type='documents', entity_id=document_uuid). Source module is auto-derived from authentication context: module code for combined JWT + API Key mode (e.g., 'coc', 'dhf') where module identity is captured from the API key even though user identity comes from JWT; 'platform' for JWT-only users; moduleCode for API key-only modules (e.g., 'aice', 'dhf'); or 'api_key' as fallback if an API key has no linked module. Priority order: req.module.code (combined mode) → req.user.moduleCode (API key mode) → 'platform' (JWT mode). Duplicate detection prevents re-upload of identical files to the same entity (enforced by checksum + entity match on active records).</p><p><strong>2. File Versioning</strong> --- Each file supports immutable version history. Uploading a new version via POST /:id/versions marks the previous version as is_current_version=false and creates a new record with incremented version number, linked via previous_version_id. Version history is retrieved via recursive CTE (Common Table Expression) that traverses the chain in both directions, returning complete lineage. Only the current version is returned in standard queries.</p><p><strong>3. Download &amp; Preview</strong> --- Download endpoint (GET /:id/download) serves files with Content-Disposition: attachment header for browser download prompt. Preview endpoint (GET /:id/preview) serves with Content-Disposition: inline for in-browser rendering. Both use storageProvider.getFullPath() with path.resolve() for absolute path resolution. File access is logged in file_access_logs table with user_id, IP address, user agent, and action type.</p><p><strong>4. File Sharing with Token-Based Access</strong> --- Create time-limited, download-limited share links via POST /:id/share. Generates 64-character hex token via crypto.randomBytes(32). Supports configurable parameters: expires_at (absolute timestamp), expires_in_hours (convenience --- auto-converted to expires_at), max_downloads (download cap), share_type ('link' or 'user'), shared_with_user_id, shared_with_email, and permissions ('view' or 'download'). Access via GET /shared/:token is a PUBLIC route (no authentication required). The service validates token existence, checks expiry, enforces max_downloads limit, increments current_downloads counter, and serves the file. Share revocation via DELETE /shares/:shareId deactivates the share.</p><p><strong>5. Storage Quota Management</strong> --- Per-tenant storage quotas tracked in storage_quotas table: max_storage_bytes (default 10GB), used_storage_bytes, file_count, max_file_size_bytes (default 100MB), allowed_mime_types, blocked_extensions. Quota auto-creates on first upload if not present. PostgreSQL trigger (trg_update_storage_usage) automatically increments/decrements used_storage_bytes and file_count on INSERT, DELETE, and UPDATE (soft-delete via SET deleted_at). Quota is checked before every upload: individual file size vs max_file_size_bytes, and total used + new file vs max_storage_bytes. Admin-only PUT /quota endpoint allows updating quota limits (requires files:admin permission).</p><p><strong>6. Soft Delete with Hard Delete Option</strong> --- Default DELETE /:id performs soft delete (sets deleted_at timestamp). PostgreSQL trigger automatically decrements quota on soft delete. Hard delete via DELETE /:id?hard=true permanently removes the database record AND the physical file from storage via storageProvider.delete(). Orphaned file cleanup: if database INSERT fails after file is saved to disk, the file is automatically cleaned up in the catch block.</p><p><strong>7. Storage Provider Abstraction (S3-Ready)</strong> --- All file I/O goes through LocalStorageProvider with 4 methods: save(relativePath, buffer), delete(relativePath), exists(relativePath), getFullPath(relativePath). No controller or service code touches the filesystem directly. Migrating to S3 requires implementing an S3StorageProvider with the same interface --- zero code changes in controllers or service logic.</p><p><strong>8. Three Authentication Modes</strong> --- Every endpoint (except public share access) supports three authentication modes via authenticate middleware: (a) JWT only --- QAssure UI users authenticate via Authorization: Bearer header; tenant derived from JWT claims; user permissions enforced via authorize(); (b) JWT + API Key combined --- module applications acting on behalf of a logged-in user send both Authorization: Bearer (JWT from verify-launch-token) and X-API-Key headers; tenant derived from JWT (X-Tenant-ID not needed); user permissions enforced (not module bypass); source_module set from module identity (e.g., 'coc', 'dhf') for accurate audit attribution; (c) API Key + X-Tenant-ID --- module system/cron operations with no user context send X-API-Key and X-Tenant-ID headers; module gets full access bypass on authorize(). This enables QAssure UI users, module applications acting on behalf of users (with real user identity in audit trails), and module background processes to consume the file attachment service. Routes use authorize() for permission checks: files:read for GET endpoints, files:write for POST/PUT/DELETE endpoints, files:admin for quota management.</p><p><strong>9. Multi-Tenant Data Isolation</strong> --- All queries are tenant-scoped. Every file, share, quota, and access log belongs to a tenant_id. API Key middleware validates module-tenant assignment via the tenant_module_access table (enabled = true) before granting access. Missing X-Tenant-ID returns error code MISSING_TENANT. Unassigned tenant returns MODULE_NOT_ASSIGNED. In combined mode (JWT + API Key), if the JWT's tenant does not match the API key's assigned tenant, error code TENANT_MISMATCH is returned; if the module is deactivated for the tenant, error code MODULE_NOT_ACTIVE is returned. Users cannot see or access files belonging to other tenants under any circumstance.</p><p><strong>10. Security Hardening</strong> --- UUID validation middleware on all :id route parameters returns 400 (INVALID_ID_FORMAT) for malformed UUIDs instead of leaking PostgreSQL errors. Error sanitization function (sanitizeErrorResponse) on all 17 controller catch blocks classifies errors into client-safe categories (QUOTA_ERROR, NOT_FOUND, VALIDATION_ERROR, etc.) and returns generic 'An internal error occurred' for unexpected failures --- never exposing constraint names, table structures, or query details. Blocked file extensions prevent executable uploads. Path traversal is impossible because stored names use UUID-based naming, not user-controlled paths.</p><p><strong>11. Observability</strong> --- Redis caching with graceful degradation (file cache 300s TTL, quota 60s, share 3600s, entity files 120s). Elasticsearch indexing for file searchability. File access logging with IP/user-agent tracking. Storage statistics with per-entity-type and per-module breakdowns. All infrastructure dependencies (Redis, Elasticsearch) fail gracefully --- the service continues operating without caching or indexing if either is unavailable.</p><h1>2. Scope</h1><h2>IN SCOPE (Phase 1):</h2><p><strong>Full file CRUD:</strong> upload single file (with auto-generated tenant-scoped storage path, checksum, source_module derivation), upload multiple files (up to 10, parallel via Promise.all), read (single by ID with Redis cache, paginated list with filtering, by entity with pagination), update metadata (description, tags, category, metadata JSONB), soft delete (with quota trigger), hard delete (with physical file removal).</p><p><strong>File versioning:</strong> upload new version (marks previous as not current, increments version number, links via previous_version_id), retrieve version history (recursive CTE traversal in both directions).</p><p><strong>Download and preview</strong> with correct Content-Disposition headers (attachment for download, inline for preview), file access logging, and path resolution via storageProvider abstraction.</p><p><strong>File sharing:</strong> create share link with configurable expiry (expires_at or expires_in_hours), max_downloads cap, share_type (link/user), permissions (view/download). Public access via token (no auth required). Share revocation. Expired/exhausted shares return 404.</p><p><strong>Storage quota management:</strong> auto-create on first upload, per-tenant limits (max_storage_bytes, max_file_size_bytes, allowed_mime_types, blocked_extensions), quota enforcement on every upload, admin-only quota configuration updates (files:admin permission), PostgreSQL trigger for automatic used_storage_bytes/file_count tracking on INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE.</p><p><strong>Three authentication modes</strong> (JWT only, JWT + API Key combined, API Key + X-Tenant-ID) on all 16 authenticated endpoints via authenticate middleware (1 public route excluded: GET /shared/:token). Combined mode derives tenant from JWT (X-Tenant-ID not needed), enforces user permissions, and sets source_module from module identity for accurate audit attribution.</p><p>Multi-tenant isolation on all read and write operations.</p><p>API Key middleware validates module-tenant access via tenant_module_access table.</p><p>UUID validation middleware on all :id parameters --- returns 400 for invalid UUIDs.</p><p>Error sanitization on all 17 catch blocks --- never leaks database internals.</p><p>File access audit trail via file_access_logs with user_id, IP address, user agent, action.</p><p>Storage statistics with quota summary, file type counts, by_entity and by_module breakdowns.</p><p>Pagination via limit and offset query parameters (default limit 50). Response is a JSON array of file objects.</p><p>Filter support on list: source_module, mime_type, entity_type, category, search (ILIKE on original_name), sort (created_at, file_size, original_name), date range (created_after, created_before).</p><p>Orphaned file cleanup: if DB INSERT fails after file save, file is automatically removed from storage.</p><p>Redis caching with graceful degradation (operates without Redis).</p><p>Elasticsearch indexing with graceful degradation (operates without ES).</p><h2>OUT OF SCOPE (Phase 1 --- File Attachment Service boundary):</h2><p><strong>S3/MinIO object storage migration</strong> --- LocalStorageProvider is production-ready; S3StorageProvider is a config swap planned for Phase 2. Storage provider abstraction is already in place.</p><p><strong>Virus/malware scanning</strong> --- scan_status and scanned_at columns exist in file_attachments table but no scanning service is integrated.</p><p><strong>Image thumbnail generation</strong> --- has_thumbnail, thumbnail_path columns exist but no thumbnail generation pipeline is implemented.</p><p>Full-text content extraction from uploaded files (PDF text extraction, OCR).</p><p>Vector embedding of file content for semantic search via Qdrant.</p><p>Real-time file upload progress (WebSocket-based progress tracking).</p><p>Client-side chunked upload for files larger than 100MB.</p><p>CDN integration for cached file delivery.</p><p>File format conversion (PDF to image, document to PDF, etc.).</p><p>Watermarking or digital stamping of downloaded files.</p><p>Bulk import/export of files.</p><p><strong>File-level access control lists (ACLs)</strong> --- access is controlled at entity level via entity_type permissions, not per-file.</p><p><strong>Retention policy enforcement</strong> --- soft-deleted files remain in database indefinitely until hard-deleted.</p><p><strong>Email/SMS notifications on file events</strong> (handled by Notification Service separately).</p><p><strong>Document lifecycle management</strong> (SOPs, versioning, review/approval) --- handled by separate Document Service (document.service.js).</p><h1>3. Clear Responsibility Split</h1><h2>3.1 SaaS Platform (Owned by Platform Team):</h2><p>Provide and maintain the File Attachment Service as a centralised, tenant-isolated binary file management engine.</p><p>Enforce file upload constraints: 100MB max file size (multer + quota), blocked dangerous extensions, per-tenant storage quotas with auto-creation and trigger-based tracking.</p><p>Generate and manage tenant-scoped storage paths: {tenant_id}/{entity_type}/{YYYY}/{MM}/{uuid}_{sanitized_name}.ext.</p><p>Calculate SHA-256 checksums on every upload for integrity verification.</p><p>Auto-derive source_module from authentication context with priority: req.module.code for combined JWT + API Key mode (e.g., 'coc', 'dhf'), req.user.moduleCode for API key-only modules, 'platform' for JWT-only users, 'api_key' fallback for API keys without linked modules. This ensures files uploaded by modules on behalf of users are correctly attributed to the originating module in storage statistics.</p><p>Manage file versioning: version number increment, previous_version_id linking, is_current_version flagging, recursive CTE version history retrieval.</p><p>Maintain storage provider abstraction (LocalStorageProvider) with save, delete, exists, getFullPath methods for future S3 migration.</p><p>Enforce tenant isolation on all queries via tenant_id filtering. No cross-tenant file access is possible.</p><p>Provide three authentication modes on all 16 authenticated endpoints via authenticate middleware (1 public route excluded: GET /shared/:token): (a) JWT only for QAssure UI users, (b) JWT + API Key combined for module applications acting on behalf of logged-in users (tenant from JWT, user permissions enforced, source_module from module identity), (c) API Key + X-Tenant-ID for module system/cron operations.</p><p>Validate module-tenant access via tenant_module_access table (enabled = true check). Return MISSING_TENANT or MODULE_NOT_ASSIGNED errors for invalid API Key requests. In combined mode, return TENANT_MISMATCH if JWT tenant differs from API key's assigned tenant, and MODULE_NOT_ACTIVE if the module is deactivated for the tenant.</p><p>Provide UUID validation middleware for all route parameters. Return 400 INVALID_ID_FORMAT for malformed UUIDs.</p><p>Sanitise all error responses to prevent database internal leakage.</p><p>Provide file sharing infrastructure: token generation, expiry enforcement, max_downloads enforcement, public access route.</p><p>Provide storage statistics with per-entity-type and per-module breakdowns.</p><p>Log all file access events (download, preview, checkout, checkin) in file_access_logs.</p><p>Provide Redis caching and Elasticsearch indexing with graceful degradation.</p><p>Expose admin-only quota management endpoint (files:admin permission).</p><h2>3.2 Module Applications (Residing on Platform; Owned by App Teams --- LTTS):</h2><p>Use the File Attachment Service API with the appropriate authentication mode:</p><p><strong>For user-context operations (user uploads a file via module UI):</strong> Send both Authorization: Bearer (JWT obtained from verify-launch-token) and X-API-Key headers. X-Tenant-ID is NOT needed --- tenant is derived from JWT. User permissions (files:read, files:write) are enforced. uploaded_by and file_access_logs.user_id record the real user identity. source_module records the module identity (e.g., 'coc').</p><p><strong>For system/cron operations (no user context):</strong> Send X-API-Key + X-Tenant-ID headers. Module gets full access bypass. source_module records the module identity from API key.</p><p>Specify entity_type and entity_id when uploading files to link them to the correct module entity (e.g., entity_type='documents', entity_id=&lt;document_uuid&gt; for Document Service; entity_type='capa', entity_id=&lt;capa_uuid&gt; for CAPA module).</p><p>Provide required fields on upload: file (multipart), entity_type (string), entity_id (UUID). Optional fields: description, category, tags, metadata (JSONB).</p><p>Use the version upload endpoint (POST /:id/versions) for updating files rather than deleting and re-uploading. This preserves version history and audit trail.</p><p>Use the share endpoint to generate time-limited, download-limited links for external stakeholders (e.g., auditors, suppliers) without granting platform login access.</p><p>Do NOT implement custom file storage logic --- always delegate to platform's file attachment service.</p><p>Do NOT store file metadata in the module's own database --- use platform as the system of record for all file attachments. Use the entity link (entity_type + entity_id) to associate files with module records.</p><p>Do NOT construct file paths manually or access the uploads directory directly --- always use service API endpoints for file access.</p><p>Use SSO launch token flow to obtain both tenant context and user session JWT dynamically in production: call POST /modules/verify-launch-token with X-API-Key and the launch token to receive a JWT (24h expiry), user details (including department, designation, user category), tenant details, and permissions. Use this JWT in Authorization: Bearer header alongside X-API-Key for all user-context platform calls. Use .env TENANT_ID + X-Tenant-ID header for development and system/cron operations only.</p><h1>4. API List (Refer to Excel Sheet shared)</h1><table><thead><tr><th><p><strong>#</strong></p></th><th><p><strong>Method</strong></p></th><th><p><strong>Endpoint</strong></p></th><th><p><strong>Auth</strong></p></th><th><p><strong>Description</strong></p></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><p><strong>1</strong></p></td><td><p><strong>POST</strong></p></td><td><p>/api/v1/files/upload</p></td><td><p>authenticate + files:write</p></td><td><p>Upload single file (multipart)</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>2</strong></p></td><td><p><strong>POST</strong></p></td><td><p>/api/v1/files/upload/multiple</p></td><td><p>authenticate + files:write</p></td><td><p>Upload multiple files (up to 10)</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>3</strong></p></td><td><p><strong>GET</strong></p></td><td><p>/api/v1/files</p></td><td><p>authenticate + files:read</p></td><td><p>List files (paginated, filterable)</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>4</strong></p></td><td><p><strong>GET</strong></p></td><td><p>/api/v1/files/:id</p></td><td><p>authenticate + files:read + validateUUID</p></td><td><p>Get single file by ID</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>5</strong></p></td><td><p><strong>PUT</strong></p></td><td><p>/api/v1/files/:id</p></td><td><p>authenticate + files:write + validateUUID</p></td><td><p>Update file metadata</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>6</strong></p></td><td><p><strong>DELETE</strong></p></td><td><p>/api/v1/files/:id</p></td><td><p>authenticate + files:write + validateUUID</p></td><td><p>Delete file (soft; ?hard=true for permanent)</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>7</strong></p></td><td><p><strong>GET</strong></p></td><td><p>/api/v1/files/:id/download</p></td><td><p>authenticate + files:read + validateUUID</p></td><td><p>Download file (Content-Disposition: attachment)</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>8</strong></p></td><td><p><strong>GET</strong></p></td><td><p>/api/v1/files/:id/preview</p></td><td><p>authenticate + files:read + validateUUID</p></td><td><p>Preview file (Content-Disposition: inline)</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>9</strong></p></td><td><p><strong>POST</strong></p></td><td><p>/api/v1/files/:id/versions</p></td><td><p>authenticate + files:write + validateUUID</p></td><td><p>Upload new version of existing file</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>10</strong></p></td><td><p><strong>GET</strong></p></td><td><p>/api/v1/files/:id/versions</p></td><td><p>authenticate + files:read + validateUUID</p></td><td><p>Get version history (recursive CTE)</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>11</strong></p></td><td><p><strong>POST</strong></p></td><td><p>/api/v1/files/:id/share</p></td><td><p>authenticate + files:write + validateUUID</p></td><td><p>Create share link (token, expiry, max_downloads)</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>12</strong></p></td><td><p><strong>DELETE</strong></p></td><td><p>/api/v1/files/shares/:shareId</p></td><td><p>authenticate + files:write + validateUUID</p></td><td><p>Revoke share link</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>13</strong></p></td><td><p><strong>GET</strong></p></td><td><p>/api/v1/files/shared/:token</p></td><td><p>PUBLIC (no auth)</p></td><td><p>Access shared file via token</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>14</strong></p></td><td><p><strong>GET</strong></p></td><td><p>/api/v1/files/entity/:entity_type/:entity_id</p></td><td><p>authenticate + files:read + validateUUID</p></td><td><p>Get files by entity (paginated)</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>15</strong></p></td><td><p><strong>GET</strong></p></td><td><p>/api/v1/files/stats</p></td><td><p>authenticate + files:read</p></td><td><p>Get storage statistics (by entity, by module)</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>16</strong></p></td><td><p><strong>GET</strong></p></td><td><p>/api/v1/files/quota</p></td><td><p>authenticate + files:read</p></td><td><p>Get tenant storage quota</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>17</strong></p></td><td><p><strong>PUT</strong></p></td><td><p>/api/v1/files/quota</p></td><td><p>authenticate + files:admin</p></td><td><p>Update tenant storage quota (admin only)</p></td></tr></tbody></table><p><em>Route ordering note: /stats, /quota, /entity/:type/:id, /shared/:token are registered BEFORE /:id to prevent Express matching static segments as UUID parameters.</em></p><h1>5. Typical Workflow</h1><h2>5.1 Write Path (File Upload &amp; Management):</h2><p><strong>1. Upload Single File:</strong> Tenant user or module calls POST /api/v1/files/upload with multipart form data: file (required, binary), entity_type (required, e.g., 'documents'), entity_id (required, UUID of parent entity), and optional: description, category, tags (comma-separated string or JSON array), metadata (JSONB). The API validates: file presence (returns VALIDATION_ERROR 'No file uploaded'), entity_type and entity_id presence (returns VALIDATION_ERROR 'entity_type and entity_id are required'), file extension not in blocked list (.exe, .bat, .cmd, .sh, .ps1, .msi, .dll, .com, .scr, .vbs, .js --- returns error 'File type .exe is not allowed'), file size under 100MB (returns FILE_TOO_LARGE 'File size exceeds 100MB limit'), tenant quota not exceeded (returns QUOTA_ERROR for both individual file and total storage).</p><p>Platform generates: stored_name as {uuid}_{sanitized_original_name}.ext, storage path as {tenant_id}/{entity_type}/{YYYY}/{MM}/{stored_name}, SHA-256 checksum via crypto.createHash('sha256').update(buffer).digest('hex'), source_module derived from auth context with priority: req.module.code for combined mode (e.g., 'coc', 'dhf'), 'platform' for JWT-only users, req.user.moduleCode for API key-only modules (e.g., 'aice', 'dhf'), or 'api_key' fallback if API key has no linked module. File is saved to storage via storageProvider.save(relativePath, buffer), then database record is inserted into file_attachments. If DB INSERT fails, file is automatically cleaned up from storage (orphan prevention). Redis cache is invalidated for entity files list. Elasticsearch index is updated via IndexingHooks. Returns full file object with 201 status.</p><p><strong>2. Upload Multiple Files:</strong> POST /api/v1/files/upload/multiple accepts up to 10 files via multer array ('files' field, max 10). All files are uploaded in parallel via Promise.all, sharing the same entity_type and entity_id. Returns array of results with per-file success/error status. Quota is checked per file individually.</p><p><strong>3. Update Metadata:</strong> PUT /api/v1/files/:id updates allowed fields: description (text), category (varchar), tags (cast as text[]), metadata (cast as JSONB). File content changes go through versioning (POST /:id/versions), not metadata update. Sets updated_at timestamp. Returns 'No valid fields to update' error if no recognized fields provided. Returns full file object with 200 status.</p><p><strong>4. Soft Delete:</strong> DELETE /api/v1/files/:id sets deleted_at = NOW() timestamp. PostgreSQL trigger (trg_update_storage_usage) automatically decrements used_storage_bytes and file_count in storage_quotas using GREATEST(..., 0) to prevent negative values. File remains on disk for potential recovery. Redis cache invalidated. Elasticsearch index updated. Returns { success: true, message: 'File deleted' }.</p><p><strong>5. Hard Delete:</strong> DELETE /api/v1/files/:id?hard=true permanently removes: database record (DELETE FROM file_attachments WHERE id = $1 AND tenant_id = $2), physical file from storage via storageProvider.delete(file_path), thumbnail if exists via storageProvider.delete(thumbnail_path). Returns 404 on subsequent GET. Irreversible. Quota adjusts via DELETE trigger.</p><h2>5.2 Versioning:</h2><p><strong>Minor Versioning:</strong> Default behavior. Every POST /api/v1/files/:id/versions with new file (multipart) performs: fetches current record by ID and tenant_id, marks it as is_current_version=false via UPDATE, inserts new record with version = previous_version + 1, is_current_version=true, previous_version_id = old record ID. SHA-256 checksum is calculated for the new file. Both old and new records remain in database --- version history is immutable and never deleted.</p><p><strong>Version History Retrieval:</strong> GET /api/v1/files/:id/versions uses a recursive CTE (Common Table Expression) that traverses the previous_version_id chain in both directions (forward via self-join on previous_version_id, backward via matching previous_version_id = id). Returns complete version lineage with: id, version, file_size, checksum, original_name, uploaded_by_email (resolved via LEFT JOIN users), created_at for each version. Ordered by version number ascending.</p><p><strong>Version Chain Structure:</strong> file_attachments.previous_version_id creates a self-referencing linked list. Version 1 has previous_version_id=null. Version 2 points to Version 1. Version 3 points to Version 2. Only the latest version has is_current_version=true. Standard queries (list, get by entity) only return current versions (WHERE is_current_version = true AND deleted_at IS NULL).</p><h2>5.3 Read/Search Path:</h2><p><strong>Get by ID:</strong> GET /api/v1/files/:id --- Redis cached (300s TTL). Returns full file object with uploaded_by_email resolved via LEFT JOIN users. Filters by tenant_id for isolation.</p><p><strong>List Files:</strong> GET /api/v1/files?limit=50&amp;offset=0 --- returns a JSON array of file objects. Supports limit and offset query parameters for pagination. Supports filters: source_module, mime_type, entity_type, category, search (ILIKE on original_name), sort_by (created_at|file_size|original_name), sort_order (ASC|DESC), created_after, created_before. Only returns active files (deleted_at IS NULL, is_current_version = true).</p><p><strong>Get by Entity:</strong> GET /api/v1/files/entity/:entity_type/:entity_id --- returns a JSON array of all files linked to a specific entity. Used by Document Service, CAPA module, Training module, etc. to retrieve their linked files.</p><p><strong>Storage Stats:</strong> GET /api/v1/files/stats --- returns four sections: quota (max_storage, used_storage, available, usage_percent), files (total, images, pdfs, documents counts), by_entity (count + size per entity_type), and by_module (count + size per source_module e.g., 'platform', 'coc').</p><p><strong>Storage Quota:</strong> GET /api/v1/files/quota --- returns current quota status: max_storage_bytes, used_storage_bytes, file_count, max_file_size_bytes, allowed_mime_types, blocked_extensions, usage_percentage.</p><h2>5.4 File Sharing Path:</h2><p><strong>Create Share:</strong> POST /api/v1/files/:id/share with body: share_type ('link'|'user'), permissions ('view'|'download'), expires_at (ISO timestamp) or expires_in_hours (number, auto-converted), max_downloads (integer), shared_with_user_id, shared_with_email. Service generates 64-char hex token via crypto.randomBytes(32). Returns share object with share_token, share_url, expires_at, max_downloads. Redis cached (3600s TTL).</p><p><strong>Access Shared File:</strong> GET /api/v1/files/shared/:token --- PUBLIC route (no authentication). Service: looks up share by token (Redis cached), validates expiry (expires_at &gt; NOW()), validates download count (current_downloads &lt; max_downloads), increments current_downloads, resolves file path via storageProvider.getFullPath(), serves file with correct Content-Type and Content-Disposition headers. Returns 404 if share not found, expired, or download limit reached.</p><p><strong>Revoke Share:</strong> DELETE /api/v1/files/shares/:shareId --- sets is_active=false. Invalidates Redis cache for the share token.</p><h2>5.5 Retention/Deletion:</h2><p>Phase 1: No automated retention policy enforcement. Soft-deleted files remain in database with deleted_at timestamp set. Hard delete permanently removes file and database record.</p><p><strong>Quota Recovery:</strong> Soft delete triggers automatic quota decrement via PostgreSQL trigger. Restoring a soft-deleted file (clearing deleted_at) would trigger quota increment.</p><p><strong>Planned Phase 2:</strong> Configurable retention periods per entity_type. Scheduled cleanup job to hard-delete files past retention period. Archived storage tier for long-term retention at reduced cost.</p><h2>5.6 Audit Trail:</h2><p>file_access_logs records (inserted on download, preview, and any file access): file_id, tenant_id, user_id, action ('download'|'preview'|'access'), ip_address (from req.ip), user_agent (from req.headers), created_at.</p><p>Storage quota changes tracked via PostgreSQL trigger --- every INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE on file_attachments automatically adjusts used_storage_bytes and file_count.</p><p>Share access tracked via current_downloads counter and last_accessed_at timestamp on file_shares.</p><p><strong>Authentication mode impacts audit attribution:</strong> In combined JWT + API Key mode, uploaded_by and file_access_logs.user_id record the real user identity (from JWT), while source_module records the originating module (from API key). This provides GxP-compliant traceability --- auditors can see both who performed the action and which module facilitated it. In API Key-only mode, uploaded_by records the module service account and source_module records the module code. In JWT-only mode, uploaded_by records the platform user and source_module is set to 'platform'.</p><h1>6. PostgreSQL DB --- MODEL</h1><p>The File Attachment Service uses 4 PostgreSQL tables: file_attachments (master record with binary file metadata, entity linking, versioning, and storage path), storage_quotas (per-tenant storage limits with trigger-maintained usage counters), file_shares (token-based file sharing with expiry and download limits), and file_access_logs (audit trail for file access events). All tables enforce multi-tenant isolation via tenant_id foreign key with ON DELETE CASCADE. The file_attachments table serves as the central entity. A PostgreSQL trigger function (update_storage_usage) automatically maintains used_storage_bytes and file_count in storage_quotas on INSERT, UPDATE (soft-delete/restore), and DELETE operations.</p><h2>6.1 ER Diagram:</h2><p><img src="data:image/png;base64," /></p><p><em>Figure 6.1: Entity Relationship Diagram --- File Attachment Service</em></p><p>file_attachments.tenant_id → tenants.id (ON DELETE CASCADE)</p><p>file_attachments.uploaded_by → users.id</p><p>file_attachments.previous_version_id → file_attachments.id (self-referencing version chain)</p><p>storage_quotas.tenant_id → tenants.id (UNIQUE --- one quota per tenant)</p><p>file_shares.tenant_id → tenants.id, file_shares.file_id → file_attachments.id</p><p>file_access_logs.file_id → file_attachments.id, file_access_logs.user_id → users.id</p><h2>6.2 Entities &amp; Attributes:</h2><h3>6.2.1 Entity: file_attachments (32 columns)</h3><table><thead><tr><th><p><strong>Attribute</strong></p></th><th><p><strong>Data Type</strong></p></th><th><p><strong>Constraint / FK</strong></p></th><th><p><strong>Description</strong></p></th><th><p><strong>Example</strong></p></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><p><strong>id</strong></p></td><td><p>UUID</p></td><td><p>PK, DEFAULT gen_random_uuid()</p></td><td><p>Unique file identifier</p></td><td><p>daf3c8ad-...</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>tenant_id</strong></p></td><td><p>UUID</p></td><td><p>NOT NULL, FK → tenants(id) ON DELETE CASCADE, INDEX</p></td><td><p>Tenant isolation</p></td><td><p>c0000000-...</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>original_name</strong></p></td><td><p>VARCHAR(500)</p></td><td><p>NOT NULL</p></td><td><p>User-uploaded filename</p></td><td><p>test-upload.txt</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>stored_name</strong></p></td><td><p>VARCHAR(500)</p></td><td><p>NOT NULL</p></td><td><p>UUID-based stored name</p></td><td><p>daf3c8ad_test-upload.txt</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>file_path</strong></p></td><td><p>VARCHAR(1000)</p></td><td><p>NOT NULL</p></td><td><p>Relative storage path</p></td><td><p>c000.../documents/2026/02/daf3...txt</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>mime_type</strong></p></td><td><p>VARCHAR(255)</p></td><td><p>NOT NULL</p></td><td><p>MIME type</p></td><td><p>text/plain</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>file_size</strong></p></td><td><p>BIGINT</p></td><td><p>NOT NULL</p></td><td><p>File size in bytes</p></td><td><p>18</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>checksum</strong></p></td><td><p>VARCHAR(128)</p></td><td><p>Nullable</p></td><td><p>SHA-256 hash</p></td><td><p>a591a6d40...</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>storage_provider</strong></p></td><td><p>VARCHAR(50)</p></td><td><p>DEFAULT 'local'</p></td><td><p>Storage backend</p></td><td><p>local</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>storage_bucket</strong></p></td><td><p>VARCHAR(255)</p></td><td><p>Nullable</p></td><td><p>S3 bucket (Phase 2)</p></td><td><p>null</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>storage_region</strong></p></td><td><p>VARCHAR(100)</p></td><td><p>Nullable</p></td><td><p>S3 region (Phase 2)</p></td><td><p>null</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>entity_type</strong></p></td><td><p>VARCHAR(100)</p></td><td><p>NOT NULL, INDEX</p></td><td><p>Parent entity type</p></td><td><p>documents</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>entity_id</strong></p></td><td><p>UUID</p></td><td><p>NOT NULL, INDEX</p></td><td><p>Parent entity ID</p></td><td><p>UUID</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>category</strong></p></td><td><p>VARCHAR(100)</p></td><td><p>Nullable</p></td><td><p>File category</p></td><td><p>specification</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>description</strong></p></td><td><p>TEXT</p></td><td><p>Nullable</p></td><td><p>File description</p></td><td><p>Test file upload</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>tags</strong></p></td><td><p>TEXT[]</p></td><td><p>Nullable</p></td><td><p>PostgreSQL text array</p></td><td><p>{sop,quality}</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>version</strong></p></td><td><p>INTEGER</p></td><td><p>DEFAULT 1</p></td><td><p>Version number</p></td><td><p>1</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>is_current_version</strong></p></td><td><p>BOOLEAN</p></td><td><p>DEFAULT true, INDEX (partial)</p></td><td><p>Active version flag</p></td><td><p>true</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>previous_version_id</strong></p></td><td><p>UUID</p></td><td><p>FK → file_attachments(id), Nullable</p></td><td><p>Version chain link</p></td><td><p>UUID or null</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>is_public</strong></p></td><td><p>BOOLEAN</p></td><td><p>DEFAULT false</p></td><td><p>Public access flag</p></td><td><p>false</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>access_level</strong></p></td><td><p>VARCHAR(50)</p></td><td><p>DEFAULT 'private'</p></td><td><p>Access level</p></td><td><p>private</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>download_count</strong></p></td><td><p>INTEGER</p></td><td><p>DEFAULT 0</p></td><td><p>Total downloads</p></td><td><p>5</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>has_thumbnail</strong></p></td><td><p>BOOLEAN</p></td><td><p>DEFAULT false</p></td><td><p>Thumbnail exists</p></td><td><p>false</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>thumbnail_path</strong></p></td><td><p>VARCHAR(1000)</p></td><td><p>Nullable</p></td><td><p>Thumbnail storage path</p></td><td><p>null</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>metadata</strong></p></td><td><p>JSONB</p></td><td><p>DEFAULT '{}'</p></td><td><p>Extensible metadata</p></td><td><p>{}</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>scan_status</strong></p></td><td><p>VARCHAR(50)</p></td><td><p>Nullable</p></td><td><p>Virus scan status (Phase 2)</p></td><td><p>null</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>scanned_at</strong></p></td><td><p>TIMESTAMPTZ</p></td><td><p>Nullable</p></td><td><p>Scan timestamp (Phase 2)</p></td><td><p>null</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>uploaded_by</strong></p></td><td><p>UUID</p></td><td><p>FK → users(id), Nullable</p></td><td><p>Uploader user ID</p></td><td><p>UUID</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>source_module</strong></p></td><td><p>VARCHAR(100)</p></td><td><p>DEFAULT 'platform', INDEX</p></td><td><p>Origin module</p></td><td><p>platform</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>created_at</strong></p></td><td><p>TIMESTAMPTZ</p></td><td><p>DEFAULT NOW()</p></td><td><p>Created timestamp</p></td><td><p>ISO datetime</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>updated_at</strong></p></td><td><p>TIMESTAMPTZ</p></td><td><p>DEFAULT NOW()</p></td><td><p>Updated timestamp</p></td><td><p>ISO datetime</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>deleted_at</strong></p></td><td><p>TIMESTAMPTZ</p></td><td><p>Nullable, INDEX</p></td><td><p>Soft delete timestamp</p></td><td><p>null</p></td></tr></tbody></table><p><em>NOTES: tags is TEXT[] not JSONB. file_size is BIGINT (returns as string from node-pg, parsed to integer in _formatAttachment). is_current_version partial index filters WHERE deleted_at IS NULL AND is_current_version = TRUE. previous_version_id creates a self-referencing linked list for version chains. source_module is auto-derived from auth context, never user-supplied.</em></p><h3>6.2.2 Entity: storage_quotas (10 columns)</h3><table><thead><tr><th><p><strong>Attribute</strong></p></th><th><p><strong>Data Type</strong></p></th><th><p><strong>Constraint / FK</strong></p></th><th><p><strong>Description</strong></p></th><th><p><strong>Example</strong></p></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><p><strong>id</strong></p></td><td><p>UUID</p></td><td><p>PK, DEFAULT gen_random_uuid()</p></td><td><p>Quota record ID</p></td><td><p>UUID</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>tenant_id</strong></p></td><td><p>UUID</p></td><td><p>NOT NULL, FK → tenants(id), UNIQUE</p></td><td><p>Tenant isolation</p></td><td><p>c0000000-...</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>max_storage_bytes</strong></p></td><td><p>BIGINT</p></td><td><p>DEFAULT 10737418240 (10GB)</p></td><td><p>Max total storage</p></td><td><p>10737418240</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>used_storage_bytes</strong></p></td><td><p>BIGINT</p></td><td><p>DEFAULT 0</p></td><td><p>Current usage (trigger-maintained)</p></td><td><p>1536</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>file_count</strong></p></td><td><p>INTEGER</p></td><td><p>DEFAULT 0</p></td><td><p>Active file count (trigger-maintained)</p></td><td><p>15</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>max_file_size_bytes</strong></p></td><td><p>BIGINT</p></td><td><p>DEFAULT 104857600 (100MB)</p></td><td><p>Max single file size</p></td><td><p>104857600</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>allowed_mime_types</strong></p></td><td><p>TEXT[]</p></td><td><p>Nullable</p></td><td><p>Whitelist (null = all allowed)</p></td><td><p>null</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>blocked_extensions</strong></p></td><td><p>TEXT[]</p></td><td><p>Nullable</p></td><td><p>Blacklist</p></td><td><p>{.exe,.bat}</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>created_at</strong></p></td><td><p>TIMESTAMPTZ</p></td><td><p>DEFAULT NOW()</p></td><td><p>Created timestamp</p></td><td><p>ISO datetime</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>updated_at</strong></p></td><td><p>TIMESTAMPTZ</p></td><td><p>DEFAULT NOW()</p></td><td><p>Updated timestamp</p></td><td><p>ISO datetime</p></td></tr></tbody></table><p><em>NOTES: max_storage_bytes and used_storage_bytes are BIGINT. PostgreSQL trigger update_storage_usage() maintains used_storage_bytes and file_count automatically on file_attachments INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE. The trigger uses GREATEST(..., 0) to prevent negative values. Quota auto-creates via service when first upload occurs for a tenant.</em></p><h3>PostgreSQL Trigger Function:</h3><p>CREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION update_storage_usage() --- Handles INSERT (increment), DELETE (decrement), UPDATE with NEW.deleted_at IS NOT NULL AND OLD.deleted_at IS NULL (soft-delete decrement), UPDATE with NEW.deleted_at IS NULL AND OLD.deleted_at IS NOT NULL (restore increment). Uses GREATEST(used_storage_bytes - file_size, 0) and GREATEST(file_count - 1, 0) to prevent negative values.</p><h3>6.2.3 Entity: file_shares (15 columns)</h3><table><thead><tr><th><p><strong>Attribute</strong></p></th><th><p><strong>Data Type</strong></p></th><th><p><strong>Constraint / FK</strong></p></th><th><p><strong>Description</strong></p></th><th><p><strong>Example</strong></p></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><p><strong>id</strong></p></td><td><p>UUID</p></td><td><p>PK, DEFAULT gen_random_uuid()</p></td><td><p>Share record ID</p></td><td><p>UUID</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>tenant_id</strong></p></td><td><p>UUID</p></td><td><p>NOT NULL, FK → tenants(id) ON DELETE CASCADE</p></td><td><p>Tenant isolation</p></td><td><p>c0000000-...</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>file_id</strong></p></td><td><p>UUID</p></td><td><p>NOT NULL, FK → file_attachments(id) ON DELETE CASCADE</p></td><td><p>Shared file</p></td><td><p>UUID</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>share_token</strong></p></td><td><p>VARCHAR(255)</p></td><td><p>NOT NULL, UNIQUE</p></td><td><p>64-char hex token</p></td><td><p>a3f8c2d1...</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>share_type</strong></p></td><td><p>VARCHAR(50)</p></td><td><p>DEFAULT 'link'</p></td><td><p>Share type</p></td><td><p>link</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>shared_with_user_id</strong></p></td><td><p>UUID</p></td><td><p>FK → users(id), Nullable</p></td><td><p>Target user (for 'user' type)</p></td><td><p>null</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>shared_with_email</strong></p></td><td><p>VARCHAR(255)</p></td><td><p>Nullable</p></td><td><p>Target email</p></td><td><p>null</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>permissions</strong></p></td><td><p>VARCHAR(50)</p></td><td><p>DEFAULT 'view'</p></td><td><p>Access level</p></td><td><p>view/download</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>expires_at</strong></p></td><td><p>TIMESTAMPTZ</p></td><td><p>Nullable</p></td><td><p>Expiry timestamp</p></td><td><p>ISO datetime</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>max_downloads</strong></p></td><td><p>INTEGER</p></td><td><p>Nullable</p></td><td><p>Download cap (null = unlimited)</p></td><td><p>5</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>current_downloads</strong></p></td><td><p>INTEGER</p></td><td><p>DEFAULT 0</p></td><td><p>Downloads consumed</p></td><td><p>2</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>is_active</strong></p></td><td><p>BOOLEAN</p></td><td><p>DEFAULT true</p></td><td><p>Active flag (false = revoked)</p></td><td><p>true</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>last_accessed_at</strong></p></td><td><p>TIMESTAMPTZ</p></td><td><p>Nullable</p></td><td><p>Last access time</p></td><td><p>ISO datetime</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>created_by</strong></p></td><td><p>UUID</p></td><td><p>FK → users(id), Nullable</p></td><td><p>Share creator</p></td><td><p>UUID</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>created_at</strong></p></td><td><p>TIMESTAMPTZ</p></td><td><p>DEFAULT NOW()</p></td><td><p>Created timestamp</p></td><td><p>ISO datetime</p></td></tr></tbody></table><p><em>NOTES: share_token is UNIQUE. expires_at can be set directly or auto-calculated from expires_in_hours (service converts: new Date(Date.now() + hours * 3600000).toISOString()). max_downloads null means unlimited. current_downloads is incremented atomically on each access. is_active=false means share has been revoked.</em></p><h3>6.2.4 Entity: file_access_logs (8 columns)</h3><table><thead><tr><th><p><strong>Attribute</strong></p></th><th><p><strong>Data Type</strong></p></th><th><p><strong>Constraint / FK</strong></p></th><th><p><strong>Description</strong></p></th><th><p><strong>Example</strong></p></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><p><strong>id</strong></p></td><td><p>UUID</p></td><td><p>PK, DEFAULT gen_random_uuid()</p></td><td><p>Log record ID</p></td><td><p>UUID</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>tenant_id</strong></p></td><td><p>UUID</p></td><td><p>NOT NULL, FK → tenants(id) ON DELETE CASCADE</p></td><td><p>Tenant isolation</p></td><td><p>UUID</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>file_id</strong></p></td><td><p>UUID</p></td><td><p>NOT NULL, FK → file_attachments(id) ON DELETE CASCADE</p></td><td><p>File accessed</p></td><td><p>UUID</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>user_id</strong></p></td><td><p>UUID</p></td><td><p>NOT NULL, FK → users(id)</p></td><td><p>Who accessed</p></td><td><p>UUID</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>action</strong></p></td><td><p>VARCHAR(50)</p></td><td><p>NOT NULL</p></td><td><p>Action type</p></td><td><p>download/preview/access</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>ip_address</strong></p></td><td><p>INET</p></td><td><p>Nullable</p></td><td><p>Client IP</p></td><td><p>192.168.1.1</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>user_agent</strong></p></td><td><p>TEXT</p></td><td><p>Nullable</p></td><td><p>Browser UA string</p></td><td><p>Mozilla/5.0...</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>created_at</strong></p></td><td><p>TIMESTAMPTZ</p></td><td><p>DEFAULT NOW()</p></td><td><p>When accessed</p></td><td><p>ISO datetime</p></td></tr></tbody></table><p><em>NOTES: Records are insert-only (append-only audit log). ip_address and user_agent are captured from Express request object when available. action values include 'download', 'preview', 'access' for share access.</em></p><h1>7. Qdrant (Vector DB)</h1><p><strong>Phase 1 Status: NOT USED by File Attachment Service.</strong></p><p>Binary file content is stored on the filesystem via LocalStorageProvider. File metadata is stored in PostgreSQL (file_attachments table). No vector embeddings of file content are generated in Phase 1.</p><h2>Planned for Phase 2:</h2><p>Collection Name: file_content_vec_v1</p><p>Vector Dimension: 1536 (OpenAI text-embedding-3-small) or 768 (sentence-transformers)</p><p>Payload Fields: file_id (string), tenant_id (string --- mandatory filter), original_name (string), entity_type (string), entity_id (string), mime_type (string), category (string), source_module (string), created_at (ISO string)</p><p>Payload Indexes: tenant_id, entity_type, mime_type, source_module, created_at</p><p>Use Case: Semantic search across extracted file content (PDF text, document text), similar file discovery, AI-powered content recommendations, duplicate detection via vector similarity rather than checksum matching.</p><p>Prerequisite: Content extraction pipeline (PDF text extraction, OCR for images, document parsing) must be implemented first to generate text from binary files before vector embedding.</p><h1>8. Object Storage</h1><h2>Folder Structure Hierarchy:</h2><p>Root: UPLOAD_DIR (default: ./uploads/, configurable via environment variable)</p><p>Pattern: {UPLOAD_DIR}/{tenant_id}/{entity_type}/{YYYY}/{MM}/{uuid}_{sanitized_name}.ext</p><p><strong>Example hierarchy:</strong></p><p>uploads/</p><p>  c0000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001/   (Tenant: Pharma Corp)</p><p>    documents/   (Entity type)</p><p>      2026/   (Year)</p><p>        02/   (Month)</p><p>          daf3c8ad-3660-4c13-8b57-0daf3cc9aaf0_test-upload.txt</p><p>          d7cbcb77-a8f1-4e22-b901-3dc77f8e22a1_report-v2.pdf</p><p>    capa/</p><p>      2026/</p><p>        02/</p><p>          f1a2b3c4-..._corrective-action-photo.jpg</p><p>    training/</p><p>      2026/</p><p>        01/</p><p>          a9b8c7d6-..._sop-training-material.pdf</p><p>  d0000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000002/   (Tenant: Another Org)</p><p>    documents/</p><p>    suppliers/</p><p>    temp/   (Temporary storage)</p><p>    thumbnails/   (Thumbnail storage)</p><h3>Storage Provider Abstraction:</h3><p>LocalStorageProvider (Phase 1 --- current):</p><p>save(relativePath, buffer) --- Creates directory tree via fs.mkdirSync({recursive: true}), writes buffer via fs.writeFileSync.</p><p>delete(relativePath) --- Removes file via fs.unlinkSync. Silently ignores if file doesn't exist.</p><p>exists(relativePath) --- Checks via fs.existsSync.</p><p>getFullPath(relativePath) --- Joins UPLOAD_DIR + relativePath. Controller uses path.resolve() for absolute path.</p><h3>S3 Migration Path (Phase 2):</h3><p>Implement S3StorageProvider with identical interface (save, delete, exists, getFullPath). S3 key structure maps 1:1 to local folder hierarchy: s3://{bucket}/{tenant_id}/{entity_type}/{YYYY}/{MM}/{uuid}_{name}.ext. No changes to service or controller code required --- swap provider via configuration.</p><h1>SECTION 10 --- REDIS CACHE DETAILS</h1><p>File Attachment Service uses 4 Redis cache key patterns with graceful degradation. The service operates normally if Redis is unavailable --- all cache operations are wrapped in try/catch and failures are logged but never block the main operation.</p><table><thead><tr><th><p><strong>Cache Key Pattern</strong></p></th><th><p><strong>Purpose</strong></p></th><th><p><strong>TTL</strong></p></th><th><p><strong>Invalidation</strong></p></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><p>file:{tenant_id}:{file_id}</p></td><td><p>Single file metadata cache</p></td><td><p>300s (5 min)</p></td><td><p>On update, delete</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>quota:{tenant_id}</p></td><td><p>Tenant storage quota</p></td><td><p>60s (1 min)</p></td><td><p>On upload, delete, quota update</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>share:{share_token}</p></td><td><p>Share token lookup</p></td><td><p>3600s (1 hr)</p></td><td><p>On revoke</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>entity_files:{entity_type}:{entity_id}</p></td><td><p>Files by entity list</p></td><td><p>120s (2 min)</p></td><td><p>On upload, delete to entity</p></td></tr></tbody></table><h3>Redis Service Integration:</h3><p>File: src/infrastructure/cache/redis.service.js --- singleton instance exported as module.exports = new RedisService().</p><p>Methods used: get(key, parseJson=true), set(key, value, ttlSeconds), delete(key). Note: The Redis service uses delete() method, NOT del().</p><p>Cache invalidation method: _invalidateCache(tenantId, fileId, entityType, entityId) --- deletes all relevant cache keys after any write operation (upload, update, delete, share revoke).</p><p>Lazy initialization: getRedis() function returns singleton Redis instance on first call, returns null if Redis is unavailable. Every cache operation checks for null before proceeding.</p><h3>Platform-level Redis usage that benefits File Attachment Service indirectly:</h3><p><strong>Session caching:</strong> JWT session data cached by AuthService for fast auth validation on every request.</p><p><strong>Permission caching:</strong> User permissions cached by PermissionService, used by authorization middleware for files:read/files:write/files:admin checks.</p><h3>Planned Phase 2 enhancements:</h3><p><strong>File content cache:</strong> Large frequently-accessed files cached in Redis for sub-millisecond retrieval.</p><p><strong>Share token bloom filter:</strong> Probabilistic check before DB lookup for invalid/revoked tokens.</p><p><strong>Upload rate limiting:</strong> Per-tenant upload rate tracking via Redis INCR + EXPIRE.</p>	Initial draft	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	4ccf68ad-485c-495a-935b-10d74b173a52	2026-03-27 14:51:09.534074+00	Coc	af82560b-71d5-42cc-9b0d-9a4267b5b4ed
c945a621-a2be-4ed4-aa80-713a4c92ba59	0f9b3448-b5f5-4153-a8d2-1b2fe672e5c7	352d7e21-5155-4ff8-8169-284bf5ece3e7	0.1	0	1	in_review	FileAttachmentService-Forma.docx	352d7e21-5155-4ff8-8169-284bf5ece3e7/documents/2026/03/718f4ef5-b3b4-44f7-bdef-b28d1a860a78_fileattachmentservice-forma.docx	1158013	application/vnd.openxmlformats-officedocument.wordprocessingml.document	4d72bf55d5e825ad06606ac236f3594ce525363f27d5edfe5568727dfb2ad3ac	SERVICE SPECIFICATION\n\nFile Attachment Services\n\n\n\nQAssure Multi-Tenant SaaS Platform\n\nVersion 1.0   ·   February 2026\n\n\n\n1. Feature of Service\n\nSERVICE NAME: FILE ATTACHMENT SERVICES\n\nCentralised, tenant-isolated, binary file lifecycle engine that governs how files (documents, images, reports, specifications, audit evidence, training materials) are uploaded, stored, versioned, downloaded, previewed, shared, and deleted across multi-tenant regulated environments. All binary file operations across the QAssure platform and module applications are routed through this single service.\n\n\n\nCore capabilities:\n\n\n\n1. File Upload & Storage --- Single and multi-file upload (up to 10 files per request) with multer memory storage, 100MB max file size enforcement, blocked dangerous extensions (.exe, .bat, .cmd, .sh, .ps1, .msi, .dll, .com, .scr, .vbs, .js), SHA-256 checksum calculation via crypto.createHash('sha256'), and automatic tenant-scoped folder hierarchy: {tenant_id}/{entity_type}/{YYYY}/{MM}/{uuid}_{sanitized_name}.ext. Files are linked to any platform entity via entity_type + entity_id (e.g., entity_type='documents', entity_id=document_uuid). Source module is auto-derived from authentication context: module code for combined JWT + API Key mode (e.g., 'coc', 'dhf') where module identity is captured from the API key even though user identity comes from JWT; 'platform' for JWT-only users; moduleCode for API key-only modules (e.g., 'aice', 'dhf'); or 'api_key' as fallback if an API key has no linked module. Priority order: req.module.code (combined mode) → req.user.moduleCode (API key mode) → 'platform' (JWT mode). Duplicate detection prevents re-upload of identical files to the same entity (enforced by checksum + entity match on active records).\n\n2. File Versioning --- Each file supports immutable version history. Uploading a new version via POST /:id/versions marks the previous version as is_current_version=false and creates a new record with incremented version number, linked via previous_version_id. Version history is retrieved via recursive CTE (Common Table Expression) that traverses the chain in both directions, returning complete lineage. Only the current version is returned in standard queries.\n\n3. Download & Preview --- Download endpoint (GET /:id/download) serves files with Content-Disposition: attachment header for browser download prompt. Preview endpoint (GET /:id/preview) serves with Content-Disposition: inline for in-browser rendering. Both use storageProvider.getFullPath() with path.resolve() for absolute path resolution. File access is logged in file_access_logs table with user_id, IP address, user agent, and action type.\n\n4. File Sharing with Token-Based Access --- Create time-limited, download-limited share links via POST /:id/share. Generates 64-character hex token via crypto.randomBytes(32). Supports configurable parameters: expires_at (absolute timestamp), expires_in_hours (convenience --- auto-converted to expires_at), max_downloads (download cap), share_type ('link' or 'user'), shared_with_user_id, shared_with_email, and permissions ('view' or 'download'). Access via GET /shared/:token is a PUBLIC route (no authentication required). The service validates token existence, checks expiry, enforces max_downloads limit, increments current_downloads counter, and serves the file. Share revocation via DELETE /shares/:shareId deactivates the share.\n\n5. Storage Quota Management --- Per-tenant storage quotas tracked in storage_quotas table: max_storage_bytes (default 10GB), used_storage_bytes, file_count, max_file_size_bytes (default 100MB), allowed_mime_types, blocked_extensions. Quota auto-creates on first upload if not present. PostgreSQL trigger (trg_update_storage_usage) automatically increments/decrements used_storage_bytes and file_count on INSERT, DELETE, and UPDATE (soft-delete via SET deleted_at). Quota is checked before every upload: individual file size vs max_file_size_bytes, and total used + new file vs max_storage_bytes. Admin-only PUT /quota endpoint allows updating quota limits (requires files:admin permission).\n\n6. Soft Delete with Hard Delete Option --- Default DELETE /:id performs soft delete (sets deleted_at timestamp). PostgreSQL trigger automatically decrements quota on soft delete. Hard delete via DELETE /:id?hard=true permanently removes the database record AND the physical file from storage via storageProvider.delete(). Orphaned file cleanup: if database INSERT fails after file is saved to disk, the file is automatically cleaned up in the catch block.\n\n7. Storage Provider Abstraction (S3-Ready) --- All file I/O goes through LocalStorageProvider with 4 methods: save(relativePath, buffer), delete(relativePath), exists(relativePath), getFullPath(relativePath). No controller or service code touches the filesystem directly. Migrating to S3 requires implementing an S3StorageProvider with the same interface --- zero code changes in controllers or service logic.\n\n8. Three Authentication Modes --- Every endpoint (except public share access) supports three authentication modes via authenticate middleware: (a) JWT only --- QAssure UI users authenticate via Authorization: Bearer header; tenant derived from JWT claims; user permissions enforced via authorize(); (b) JWT + API Key combined --- module applications acting on behalf of a logged-in user send both Authorization: Bearer (JWT from verify-launch-token) and X-API-Key headers; tenant derived from JWT (X-Tenant-ID not needed); user permissions enforced (not module bypass); source_module set from module identity (e.g., 'coc', 'dhf') for accurate audit attribution; (c) API Key + X-Tenant-ID --- module system/cron operations with no user context send X-API-Key and X-Tenant-ID headers; module gets full access bypass on authorize(). This enables QAssure UI users, module applications acting on behalf of users (with real user identity in audit trails), and module background processes to consume the file attachment service. Routes use authorize() for permission checks: files:read for GET endpoints, files:write for POST/PUT/DELETE endpoints, files:admin for quota management.\n\n9. Multi-Tenant Data Isolation --- All queries are tenant-scoped. Every file, share, quota, and access log belongs to a tenant_id. API Key middleware validates module-tenant assignment via the tenant_module_access table (enabled = true) before granting access. Missing X-Tenant-ID returns error code MISSING_TENANT. Unassigned tenant returns MODULE_NOT_ASSIGNED. In combined mode (JWT + API Key), if the JWT's tenant does not match the API key's assigned tenant, error code TENANT_MISMATCH is returned; if the module is deactivated for the tenant, error code MODULE_NOT_ACTIVE is returned. Users cannot see or access files belonging to other tenants under any circumstance.\n\n10. Security Hardening --- UUID validation middleware on all :id route parameters returns 400 (INVALID_ID_FORMAT) for malformed UUIDs instead of leaking PostgreSQL errors. Error sanitization function (sanitizeErrorResponse) on all 17 controller catch blocks classifies errors into client-safe categories (QUOTA_ERROR, NOT_FOUND, VALIDATION_ERROR, etc.) and returns generic 'An internal error occurred' for unexpected failures --- never exposing constraint names, table structures, or query details. Blocked file extensions prevent executable uploads. Path traversal is impossible because stored names use UUID-based naming, not user-controlled paths.\n\n11. Observability --- Redis caching with graceful degradation (file cache 300s TTL, quota 60s, share 3600s, entity files 120s). Elasticsearch indexing for file searchability. File access logging with IP/user-agent tracking. Storage statistics with per-entity-type and per-module breakdowns. All infrastructure dependencies (Redis, Elasticsearch) fail gracefully --- the service continues operating without caching or indexing if either is unavailable.\n\n\n\n2. Scope\n\nIN SCOPE (Phase 1):\n\nFull file CRUD: upload single file (with auto-generated tenant-scoped storage path, checksum, source_module derivation), upload multiple files (up to 10, parallel via Promise.all), read (single by ID with Redis cache, paginated list with filtering, by entity with pagination), update metadata (description, tags, category, metadata JSONB), soft delete (with quota trigger), hard delete (with physical file removal).\n\nFile versioning: upload new version (marks previous as not current, increments version number, links via previous_version_id), retrieve version history (recursive CTE traversal in both directions).\n\nDownload and preview with correct Content-Disposition headers (attachment for download, inline for preview), file access logging, and path resolution via storageProvider abstraction.\n\nFile sharing: create share link with configurable expiry (expires_at or expires_in_hours), max_downloads cap, share_type (link/user), permissions (view/download). Public access via token (no auth required). Share revocation. Expired/exhausted shares return 404.\n\nStorage quota management: auto-create on first upload, per-tenant limits (max_storage_bytes, max_file_size_bytes, allowed_mime_types, blocked_extensions), quota enforcement on every upload, admin-only quota configuration updates (files:admin permission), PostgreSQL trigger for automatic used_storage_bytes/file_count tracking on INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE.\n\nThree authentication modes (JWT only, JWT + API Key combined, API Key + X-Tenant-ID) on all 16 authenticated endpoints via authenticate middleware (1 public route excluded: GET /shared/:token). Combined mode derives tenant from JWT (X-Tenant-ID not needed), enforces user permissions, and sets source_module from module identity for accurate audit attribution.\n\nMulti-tenant isolation on all read and write operations.\n\nAPI Key middleware validates module-tenant access via tenant_module_access table.\n\nUUID validation middleware on all :id parameters --- returns 400 for invalid UUIDs.\n\nError sanitization on all 17 catch blocks --- never leaks database internals.\n\nFile access audit trail via file_access_logs with user_id, IP address, user agent, action.\n\nStorage statistics with quota summary, file type counts, by_entity and by_module breakdowns.\n\nPagination via limit and offset query parameters (default limit 50). Response is a JSON array of file objects.\n\nFilter support on list: source_module, mime_type, entity_type, category, search (ILIKE on original_name), sort (created_at, file_size, original_name), date range (created_after, created_before).\n\nOrphaned file cleanup: if DB INSERT fails after file save, file is automatically removed from storage.\n\nRedis caching with graceful degradation (operates without Redis).\n\nElasticsearch indexing with graceful degradation (operates without ES).\n\n\n\nOUT OF SCOPE (Phase 1 --- File Attachment Service boundary):\n\nS3/MinIO object storage migration --- LocalStorageProvider is production-ready; S3StorageProvider is a config swap planned for Phase 2. Storage provider abstraction is already in place.\n\nVirus/malware scanning --- scan_status and scanned_at columns exist in file_attachments table but no scanning service is integrated.\n\nImage thumbnail generation --- has_thumbnail, thumbnail_path columns exist but no thumbnail generation pipeline is implemented.\n\nFull-text content extraction from uploaded files (PDF text extraction, OCR).\n\nVector embedding of file content for semantic search via Qdrant.\n\nReal-time file upload progress (WebSocket-based progress tracking).\n\nClient-side chunked upload for files larger than 100MB.\n\nCDN integration for cached file delivery.\n\nFile format conversion (PDF to image, document to PDF, etc.).\n\nWatermarking or digital stamping of downloaded files.\n\nBulk import/export of files.\n\nFile-level access control lists (ACLs) --- access is controlled at entity level via entity_type permissions, not per-file.\n\nRetention policy enforcement --- soft-deleted files remain in database indefinitely until hard-deleted.\n\nEmail/SMS notifications on file events (handled by Notification Service separately).\n\nDocument lifecycle management (SOPs, versioning, review/approval) --- handled by separate Document Service (document.service.js).\n\n\n\n3. Clear Responsibility Split\n\n3.1 SaaS Platform (Owned by Platform Team):\n\nProvide and maintain the File Attachment Service as a centralised, tenant-isolated binary file management engine.\n\nEnforce file upload constraints: 100MB max file size (multer + quota), blocked dangerous extensions, per-tenant storage quotas with auto-creation and trigger-based tracking.\n\nGenerate and manage tenant-scoped storage paths: {tenant_id}/{entity_type}/{YYYY}/{MM}/{uuid}_{sanitized_name}.ext.\n\nCalculate SHA-256 checksums on every upload for integrity verification.\n\nAuto-derive source_module from authentication context with priority: req.module.code for combined JWT + API Key mode (e.g., 'coc', 'dhf'), req.user.moduleCode for API key-only modules, 'platform' for JWT-only users, 'api_key' fallback for API keys without linked modules. This ensures files uploaded by modules on behalf of users are correctly attributed to the originating module in storage statistics.\n\nManage file versioning: version number increment, previous_version_id linking, is_current_version flagging, recursive CTE version history retrieval.\n\nMaintain storage provider abstraction (LocalStorageProvider) with save, delete, exists, getFullPath methods for future S3 migration.\n\nEnforce tenant isolation on all queries via tenant_id filtering. No cross-tenant file access is possible.\n\nProvide three authentication modes on all 16 authenticated endpoints via authenticate middleware (1 public route excluded: GET /shared/:token): (a) JWT only for QAssure UI users, (b) JWT + API Key combined for module applications acting on behalf of logged-in users (tenant from JWT, user permissions enforced, source_module from module identity), (c) API Key + X-Tenant-ID for module system/cron operations.\n\nValidate module-tenant access via tenant_module_access table (enabled = true check). Return MISSING_TENANT or MODULE_NOT_ASSIGNED errors for invalid API Key requests. In combined mode, return TENANT_MISMATCH if JWT tenant differs from API key's assigned tenant, and MODULE_NOT_ACTIVE if the module is deactivated for the tenant.\n\nProvide UUID validation middleware for all route parameters. Return 400 INVALID_ID_FORMAT for malformed UUIDs.\n\nSanitise all error responses to prevent database internal leakage.\n\nProvide file sharing infrastructure: token generation, expiry enforcement, max_downloads enforcement, public access route.\n\nProvide storage statistics with per-entity-type and per-module breakdowns.\n\nLog all file access events (download, preview, checkout, checkin) in file_access_logs.\n\nProvide Redis caching and Elasticsearch indexing with graceful degradation.\n\nExpose admin-only quota management endpoint (files:admin permission).\n\n\n\n3.2 Module Applications (Residing on Platform; Owned by App Teams --- LTTS):\n\nUse the File Attachment Service API with the appropriate authentication mode:\n\nFor user-context operations (user uploads a file via module UI): Send both Authorization: Bearer (JWT obtained from verify-launch-token) and X-API-Key headers. X-Tenant-ID is NOT needed --- tenant is derived from JWT. User permissions (files:read, files:write) are enforced. uploaded_by and file_access_logs.user_id record the real user identity. source_module records the module identity (e.g., 'coc').\n\nFor system/cron operations (no user context): Send X-API-Key + X-Tenant-ID headers. Module gets full access bypass. source_module records the module identity from API key.\n\nSpecify entity_type and entity_id when uploading files to link them to the correct module entity (e.g., entity_type='documents', entity_id=<document_uuid> for Document Service; entity_type='capa', entity_id=<capa_uuid> for CAPA module).\n\nProvide required fields on upload: file (multipart), entity_type (string), entity_id (UUID). Optional fields: description, category, tags, metadata (JSONB).\n\nUse the version upload endpoint (POST /:id/versions) for updating files rather than deleting and re-uploading. This preserves version history and audit trail.\n\nUse the share endpoint to generate time-limited, download-limited links for external stakeholders (e.g., auditors, suppliers) without granting platform login access.\n\nDo NOT implement custom file storage logic --- always delegate to platform's file attachment service.\n\nDo NOT store file metadata in the module's own database --- use platform as the system of record for all file attachments. Use the entity link (entity_type + entity_id) to associate files with module records.\n\nDo NOT construct file paths manually or access the uploads directory directly --- always use service API endpoints for file access.\n\nUse SSO launch token flow to obtain both tenant context and user session JWT dynamically in production: call POST /modules/verify-launch-token with X-API-Key and the launch token to receive a JWT (24h expiry), user details (including department, designation, user category), tenant details, and permissions. Use this JWT in Authorization: Bearer header alongside X-API-Key for all user-context platform calls. Use .env TENANT_ID + X-Tenant-ID header for development and system/cron operations only.\n\n\n\n4. API List (Refer to Excel Sheet shared)\n\n\n\n#\n\nMethod\n\nEndpoint\n\nAuth\n\nDescription\n\n1\n\nPOST\n\n/api/v1/files/upload\n\nauthenticate + files:write\n\nUpload single file (multipart)\n\n2\n\nPOST\n\n/api/v1/files/upload/multiple\n\nauthenticate + files:write\n\nUpload multiple files (up to 10)\n\n3\n\nGET\n\n/api/v1/files\n\nauthenticate + files:read\n\nList files (paginated, filterable)\n\n4\n\nGET\n\n/api/v1/files/:id\n\nauthenticate + files:read + validateUUID\n\nGet single file by ID\n\n5\n\nPUT\n\n/api/v1/files/:id\n\nauthenticate + files:write + validateUUID\n\nUpdate file metadata\n\n6\n\nDELETE\n\n/api/v1/files/:id\n\nauthenticate + files:write + validateUUID\n\nDelete file (soft; ?hard=true for permanent)\n\n7\n\nGET\n\n/api/v1/files/:id/download\n\nauthenticate + files:read + validateUUID\n\nDownload file (Content-Disposition: attachment)\n\n8\n\nGET\n\n/api/v1/files/:id/preview\n\nauthenticate + files:read + validateUUID\n\nPreview file (Content-Disposition: inline)\n\n9\n\nPOST\n\n/api/v1/files/:id/versions\n\nauthenticate + files:write + validateUUID\n\nUpload new version of existing file\n\n10\n\nGET\n\n/api/v1/files/:id/versions\n\nauthenticate + files:read + validateUUID\n\nGet version history (recursive CTE)\n\n11\n\nPOST\n\n/api/v1/files/:id/share\n\nauthenticate + files:write + validateUUID\n\nCreate share link (token, expiry, max_downloads)\n\n12\n\nDELETE\n\n/api/v1/files/shares/:shareId\n\nauthenticate + files:write + validateUUID\n\nRevoke share link\n\n13\n\nGET\n\n/api/v1/files/shared/:token\n\nPUBLIC (no auth)\n\nAccess shared file via token\n\n14\n\nGET\n\n/api/v1/files/entity/:entity_type/:entity_id\n\nauthenticate + files:read + validateUUID\n\nGet files by entity (paginated)\n\n15\n\nGET\n\n/api/v1/files/stats\n\nauthenticate + files:read\n\nGet storage statistics (by entity, by module)\n\n16\n\nGET\n\n/api/v1/files/quota\n\nauthenticate + files:read\n\nGet tenant storage quota\n\n17\n\nPUT\n\n/api/v1/files/quota\n\nauthenticate + files:admin\n\nUpdate tenant storage quota (admin only)\n\n\n\nRoute ordering note: /stats, /quota, /entity/:type/:id, /shared/:token are registered BEFORE /:id to prevent Express matching static segments as UUID parameters.\n\n\n\n5. Typical Workflow\n\n5.1 Write Path (File Upload & Management):\n\n1. Upload Single File: Tenant user or module calls POST /api/v1/files/upload with multipart form data: file (required, binary), entity_type (required, e.g., 'documents'), entity_id (required, UUID of parent entity), and optional: description, category, tags (comma-separated string or JSON array), metadata (JSONB). The API validates: file presence (returns VALIDATION_ERROR 'No file uploaded'), entity_type and entity_id presence (returns VALIDATION_ERROR 'entity_type and entity_id are required'), file extension not in blocked list (.exe, .bat, .cmd, .sh, .ps1, .msi, .dll, .com, .scr, .vbs, .js --- returns error 'File type .exe is not allowed'), file size under 100MB (returns FILE_TOO_LARGE 'File size exceeds 100MB limit'), tenant quota not exceeded (returns QUOTA_ERROR for both individual file and total storage).\n\nPlatform generates: stored_name as {uuid}_{sanitized_original_name}.ext, storage path as {tenant_id}/{entity_type}/{YYYY}/{MM}/{stored_name}, SHA-256 checksum via crypto.createHash('sha256').update(buffer).digest('hex'), source_module derived from auth context with priority: req.module.code for combined mode (e.g., 'coc', 'dhf'), 'platform' for JWT-only users, req.user.moduleCode for API key-only modules (e.g., 'aice', 'dhf'), or 'api_key' fallback if API key has no linked module. File is saved to storage via storageProvider.save(relativePath, buffer), then database record is inserted into file_attachments. If DB INSERT fails, file is automatically cleaned up from storage (orphan prevention). Redis cache is invalidated for entity files list. Elasticsearch index is updated via IndexingHooks. Returns full file object with 201 status.\n\n2. Upload Multiple Files: POST /api/v1/files/upload/multiple accepts up to 10 files via multer array ('files' field, max 10). All files are uploaded in parallel via Promise.all, sharing the same entity_type and entity_id. Returns array of results with per-file success/error status. Quota is checked per file individually.\n\n3. Update Metadata: PUT /api/v1/files/:id updates allowed fields: description (text), category (varchar), tags (cast as text[]), metadata (cast as JSONB). File content changes go through versioning (POST /:id/versions), not metadata update. Sets updated_at timestamp. Returns 'No valid fields to update' error if no recognized fields provided. Returns full file object with 200 status.\n\n4. Soft Delete: DELETE /api/v1/files/:id sets deleted_at = NOW() timestamp. PostgreSQL trigger (trg_update_storage_usage) automatically decrements used_storage_bytes and file_count in storage_quotas using GREATEST(..., 0) to prevent negative values. File remains on disk for potential recovery. Redis cache invalidated. Elasticsearch index updated. Returns { success: true, message: 'File deleted' }.\n\n5. Hard Delete: DELETE /api/v1/files/:id?hard=true permanently removes: database record (DELETE FROM file_attachments WHERE id = $1 AND tenant_id = $2), physical file from storage via storageProvider.delete(file_path), thumbnail if exists via storageProvider.delete(thumbnail_path). Returns 404 on subsequent GET. Irreversible. Quota adjusts via DELETE trigger.\n\n\n\n5.2 Versioning:\n\nMinor Versioning: Default behavior. Every POST /api/v1/files/:id/versions with new file (multipart) performs: fetches current record by ID and tenant_id, marks it as is_current_version=false via UPDATE, inserts new record with version = previous_version + 1, is_current_version=true, previous_version_id = old record ID. SHA-256 checksum is calculated for the new file. Both old and new records remain in database --- version history is immutable and never deleted.\n\nVersion History Retrieval: GET /api/v1/files/:id/versions uses a recursive CTE (Common Table Expression) that traverses the previous_version_id chain in both directions (forward via self-join on previous_version_id, backward via matching previous_version_id = id). Returns complete version lineage with: id, version, file_size, checksum, original_name, uploaded_by_email (resolved via LEFT JOIN users), created_at for each version. Ordered by version number ascending.\n\nVersion Chain Structure: file_attachments.previous_version_id creates a self-referencing linked list. Version 1 has previous_version_id=null. Version 2 points to Version 1. Version 3 points to Version 2. Only the latest version has is_current_version=true. Standard queries (list, get by entity) only return current versions (WHERE is_current_version = true AND deleted_at IS NULL).\n\n\n\n5.3 Read/Search Path:\n\nGet by ID: GET /api/v1/files/:id --- Redis cached (300s TTL). Returns full file object with uploaded_by_email resolved via LEFT JOIN users. Filters by tenant_id for isolation.\n\nList Files: GET /api/v1/files?limit=50&offset=0 --- returns a JSON array of file objects. Supports limit and offset query parameters for pagination. Supports filters: source_module, mime_type, entity_type, category, search (ILIKE on original_name), sort_by (created_at|file_size|original_name), sort_order (ASC|DESC), created_after, created_before. Only returns active files (deleted_at IS NULL, is_current_version = true).\n\nGet by Entity: GET /api/v1/files/entity/:entity_type/:entity_id --- returns a JSON array of all files linked to a specific entity. Used by Document Service, CAPA module, Training module, etc. to retrieve their linked files.\n\nStorage Stats: GET /api/v1/files/stats --- returns four sections: quota (max_storage, used_storage, available, usage_percent), files (total, images, pdfs, documents counts), by_entity (count + size per entity_type), and by_module (count + size per source_module e.g., 'platform', 'coc').\n\nStorage Quota: GET /api/v1/files/quota --- returns current quota status: max_storage_bytes, used_storage_bytes, file_count, max_file_size_bytes, allowed_mime_types, blocked_extensions, usage_percentage.\n\n\n\n5.4 File Sharing Path:\n\nCreate Share: POST /api/v1/files/:id/share with body: share_type ('link'|'user'), permissions ('view'|'download'), expires_at (ISO timestamp) or expires_in_hours (number, auto-converted), max_downloads (integer), shared_with_user_id, shared_with_email. Service generates 64-char hex token via crypto.randomBytes(32). Returns share object with share_token, share_url, expires_at, max_downloads. Redis cached (3600s TTL).\n\nAccess Shared File: GET /api/v1/files/shared/:token --- PUBLIC route (no authentication). Service: looks up share by token (Redis cached), validates expiry (expires_at > NOW()), validates download count (current_downloads < max_downloads), increments current_downloads, resolves file path via storageProvider.getFullPath(), serves file with correct Content-Type and Content-Disposition headers. Returns 404 if share not found, expired, or download limit reached.\n\nRevoke Share: DELETE /api/v1/files/shares/:shareId --- sets is_active=false. Invalidates Redis cache for the share token.\n\n\n\n5.5 Retention/Deletion:\n\nPhase 1: No automated retention policy enforcement. Soft-deleted files remain in database with deleted_at timestamp set. Hard delete permanently removes file and database record.\n\nQuota Recovery: Soft delete triggers automatic quota decrement via PostgreSQL trigger. Restoring a soft-deleted file (clearing deleted_at) would trigger quota increment.\n\nPlanned Phase 2: Configurable retention periods per entity_type. Scheduled cleanup job to hard-delete files past retention period. Archived storage tier for long-term retention at reduced cost.\n\n\n\n5.6 Audit Trail:\n\nfile_access_logs records (inserted on download, preview, and any file access): file_id, tenant_id, user_id, action ('download'|'preview'|'access'), ip_address (from req.ip), user_agent (from req.headers), created_at.\n\nStorage quota changes tracked via PostgreSQL trigger --- every INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE on file_attachments automatically adjusts used_storage_bytes and file_count.\n\nShare access tracked via current_downloads counter and last_accessed_at timestamp on file_shares.\n\nAuthentication mode impacts audit attribution: In combined JWT + API Key mode, uploaded_by and file_access_logs.user_id record the real user identity (from JWT), while source_module records the originating module (from API key). This provides GxP-compliant traceability --- auditors can see both who performed the action and which module facilitated it. In API Key-only mode, uploaded_by records the module service account and source_module records the module code. In JWT-only mode, uploaded_by records the platform user and source_module is set to 'platform'.\n\n\n\n6. PostgreSQL DB --- MODEL\n\nThe File Attachment Service uses 4 PostgreSQL tables: file_attachments (master record with binary file metadata, entity linking, versioning, and storage path), storage_quotas (per-tenant storage limits with trigger-maintained usage counters), file_shares (token-based file sharing with expiry and download limits), and file_access_logs (audit trail for file access events). All tables enforce multi-tenant isolation via tenant_id foreign key with ON DELETE CASCADE. The file_attachments table serves as the central entity. A PostgreSQL trigger function (update_storage_usage) automatically maintains used_storage_bytes and file_count in storage_quotas on INSERT, UPDATE (soft-delete/restore), and DELETE operations.\n\n6.1 ER Diagram:\n\n\n\nFigure 6.1: Entity Relationship Diagram --- File Attachment Service\n\nfile_attachments.tenant_id → tenants.id (ON DELETE CASCADE)\n\nfile_attachments.uploaded_by → users.id\n\nfile_attachments.previous_version_id → file_attachments.id (self-referencing version chain)\n\nstorage_quotas.tenant_id → tenants.id (UNIQUE --- one quota per tenant)\n\nfile_shares.tenant_id → tenants.id, file_shares.file_id → file_attachments.id\n\nfile_access_logs.file_id → file_attachments.id, file_access_logs.user_id → users.id\n\n6.2 Entities & Attributes:\n\n6.2.1 Entity: file_attachments (32 columns)\n\n\n\nAttribute\n\nData Type\n\nConstraint / FK\n\nDescription\n\nExample\n\nid\n\nUUID\n\nPK, DEFAULT gen_random_uuid()\n\nUnique file identifier\n\ndaf3c8ad-...\n\ntenant_id\n\nUUID\n\nNOT NULL, FK → tenants(id) ON DELETE CASCADE, INDEX\n\nTenant isolation\n\nc0000000-...\n\noriginal_name\n\nVARCHAR(500)\n\nNOT NULL\n\nUser-uploaded filename\n\ntest-upload.txt\n\nstored_name\n\nVARCHAR(500)\n\nNOT NULL\n\nUUID-based stored name\n\ndaf3c8ad_test-upload.txt\n\nfile_path\n\nVARCHAR(1000)\n\nNOT NULL\n\nRelative storage path\n\nc000.../documents/2026/02/daf3...txt\n\nmime_type\n\nVARCHAR(255)\n\nNOT NULL\n\nMIME type\n\ntext/plain\n\nfile_size\n\nBIGINT\n\nNOT NULL\n\nFile size in bytes\n\n18\n\nchecksum\n\nVARCHAR(128)\n\nNullable\n\nSHA-256 hash\n\na591a6d40...\n\nstorage_provider\n\nVARCHAR(50)\n\nDEFAULT 'local'\n\nStorage backend\n\nlocal\n\nstorage_bucket\n\nVARCHAR(255)\n\nNullable\n\nS3 bucket (Phase 2)\n\nnull\n\nstorage_region\n\nVARCHAR(100)\n\nNullable\n\nS3 region (Phase 2)\n\nnull\n\nentity_type\n\nVARCHAR(100)\n\nNOT NULL, INDEX\n\nParent entity type\n\ndocuments\n\nentity_id\n\nUUID\n\nNOT NULL, INDEX\n\nParent entity ID\n\nUUID\n\ncategory\n\nVARCHAR(100)\n\nNullable\n\nFile category\n\nspecification\n\ndescription\n\nTEXT\n\nNullable\n\nFile description\n\nTest file upload\n\ntags\n\nTEXT[]\n\nNullable\n\nPostgreSQL text array\n\n{sop,quality}\n\nversion\n\nINTEGER\n\nDEFAULT 1\n\nVersion number\n\n1\n\nis_current_version\n\nBOOLEAN\n\nDEFAULT true, INDEX (partial)\n\nActive version flag\n\ntrue\n\nprevious_version_id\n\nUUID\n\nFK → file_attachments(id), Nullable\n\nVersion chain link\n\nUUID or null\n\nis_public\n\nBOOLEAN\n\nDEFAULT false\n\nPublic access flag\n\nfalse\n\naccess_level\n\nVARCHAR(50)\n\nDEFAULT 'private'\n\nAccess level\n\nprivate\n\ndownload_count\n\nINTEGER\n\nDEFAULT 0\n\nTotal downloads\n\n5\n\nhas_thumbnail\n\nBOOLEAN\n\nDEFAULT false\n\nThumbnail exists\n\nfalse\n\nthumbnail_path\n\nVARCHAR(1000)\n\nNullable\n\nThumbnail storage path\n\nnull\n\nmetadata\n\nJSONB\n\nDEFAULT '{}'\n\nExtensible metadata\n\n{}\n\nscan_status\n\nVARCHAR(50)\n\nNullable\n\nVirus scan status (Phase 2)\n\nnull\n\nscanned_at\n\nTIMESTAMPTZ\n\nNullable\n\nScan timestamp (Phase 2)\n\nnull\n\nuploaded_by\n\nUUID\n\nFK → users(id), Nullable\n\nUploader user ID\n\nUUID\n\nsource_module\n\nVARCHAR(100)\n\nDEFAULT 'platform', INDEX\n\nOrigin module\n\nplatform\n\ncreated_at\n\nTIMESTAMPTZ\n\nDEFAULT NOW()\n\nCreated timestamp\n\nISO datetime\n\nupdated_at\n\nTIMESTAMPTZ\n\nDEFAULT NOW()\n\nUpdated timestamp\n\nISO datetime\n\ndeleted_at\n\nTIMESTAMPTZ\n\nNullable, INDEX\n\nSoft delete timestamp\n\nnull\n\n\n\nNOTES: tags is TEXT[] not JSONB. file_size is BIGINT (returns as string from node-pg, parsed to integer in _formatAttachment). is_current_version partial index filters WHERE deleted_at IS NULL AND is_current_version = TRUE. previous_version_id creates a self-referencing linked list for version chains. source_module is auto-derived from auth context, never user-supplied.\n\n\n\n6.2.2 Entity: storage_quotas (10 columns)\n\n\n\nAttribute\n\nData Type\n\nConstraint / FK\n\nDescription\n\nExample\n\nid\n\nUUID\n\nPK, DEFAULT gen_random_uuid()\n\nQuota record ID\n\nUUID\n\ntenant_id\n\nUUID\n\nNOT NULL, FK → tenants(id), UNIQUE\n\nTenant isolation\n\nc0000000-...\n\nmax_storage_bytes\n\nBIGINT\n\nDEFAULT 10737418240 (10GB)\n\nMax total storage\n\n10737418240\n\nused_storage_bytes\n\nBIGINT\n\nDEFAULT 0\n\nCurrent usage (trigger-maintained)\n\n1536\n\nfile_count\n\nINTEGER\n\nDEFAULT 0\n\nActive file count (trigger-maintained)\n\n15\n\nmax_file_size_bytes\n\nBIGINT\n\nDEFAULT 104857600 (100MB)\n\nMax single file size\n\n104857600\n\nallowed_mime_types\n\nTEXT[]\n\nNullable\n\nWhitelist (null = all allowed)\n\nnull\n\nblocked_extensions\n\nTEXT[]\n\nNullable\n\nBlacklist\n\n{.exe,.bat}\n\ncreated_at\n\nTIMESTAMPTZ\n\nDEFAULT NOW()\n\nCreated timestamp\n\nISO datetime\n\nupdated_at\n\nTIMESTAMPTZ\n\nDEFAULT NOW()\n\nUpdated timestamp\n\nISO datetime\n\n\n\nNOTES: max_storage_bytes and used_storage_bytes are BIGINT. PostgreSQL trigger update_storage_usage() maintains used_storage_bytes and file_count automatically on file_attachments INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE. The trigger uses GREATEST(..., 0) to prevent negative values. Quota auto-creates via service when first upload occurs for a tenant.\n\n\n\nPostgreSQL Trigger Function:\n\nCREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION update_storage_usage() --- Handles INSERT (increment), DELETE (decrement), UPDATE with NEW.deleted_at IS NOT NULL AND OLD.deleted_at IS NULL (soft-delete decrement), UPDATE with NEW.deleted_at IS NULL AND OLD.deleted_at IS NOT NULL (restore increment). Uses GREATEST(used_storage_bytes - file_size, 0) and GREATEST(file_count - 1, 0) to prevent negative values.\n\n6.2.3 Entity: file_shares (15 columns)\n\n\n\nAttribute\n\nData Type\n\nConstraint / FK\n\nDescription\n\nExample\n\nid\n\nUUID\n\nPK, DEFAULT gen_random_uuid()\n\nShare record ID\n\nUUID\n\ntenant_id\n\nUUID\n\nNOT NULL, FK → tenants(id) ON DELETE CASCADE\n\nTenant isolation\n\nc0000000-...\n\nfile_id\n\nUUID\n\nNOT NULL, FK → file_attachments(id) ON DELETE CASCADE\n\nShared file\n\nUUID\n\nshare_token\n\nVARCHAR(255)\n\nNOT NULL, UNIQUE\n\n64-char hex token\n\na3f8c2d1...\n\nshare_type\n\nVARCHAR(50)\n\nDEFAULT 'link'\n\nShare type\n\nlink\n\nshared_with_user_id\n\nUUID\n\nFK → users(id), Nullable\n\nTarget user (for 'user' type)\n\nnull\n\nshared_with_email\n\nVARCHAR(255)\n\nNullable\n\nTarget email\n\nnull\n\npermissions\n\nVARCHAR(50)\n\nDEFAULT 'view'\n\nAccess level\n\nview/download\n\nexpires_at\n\nTIMESTAMPTZ\n\nNullable\n\nExpiry timestamp\n\nISO datetime\n\nmax_downloads\n\nINTEGER\n\nNullable\n\nDownload cap (null = unlimited)\n\n5\n\ncurrent_downloads\n\nINTEGER\n\nDEFAULT 0\n\nDownloads consumed\n\n2\n\nis_active\n\nBOOLEAN\n\nDEFAULT true\n\nActive flag (false = revoked)\n\ntrue\n\nlast_accessed_at\n\nTIMESTAMPTZ\n\nNullable\n\nLast access time\n\nISO datetime\n\ncreated_by\n\nUUID\n\nFK → users(id), Nullable\n\nShare creator\n\nUUID\n\ncreated_at\n\nTIMESTAMPTZ\n\nDEFAULT NOW()\n\nCreated timestamp\n\nISO datetime\n\n\n\nNOTES: share_token is UNIQUE. expires_at can be set directly or auto-calculated from expires_in_hours (service converts: new Date(Date.now() + hours * 3600000).toISOString()). max_downloads null means unlimited. current_downloads is incremented atomically on each access. is_active=false means share has been revoked.\n\n\n\n6.2.4 Entity: file_access_logs (8 columns)\n\n\n\nAttribute\n\nData Type\n\nConstraint / FK\n\nDescription\n\nExample\n\nid\n\nUUID\n\nPK, DEFAULT gen_random_uuid()\n\nLog record ID\n\nUUID\n\ntenant_id\n\nUUID\n\nNOT NULL, FK → tenants(id) ON DELETE CASCADE\n\nTenant isolation\n\nUUID\n\nfile_id\n\nUUID\n\nNOT NULL, FK → file_attachments(id) ON DELETE CASCADE\n\nFile accessed\n\nUUID\n\nuser_id\n\nUUID\n\nNOT NULL, FK → users(id)\n\nWho accessed\n\nUUID\n\naction\n\nVARCHAR(50)\n\nNOT NULL\n\nAction type\n\ndownload/preview/access\n\nip_address\n\nINET\n\nNullable\n\nClient IP\n\n192.168.1.1\n\nuser_agent\n\nTEXT\n\nNullable\n\nBrowser UA string\n\nMozilla/5.0...\n\ncreated_at\n\nTIMESTAMPTZ\n\nDEFAULT NOW()\n\nWhen accessed\n\nISO datetime\n\n\n\nNOTES: Records are insert-only (append-only audit log). ip_address and user_agent are captured from Express request object when available. action values include 'download', 'preview', 'access' for share access.\n\n\n\n7. Qdrant (Vector DB)\n\nPhase 1 Status: NOT USED by File Attachment Service.\n\nBinary file content is stored on the filesystem via LocalStorageProvider. File metadata is stored in PostgreSQL (file_attachments table). No vector embeddings of file content are generated in Phase 1.\n\n\n\nPlanned for Phase 2:\n\nCollection Name: file_content_vec_v1\n\nVector Dimension: 1536 (OpenAI text-embedding-3-small) or 768 (sentence-transformers)\n\nPayload Fields: file_id (string), tenant_id (string --- mandatory filter), original_name (string), entity_type (string), entity_id (string), mime_type (string), category (string), source_module (string), created_at (ISO string)\n\nPayload Indexes: tenant_id, entity_type, mime_type, source_module, created_at\n\nUse Case: Semantic search across extracted file content (PDF text, document text), similar file discovery, AI-powered content recommendations, duplicate detection via vector similarity rather than checksum matching.\n\nPrerequisite: Content extraction pipeline (PDF text extraction, OCR for images, document parsing) must be implemented first to generate text from binary files before vector embedding.\n\n\n\n8. Object Storage\n\nFolder Structure Hierarchy:\n\nRoot: UPLOAD_DIR (default: ./uploads/, configurable via environment variable)\n\nPattern: {UPLOAD_DIR}/{tenant_id}/{entity_type}/{YYYY}/{MM}/{uuid}_{sanitized_name}.ext\n\n\n\nExample hierarchy:\n\nuploads/\n\n  c0000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001/   (Tenant: Pharma Corp)\n\n    documents/   (Entity type)\n\n      2026/   (Year)\n\n        02/   (Month)\n\n          daf3c8ad-3660-4c13-8b57-0daf3cc9aaf0_test-upload.txt\n\n          d7cbcb77-a8f1-4e22-b901-3dc77f8e22a1_report-v2.pdf\n\n    capa/\n\n      2026/\n\n        02/\n\n          f1a2b3c4-..._corrective-action-photo.jpg\n\n    training/\n\n      2026/\n\n        01/\n\n          a9b8c7d6-..._sop-training-material.pdf\n\n  d0000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000002/   (Tenant: Another Org)\n\n    documents/\n\n    suppliers/\n\n    temp/   (Temporary storage)\n\n    thumbnails/   (Thumbnail storage)\n\n\n\nStorage Provider Abstraction:\n\nLocalStorageProvider (Phase 1 --- current):\n\nsave(relativePath, buffer) --- Creates directory tree via fs.mkdirSync({recursive: true}), writes buffer via fs.writeFileSync.\n\ndelete(relativePath) --- Removes file via fs.unlinkSync. Silently ignores if file doesn't exist.\n\nexists(relativePath) --- Checks via fs.existsSync.\n\ngetFullPath(relativePath) --- Joins UPLOAD_DIR + relativePath. Controller uses path.resolve() for absolute path.\n\nS3 Migration Path (Phase 2):\n\nImplement S3StorageProvider with identical interface (save, delete, exists, getFullPath). S3 key structure maps 1:1 to local folder hierarchy: s3://{bucket}/{tenant_id}/{entity_type}/{YYYY}/{MM}/{uuid}_{name}.ext. No changes to service or controller code required --- swap provider via configuration.\n\n\n\nSECTION 10 --- REDIS CACHE DETAILS\n\nFile Attachment Service uses 4 Redis cache key patterns with graceful degradation. The service operates normally if Redis is unavailable --- all cache operations are wrapped in try/catch and failures are logged but never block the main operation.\n\n\n\nCache Key Pattern\n\nPurpose\n\nTTL\n\nInvalidation\n\nfile:{tenant_id}:{file_id}\n\nSingle file metadata cache\n\n300s (5 min)\n\nOn update, delete\n\nquota:{tenant_id}\n\nTenant storage quota\n\n60s (1 min)\n\nOn upload, delete, quota update\n\nshare:{share_token}\n\nShare token lookup\n\n3600s (1 hr)\n\nOn revoke\n\nentity_files:{entity_type}:{entity_id}\n\nFiles by entity list\n\n120s (2 min)\n\nOn upload, delete to entity\n\n\n\nRedis Service Integration:\n\nFile: src/infrastructure/cache/redis.service.js --- singleton instance exported as module.exports = new RedisService().\n\nMethods used: get(key, parseJson=true), set(key, value, ttlSeconds), delete(key). Note: The Redis service uses delete() method, NOT del().\n\nCache invalidation method: _invalidateCache(tenantId, fileId, entityType, entityId) --- deletes all relevant cache keys after any write operation (upload, update, delete, share revoke).\n\nLazy initialization: getRedis() function returns singleton Redis instance on first call, returns null if Redis is unavailable. Every cache operation checks for null before proceeding.\n\n\n\nPlatform-level Redis usage that benefits File Attachment Service indirectly:\n\nSession caching: JWT session data cached by AuthService for fast auth validation on every request.\n\nPermission caching: User permissions cached by PermissionService, used by authorization middleware for files:read/files:write/files:admin checks.\n\n\n\nPlanned Phase 2 enhancements:\n\nFile content cache: Large frequently-accessed files cached in Redis for sub-millisecond retrieval.\n\nShare token bloom filter: Probabilistic check before DB lookup for invalid/revoked tokens.\n\nUpload rate limiting: Per-tenant upload rate tracking via Redis INCR + EXPIRE.	<p>SERVICE SPECIFICATION</p><p><strong>File Attachment Services</strong></p><p>QAssure Multi-Tenant SaaS Platform</p><p>Version 1.0   ·   February 2026</p><h1>1. Feature of Service</h1><h3>SERVICE NAME: FILE ATTACHMENT SERVICES</h3><p>Centralised, tenant-isolated, binary file lifecycle engine that governs how files (documents, images, reports, specifications, audit evidence, training materials) are uploaded, stored, versioned, downloaded, previewed, shared, and deleted across multi-tenant regulated environments. All binary file operations across the QAssure platform and module applications are routed through this single service.</p><p><strong>Core capabilities:</strong></p><p><strong>1. File Upload &amp; Storage</strong> --- Single and multi-file upload (up to 10 files per request) with multer memory storage, 100MB max file size enforcement, blocked dangerous extensions (.exe, .bat, .cmd, .sh, .ps1, .msi, .dll, .com, .scr, .vbs, .js), SHA-256 checksum calculation via crypto.createHash('sha256'), and automatic tenant-scoped folder hierarchy: {tenant_id}/{entity_type}/{YYYY}/{MM}/{uuid}_{sanitized_name}.ext. Files are linked to any platform entity via entity_type + entity_id (e.g., entity_type='documents', entity_id=document_uuid). Source module is auto-derived from authentication context: module code for combined JWT + API Key mode (e.g., 'coc', 'dhf') where module identity is captured from the API key even though user identity comes from JWT; 'platform' for JWT-only users; moduleCode for API key-only modules (e.g., 'aice', 'dhf'); or 'api_key' as fallback if an API key has no linked module. Priority order: req.module.code (combined mode) → req.user.moduleCode (API key mode) → 'platform' (JWT mode). Duplicate detection prevents re-upload of identical files to the same entity (enforced by checksum + entity match on active records).</p><p><strong>2. File Versioning</strong> --- Each file supports immutable version history. Uploading a new version via POST /:id/versions marks the previous version as is_current_version=false and creates a new record with incremented version number, linked via previous_version_id. Version history is retrieved via recursive CTE (Common Table Expression) that traverses the chain in both directions, returning complete lineage. Only the current version is returned in standard queries.</p><p><strong>3. Download &amp; Preview</strong> --- Download endpoint (GET /:id/download) serves files with Content-Disposition: attachment header for browser download prompt. Preview endpoint (GET /:id/preview) serves with Content-Disposition: inline for in-browser rendering. Both use storageProvider.getFullPath() with path.resolve() for absolute path resolution. File access is logged in file_access_logs table with user_id, IP address, user agent, and action type.</p><p><strong>4. File Sharing with Token-Based Access</strong> --- Create time-limited, download-limited share links via POST /:id/share. Generates 64-character hex token via crypto.randomBytes(32). Supports configurable parameters: expires_at (absolute timestamp), expires_in_hours (convenience --- auto-converted to expires_at), max_downloads (download cap), share_type ('link' or 'user'), shared_with_user_id, shared_with_email, and permissions ('view' or 'download'). Access via GET /shared/:token is a PUBLIC route (no authentication required). The service validates token existence, checks expiry, enforces max_downloads limit, increments current_downloads counter, and serves the file. Share revocation via DELETE /shares/:shareId deactivates the share.</p><p><strong>5. Storage Quota Management</strong> --- Per-tenant storage quotas tracked in storage_quotas table: max_storage_bytes (default 10GB), used_storage_bytes, file_count, max_file_size_bytes (default 100MB), allowed_mime_types, blocked_extensions. Quota auto-creates on first upload if not present. PostgreSQL trigger (trg_update_storage_usage) automatically increments/decrements used_storage_bytes and file_count on INSERT, DELETE, and UPDATE (soft-delete via SET deleted_at). Quota is checked before every upload: individual file size vs max_file_size_bytes, and total used + new file vs max_storage_bytes. Admin-only PUT /quota endpoint allows updating quota limits (requires files:admin permission).</p><p><strong>6. Soft Delete with Hard Delete Option</strong> --- Default DELETE /:id performs soft delete (sets deleted_at timestamp). PostgreSQL trigger automatically decrements quota on soft delete. Hard delete via DELETE /:id?hard=true permanently removes the database record AND the physical file from storage via storageProvider.delete(). Orphaned file cleanup: if database INSERT fails after file is saved to disk, the file is automatically cleaned up in the catch block.</p><p><strong>7. Storage Provider Abstraction (S3-Ready)</strong> --- All file I/O goes through LocalStorageProvider with 4 methods: save(relativePath, buffer), delete(relativePath), exists(relativePath), getFullPath(relativePath). No controller or service code touches the filesystem directly. Migrating to S3 requires implementing an S3StorageProvider with the same interface --- zero code changes in controllers or service logic.</p><p><strong>8. Three Authentication Modes</strong> --- Every endpoint (except public share access) supports three authentication modes via authenticate middleware: (a) JWT only --- QAssure UI users authenticate via Authorization: Bearer header; tenant derived from JWT claims; user permissions enforced via authorize(); (b) JWT + API Key combined --- module applications acting on behalf of a logged-in user send both Authorization: Bearer (JWT from verify-launch-token) and X-API-Key headers; tenant derived from JWT (X-Tenant-ID not needed); user permissions enforced (not module bypass); source_module set from module identity (e.g., 'coc', 'dhf') for accurate audit attribution; (c) API Key + X-Tenant-ID --- module system/cron operations with no user context send X-API-Key and X-Tenant-ID headers; module gets full access bypass on authorize(). This enables QAssure UI users, module applications acting on behalf of users (with real user identity in audit trails), and module background processes to consume the file attachment service. Routes use authorize() for permission checks: files:read for GET endpoints, files:write for POST/PUT/DELETE endpoints, files:admin for quota management.</p><p><strong>9. Multi-Tenant Data Isolation</strong> --- All queries are tenant-scoped. Every file, share, quota, and access log belongs to a tenant_id. API Key middleware validates module-tenant assignment via the tenant_module_access table (enabled = true) before granting access. Missing X-Tenant-ID returns error code MISSING_TENANT. Unassigned tenant returns MODULE_NOT_ASSIGNED. In combined mode (JWT + API Key), if the JWT's tenant does not match the API key's assigned tenant, error code TENANT_MISMATCH is returned; if the module is deactivated for the tenant, error code MODULE_NOT_ACTIVE is returned. Users cannot see or access files belonging to other tenants under any circumstance.</p><p><strong>10. Security Hardening</strong> --- UUID validation middleware on all :id route parameters returns 400 (INVALID_ID_FORMAT) for malformed UUIDs instead of leaking PostgreSQL errors. Error sanitization function (sanitizeErrorResponse) on all 17 controller catch blocks classifies errors into client-safe categories (QUOTA_ERROR, NOT_FOUND, VALIDATION_ERROR, etc.) and returns generic 'An internal error occurred' for unexpected failures --- never exposing constraint names, table structures, or query details. Blocked file extensions prevent executable uploads. Path traversal is impossible because stored names use UUID-based naming, not user-controlled paths.</p><p><strong>11. Observability</strong> --- Redis caching with graceful degradation (file cache 300s TTL, quota 60s, share 3600s, entity files 120s). Elasticsearch indexing for file searchability. File access logging with IP/user-agent tracking. Storage statistics with per-entity-type and per-module breakdowns. All infrastructure dependencies (Redis, Elasticsearch) fail gracefully --- the service continues operating without caching or indexing if either is unavailable.</p><h1>2. Scope</h1><h2>IN SCOPE (Phase 1):</h2><p><strong>Full file CRUD:</strong> upload single file (with auto-generated tenant-scoped storage path, checksum, source_module derivation), upload multiple files (up to 10, parallel via Promise.all), read (single by ID with Redis cache, paginated list with filtering, by entity with pagination), update metadata (description, tags, category, metadata JSONB), soft delete (with quota trigger), hard delete (with physical file removal).</p><p><strong>File versioning:</strong> upload new version (marks previous as not current, increments version number, links via previous_version_id), retrieve version history (recursive CTE traversal in both directions).</p><p><strong>Download and preview</strong> with correct Content-Disposition headers (attachment for download, inline for preview), file access logging, and path resolution via storageProvider abstraction.</p><p><strong>File sharing:</strong> create share link with configurable expiry (expires_at or expires_in_hours), max_downloads cap, share_type (link/user), permissions (view/download). Public access via token (no auth required). Share revocation. Expired/exhausted shares return 404.</p><p><strong>Storage quota management:</strong> auto-create on first upload, per-tenant limits (max_storage_bytes, max_file_size_bytes, allowed_mime_types, blocked_extensions), quota enforcement on every upload, admin-only quota configuration updates (files:admin permission), PostgreSQL trigger for automatic used_storage_bytes/file_count tracking on INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE.</p><p><strong>Three authentication modes</strong> (JWT only, JWT + API Key combined, API Key + X-Tenant-ID) on all 16 authenticated endpoints via authenticate middleware (1 public route excluded: GET /shared/:token). Combined mode derives tenant from JWT (X-Tenant-ID not needed), enforces user permissions, and sets source_module from module identity for accurate audit attribution.</p><p>Multi-tenant isolation on all read and write operations.</p><p>API Key middleware validates module-tenant access via tenant_module_access table.</p><p>UUID validation middleware on all :id parameters --- returns 400 for invalid UUIDs.</p><p>Error sanitization on all 17 catch blocks --- never leaks database internals.</p><p>File access audit trail via file_access_logs with user_id, IP address, user agent, action.</p><p>Storage statistics with quota summary, file type counts, by_entity and by_module breakdowns.</p><p>Pagination via limit and offset query parameters (default limit 50). Response is a JSON array of file objects.</p><p>Filter support on list: source_module, mime_type, entity_type, category, search (ILIKE on original_name), sort (created_at, file_size, original_name), date range (created_after, created_before).</p><p>Orphaned file cleanup: if DB INSERT fails after file save, file is automatically removed from storage.</p><p>Redis caching with graceful degradation (operates without Redis).</p><p>Elasticsearch indexing with graceful degradation (operates without ES).</p><h2>OUT OF SCOPE (Phase 1 --- File Attachment Service boundary):</h2><p><strong>S3/MinIO object storage migration</strong> --- LocalStorageProvider is production-ready; S3StorageProvider is a config swap planned for Phase 2. Storage provider abstraction is already in place.</p><p><strong>Virus/malware scanning</strong> --- scan_status and scanned_at columns exist in file_attachments table but no scanning service is integrated.</p><p><strong>Image thumbnail generation</strong> --- has_thumbnail, thumbnail_path columns exist but no thumbnail generation pipeline is implemented.</p><p>Full-text content extraction from uploaded files (PDF text extraction, OCR).</p><p>Vector embedding of file content for semantic search via Qdrant.</p><p>Real-time file upload progress (WebSocket-based progress tracking).</p><p>Client-side chunked upload for files larger than 100MB.</p><p>CDN integration for cached file delivery.</p><p>File format conversion (PDF to image, document to PDF, etc.).</p><p>Watermarking or digital stamping of downloaded files.</p><p>Bulk import/export of files.</p><p><strong>File-level access control lists (ACLs)</strong> --- access is controlled at entity level via entity_type permissions, not per-file.</p><p><strong>Retention policy enforcement</strong> --- soft-deleted files remain in database indefinitely until hard-deleted.</p><p><strong>Email/SMS notifications on file events</strong> (handled by Notification Service separately).</p><p><strong>Document lifecycle management</strong> (SOPs, versioning, review/approval) --- handled by separate Document Service (document.service.js).</p><h1>3. Clear Responsibility Split</h1><h2>3.1 SaaS Platform (Owned by Platform Team):</h2><p>Provide and maintain the File Attachment Service as a centralised, tenant-isolated binary file management engine.</p><p>Enforce file upload constraints: 100MB max file size (multer + quota), blocked dangerous extensions, per-tenant storage quotas with auto-creation and trigger-based tracking.</p><p>Generate and manage tenant-scoped storage paths: {tenant_id}/{entity_type}/{YYYY}/{MM}/{uuid}_{sanitized_name}.ext.</p><p>Calculate SHA-256 checksums on every upload for integrity verification.</p><p>Auto-derive source_module from authentication context with priority: req.module.code for combined JWT + API Key mode (e.g., 'coc', 'dhf'), req.user.moduleCode for API key-only modules, 'platform' for JWT-only users, 'api_key' fallback for API keys without linked modules. This ensures files uploaded by modules on behalf of users are correctly attributed to the originating module in storage statistics.</p><p>Manage file versioning: version number increment, previous_version_id linking, is_current_version flagging, recursive CTE version history retrieval.</p><p>Maintain storage provider abstraction (LocalStorageProvider) with save, delete, exists, getFullPath methods for future S3 migration.</p><p>Enforce tenant isolation on all queries via tenant_id filtering. No cross-tenant file access is possible.</p><p>Provide three authentication modes on all 16 authenticated endpoints via authenticate middleware (1 public route excluded: GET /shared/:token): (a) JWT only for QAssure UI users, (b) JWT + API Key combined for module applications acting on behalf of logged-in users (tenant from JWT, user permissions enforced, source_module from module identity), (c) API Key + X-Tenant-ID for module system/cron operations.</p><p>Validate module-tenant access via tenant_module_access table (enabled = true check). Return MISSING_TENANT or MODULE_NOT_ASSIGNED errors for invalid API Key requests. In combined mode, return TENANT_MISMATCH if JWT tenant differs from API key's assigned tenant, and MODULE_NOT_ACTIVE if the module is deactivated for the tenant.</p><p>Provide UUID validation middleware for all route parameters. Return 400 INVALID_ID_FORMAT for malformed UUIDs.</p><p>Sanitise all error responses to prevent database internal leakage.</p><p>Provide file sharing infrastructure: token generation, expiry enforcement, max_downloads enforcement, public access route.</p><p>Provide storage statistics with per-entity-type and per-module breakdowns.</p><p>Log all file access events (download, preview, checkout, checkin) in file_access_logs.</p><p>Provide Redis caching and Elasticsearch indexing with graceful degradation.</p><p>Expose admin-only quota management endpoint (files:admin permission).</p><h2>3.2 Module Applications (Residing on Platform; Owned by App Teams --- LTTS):</h2><p>Use the File Attachment Service API with the appropriate authentication mode:</p><p><strong>For user-context operations (user uploads a file via module UI):</strong> Send both Authorization: Bearer (JWT obtained from verify-launch-token) and X-API-Key headers. X-Tenant-ID is NOT needed --- tenant is derived from JWT. User permissions (files:read, files:write) are enforced. uploaded_by and file_access_logs.user_id record the real user identity. source_module records the module identity (e.g., 'coc').</p><p><strong>For system/cron operations (no user context):</strong> Send X-API-Key + X-Tenant-ID headers. Module gets full access bypass. source_module records the module identity from API key.</p><p>Specify entity_type and entity_id when uploading files to link them to the correct module entity (e.g., entity_type='documents', entity_id=&lt;document_uuid&gt; for Document Service; entity_type='capa', entity_id=&lt;capa_uuid&gt; for CAPA module).</p><p>Provide required fields on upload: file (multipart), entity_type (string), entity_id (UUID). Optional fields: description, category, tags, metadata (JSONB).</p><p>Use the version upload endpoint (POST /:id/versions) for updating files rather than deleting and re-uploading. This preserves version history and audit trail.</p><p>Use the share endpoint to generate time-limited, download-limited links for external stakeholders (e.g., auditors, suppliers) without granting platform login access.</p><p>Do NOT implement custom file storage logic --- always delegate to platform's file attachment service.</p><p>Do NOT store file metadata in the module's own database --- use platform as the system of record for all file attachments. Use the entity link (entity_type + entity_id) to associate files with module records.</p><p>Do NOT construct file paths manually or access the uploads directory directly --- always use service API endpoints for file access.</p><p>Use SSO launch token flow to obtain both tenant context and user session JWT dynamically in production: call POST /modules/verify-launch-token with X-API-Key and the launch token to receive a JWT (24h expiry), user details (including department, designation, user category), tenant details, and permissions. Use this JWT in Authorization: Bearer header alongside X-API-Key for all user-context platform calls. Use .env TENANT_ID + X-Tenant-ID header for development and system/cron operations only.</p><h1>4. API List (Refer to Excel Sheet shared)</h1><table><thead><tr><th><p><strong>#</strong></p></th><th><p><strong>Method</strong></p></th><th><p><strong>Endpoint</strong></p></th><th><p><strong>Auth</strong></p></th><th><p><strong>Description</strong></p></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><p><strong>1</strong></p></td><td><p><strong>POST</strong></p></td><td><p>/api/v1/files/upload</p></td><td><p>authenticate + files:write</p></td><td><p>Upload single file (multipart)</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>2</strong></p></td><td><p><strong>POST</strong></p></td><td><p>/api/v1/files/upload/multiple</p></td><td><p>authenticate + files:write</p></td><td><p>Upload multiple files (up to 10)</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>3</strong></p></td><td><p><strong>GET</strong></p></td><td><p>/api/v1/files</p></td><td><p>authenticate + files:read</p></td><td><p>List files (paginated, filterable)</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>4</strong></p></td><td><p><strong>GET</strong></p></td><td><p>/api/v1/files/:id</p></td><td><p>authenticate + files:read + validateUUID</p></td><td><p>Get single file by ID</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>5</strong></p></td><td><p><strong>PUT</strong></p></td><td><p>/api/v1/files/:id</p></td><td><p>authenticate + files:write + validateUUID</p></td><td><p>Update file metadata</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>6</strong></p></td><td><p><strong>DELETE</strong></p></td><td><p>/api/v1/files/:id</p></td><td><p>authenticate + files:write + validateUUID</p></td><td><p>Delete file (soft; ?hard=true for permanent)</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>7</strong></p></td><td><p><strong>GET</strong></p></td><td><p>/api/v1/files/:id/download</p></td><td><p>authenticate + files:read + validateUUID</p></td><td><p>Download file (Content-Disposition: attachment)</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>8</strong></p></td><td><p><strong>GET</strong></p></td><td><p>/api/v1/files/:id/preview</p></td><td><p>authenticate + files:read + validateUUID</p></td><td><p>Preview file (Content-Disposition: inline)</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>9</strong></p></td><td><p><strong>POST</strong></p></td><td><p>/api/v1/files/:id/versions</p></td><td><p>authenticate + files:write + validateUUID</p></td><td><p>Upload new version of existing file</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>10</strong></p></td><td><p><strong>GET</strong></p></td><td><p>/api/v1/files/:id/versions</p></td><td><p>authenticate + files:read + validateUUID</p></td><td><p>Get version history (recursive CTE)</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>11</strong></p></td><td><p><strong>POST</strong></p></td><td><p>/api/v1/files/:id/share</p></td><td><p>authenticate + files:write + validateUUID</p></td><td><p>Create share link (token, expiry, max_downloads)</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>12</strong></p></td><td><p><strong>DELETE</strong></p></td><td><p>/api/v1/files/shares/:shareId</p></td><td><p>authenticate + files:write + validateUUID</p></td><td><p>Revoke share link</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>13</strong></p></td><td><p><strong>GET</strong></p></td><td><p>/api/v1/files/shared/:token</p></td><td><p>PUBLIC (no auth)</p></td><td><p>Access shared file via token</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>14</strong></p></td><td><p><strong>GET</strong></p></td><td><p>/api/v1/files/entity/:entity_type/:entity_id</p></td><td><p>authenticate + files:read + validateUUID</p></td><td><p>Get files by entity (paginated)</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>15</strong></p></td><td><p><strong>GET</strong></p></td><td><p>/api/v1/files/stats</p></td><td><p>authenticate + files:read</p></td><td><p>Get storage statistics (by entity, by module)</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>16</strong></p></td><td><p><strong>GET</strong></p></td><td><p>/api/v1/files/quota</p></td><td><p>authenticate + files:read</p></td><td><p>Get tenant storage quota</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>17</strong></p></td><td><p><strong>PUT</strong></p></td><td><p>/api/v1/files/quota</p></td><td><p>authenticate + files:admin</p></td><td><p>Update tenant storage quota (admin only)</p></td></tr></tbody></table><p><em>Route ordering note: /stats, /quota, /entity/:type/:id, /shared/:token are registered BEFORE /:id to prevent Express matching static segments as UUID parameters.</em></p><h1>5. Typical Workflow</h1><h2>5.1 Write Path (File Upload &amp; Management):</h2><p><strong>1. Upload Single File:</strong> Tenant user or module calls POST /api/v1/files/upload with multipart form data: file (required, binary), entity_type (required, e.g., 'documents'), entity_id (required, UUID of parent entity), and optional: description, category, tags (comma-separated string or JSON array), metadata (JSONB). The API validates: file presence (returns VALIDATION_ERROR 'No file uploaded'), entity_type and entity_id presence (returns VALIDATION_ERROR 'entity_type and entity_id are required'), file extension not in blocked list (.exe, .bat, .cmd, .sh, .ps1, .msi, .dll, .com, .scr, .vbs, .js --- returns error 'File type .exe is not allowed'), file size under 100MB (returns FILE_TOO_LARGE 'File size exceeds 100MB limit'), tenant quota not exceeded (returns QUOTA_ERROR for both individual file and total storage).</p><p>Platform generates: stored_name as {uuid}_{sanitized_original_name}.ext, storage path as {tenant_id}/{entity_type}/{YYYY}/{MM}/{stored_name}, SHA-256 checksum via crypto.createHash('sha256').update(buffer).digest('hex'), source_module derived from auth context with priority: req.module.code for combined mode (e.g., 'coc', 'dhf'), 'platform' for JWT-only users, req.user.moduleCode for API key-only modules (e.g., 'aice', 'dhf'), or 'api_key' fallback if API key has no linked module. File is saved to storage via storageProvider.save(relativePath, buffer), then database record is inserted into file_attachments. If DB INSERT fails, file is automatically cleaned up from storage (orphan prevention). Redis cache is invalidated for entity files list. Elasticsearch index is updated via IndexingHooks. Returns full file object with 201 status.</p><p><strong>2. Upload Multiple Files:</strong> POST /api/v1/files/upload/multiple accepts up to 10 files via multer array ('files' field, max 10). All files are uploaded in parallel via Promise.all, sharing the same entity_type and entity_id. Returns array of results with per-file success/error status. Quota is checked per file individually.</p><p><strong>3. Update Metadata:</strong> PUT /api/v1/files/:id updates allowed fields: description (text), category (varchar), tags (cast as text[]), metadata (cast as JSONB). File content changes go through versioning (POST /:id/versions), not metadata update. Sets updated_at timestamp. Returns 'No valid fields to update' error if no recognized fields provided. Returns full file object with 200 status.</p><p><strong>4. Soft Delete:</strong> DELETE /api/v1/files/:id sets deleted_at = NOW() timestamp. PostgreSQL trigger (trg_update_storage_usage) automatically decrements used_storage_bytes and file_count in storage_quotas using GREATEST(..., 0) to prevent negative values. File remains on disk for potential recovery. Redis cache invalidated. Elasticsearch index updated. Returns { success: true, message: 'File deleted' }.</p><p><strong>5. Hard Delete:</strong> DELETE /api/v1/files/:id?hard=true permanently removes: database record (DELETE FROM file_attachments WHERE id = $1 AND tenant_id = $2), physical file from storage via storageProvider.delete(file_path), thumbnail if exists via storageProvider.delete(thumbnail_path). Returns 404 on subsequent GET. Irreversible. Quota adjusts via DELETE trigger.</p><h2>5.2 Versioning:</h2><p><strong>Minor Versioning:</strong> Default behavior. Every POST /api/v1/files/:id/versions with new file (multipart) performs: fetches current record by ID and tenant_id, marks it as is_current_version=false via UPDATE, inserts new record with version = previous_version + 1, is_current_version=true, previous_version_id = old record ID. SHA-256 checksum is calculated for the new file. Both old and new records remain in database --- version history is immutable and never deleted.</p><p><strong>Version History Retrieval:</strong> GET /api/v1/files/:id/versions uses a recursive CTE (Common Table Expression) that traverses the previous_version_id chain in both directions (forward via self-join on previous_version_id, backward via matching previous_version_id = id). Returns complete version lineage with: id, version, file_size, checksum, original_name, uploaded_by_email (resolved via LEFT JOIN users), created_at for each version. Ordered by version number ascending.</p><p><strong>Version Chain Structure:</strong> file_attachments.previous_version_id creates a self-referencing linked list. Version 1 has previous_version_id=null. Version 2 points to Version 1. Version 3 points to Version 2. Only the latest version has is_current_version=true. Standard queries (list, get by entity) only return current versions (WHERE is_current_version = true AND deleted_at IS NULL).</p><h2>5.3 Read/Search Path:</h2><p><strong>Get by ID:</strong> GET /api/v1/files/:id --- Redis cached (300s TTL). Returns full file object with uploaded_by_email resolved via LEFT JOIN users. Filters by tenant_id for isolation.</p><p><strong>List Files:</strong> GET /api/v1/files?limit=50&amp;offset=0 --- returns a JSON array of file objects. Supports limit and offset query parameters for pagination. Supports filters: source_module, mime_type, entity_type, category, search (ILIKE on original_name), sort_by (created_at|file_size|original_name), sort_order (ASC|DESC), created_after, created_before. Only returns active files (deleted_at IS NULL, is_current_version = true).</p><p><strong>Get by Entity:</strong> GET /api/v1/files/entity/:entity_type/:entity_id --- returns a JSON array of all files linked to a specific entity. Used by Document Service, CAPA module, Training module, etc. to retrieve their linked files.</p><p><strong>Storage Stats:</strong> GET /api/v1/files/stats --- returns four sections: quota (max_storage, used_storage, available, usage_percent), files (total, images, pdfs, documents counts), by_entity (count + size per entity_type), and by_module (count + size per source_module e.g., 'platform', 'coc').</p><p><strong>Storage Quota:</strong> GET /api/v1/files/quota --- returns current quota status: max_storage_bytes, used_storage_bytes, file_count, max_file_size_bytes, allowed_mime_types, blocked_extensions, usage_percentage.</p><h2>5.4 File Sharing Path:</h2><p><strong>Create Share:</strong> POST /api/v1/files/:id/share with body: share_type ('link'|'user'), permissions ('view'|'download'), expires_at (ISO timestamp) or expires_in_hours (number, auto-converted), max_downloads (integer), shared_with_user_id, shared_with_email. Service generates 64-char hex token via crypto.randomBytes(32). Returns share object with share_token, share_url, expires_at, max_downloads. Redis cached (3600s TTL).</p><p><strong>Access Shared File:</strong> GET /api/v1/files/shared/:token --- PUBLIC route (no authentication). Service: looks up share by token (Redis cached), validates expiry (expires_at &gt; NOW()), validates download count (current_downloads &lt; max_downloads), increments current_downloads, resolves file path via storageProvider.getFullPath(), serves file with correct Content-Type and Content-Disposition headers. Returns 404 if share not found, expired, or download limit reached.</p><p><strong>Revoke Share:</strong> DELETE /api/v1/files/shares/:shareId --- sets is_active=false. Invalidates Redis cache for the share token.</p><h2>5.5 Retention/Deletion:</h2><p>Phase 1: No automated retention policy enforcement. Soft-deleted files remain in database with deleted_at timestamp set. Hard delete permanently removes file and database record.</p><p><strong>Quota Recovery:</strong> Soft delete triggers automatic quota decrement via PostgreSQL trigger. Restoring a soft-deleted file (clearing deleted_at) would trigger quota increment.</p><p><strong>Planned Phase 2:</strong> Configurable retention periods per entity_type. Scheduled cleanup job to hard-delete files past retention period. Archived storage tier for long-term retention at reduced cost.</p><h2>5.6 Audit Trail:</h2><p>file_access_logs records (inserted on download, preview, and any file access): file_id, tenant_id, user_id, action ('download'|'preview'|'access'), ip_address (from req.ip), user_agent (from req.headers), created_at.</p><p>Storage quota changes tracked via PostgreSQL trigger --- every INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE on file_attachments automatically adjusts used_storage_bytes and file_count.</p><p>Share access tracked via current_downloads counter and last_accessed_at timestamp on file_shares.</p><p><strong>Authentication mode impacts audit attribution:</strong> In combined JWT + API Key mode, uploaded_by and file_access_logs.user_id record the real user identity (from JWT), while source_module records the originating module (from API key). This provides GxP-compliant traceability --- auditors can see both who performed the action and which module facilitated it. In API Key-only mode, uploaded_by records the module service account and source_module records the module code. In JWT-only mode, uploaded_by records the platform user and source_module is set to 'platform'.</p><h1>6. PostgreSQL DB --- MODEL</h1><p>The File Attachment Service uses 4 PostgreSQL tables: file_attachments (master record with binary file metadata, entity linking, versioning, and storage path), storage_quotas (per-tenant storage limits with trigger-maintained usage counters), file_shares (token-based file sharing with expiry and download limits), and file_access_logs (audit trail for file access events). All tables enforce multi-tenant isolation via tenant_id foreign key with ON DELETE CASCADE. The file_attachments table serves as the central entity. A PostgreSQL trigger function (update_storage_usage) automatically maintains used_storage_bytes and file_count in storage_quotas on INSERT, UPDATE (soft-delete/restore), and DELETE operations.</p><h2>6.1 ER Diagram:</h2><p><img src="data:image/png;base64," /></p><p><em>Figure 6.1: Entity Relationship Diagram --- File Attachment Service</em></p><p>file_attachments.tenant_id → tenants.id (ON DELETE CASCADE)</p><p>file_attachments.uploaded_by → users.id</p><p>file_attachments.previous_version_id → file_attachments.id (self-referencing version chain)</p><p>storage_quotas.tenant_id → tenants.id (UNIQUE --- one quota per tenant)</p><p>file_shares.tenant_id → tenants.id, file_shares.file_id → file_attachments.id</p><p>file_access_logs.file_id → file_attachments.id, file_access_logs.user_id → users.id</p><h2>6.2 Entities &amp; Attributes:</h2><h3>6.2.1 Entity: file_attachments (32 columns)</h3><table><thead><tr><th><p><strong>Attribute</strong></p></th><th><p><strong>Data Type</strong></p></th><th><p><strong>Constraint / FK</strong></p></th><th><p><strong>Description</strong></p></th><th><p><strong>Example</strong></p></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><p><strong>id</strong></p></td><td><p>UUID</p></td><td><p>PK, DEFAULT gen_random_uuid()</p></td><td><p>Unique file identifier</p></td><td><p>daf3c8ad-...</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>tenant_id</strong></p></td><td><p>UUID</p></td><td><p>NOT NULL, FK → tenants(id) ON DELETE CASCADE, INDEX</p></td><td><p>Tenant isolation</p></td><td><p>c0000000-...</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>original_name</strong></p></td><td><p>VARCHAR(500)</p></td><td><p>NOT NULL</p></td><td><p>User-uploaded filename</p></td><td><p>test-upload.txt</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>stored_name</strong></p></td><td><p>VARCHAR(500)</p></td><td><p>NOT NULL</p></td><td><p>UUID-based stored name</p></td><td><p>daf3c8ad_test-upload.txt</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>file_path</strong></p></td><td><p>VARCHAR(1000)</p></td><td><p>NOT NULL</p></td><td><p>Relative storage path</p></td><td><p>c000.../documents/2026/02/daf3...txt</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>mime_type</strong></p></td><td><p>VARCHAR(255)</p></td><td><p>NOT NULL</p></td><td><p>MIME type</p></td><td><p>text/plain</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>file_size</strong></p></td><td><p>BIGINT</p></td><td><p>NOT NULL</p></td><td><p>File size in bytes</p></td><td><p>18</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>checksum</strong></p></td><td><p>VARCHAR(128)</p></td><td><p>Nullable</p></td><td><p>SHA-256 hash</p></td><td><p>a591a6d40...</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>storage_provider</strong></p></td><td><p>VARCHAR(50)</p></td><td><p>DEFAULT 'local'</p></td><td><p>Storage backend</p></td><td><p>local</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>storage_bucket</strong></p></td><td><p>VARCHAR(255)</p></td><td><p>Nullable</p></td><td><p>S3 bucket (Phase 2)</p></td><td><p>null</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>storage_region</strong></p></td><td><p>VARCHAR(100)</p></td><td><p>Nullable</p></td><td><p>S3 region (Phase 2)</p></td><td><p>null</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>entity_type</strong></p></td><td><p>VARCHAR(100)</p></td><td><p>NOT NULL, INDEX</p></td><td><p>Parent entity type</p></td><td><p>documents</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>entity_id</strong></p></td><td><p>UUID</p></td><td><p>NOT NULL, INDEX</p></td><td><p>Parent entity ID</p></td><td><p>UUID</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>category</strong></p></td><td><p>VARCHAR(100)</p></td><td><p>Nullable</p></td><td><p>File category</p></td><td><p>specification</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>description</strong></p></td><td><p>TEXT</p></td><td><p>Nullable</p></td><td><p>File description</p></td><td><p>Test file upload</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>tags</strong></p></td><td><p>TEXT[]</p></td><td><p>Nullable</p></td><td><p>PostgreSQL text array</p></td><td><p>{sop,quality}</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>version</strong></p></td><td><p>INTEGER</p></td><td><p>DEFAULT 1</p></td><td><p>Version number</p></td><td><p>1</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>is_current_version</strong></p></td><td><p>BOOLEAN</p></td><td><p>DEFAULT true, INDEX (partial)</p></td><td><p>Active version flag</p></td><td><p>true</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>previous_version_id</strong></p></td><td><p>UUID</p></td><td><p>FK → file_attachments(id), Nullable</p></td><td><p>Version chain link</p></td><td><p>UUID or null</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>is_public</strong></p></td><td><p>BOOLEAN</p></td><td><p>DEFAULT false</p></td><td><p>Public access flag</p></td><td><p>false</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>access_level</strong></p></td><td><p>VARCHAR(50)</p></td><td><p>DEFAULT 'private'</p></td><td><p>Access level</p></td><td><p>private</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>download_count</strong></p></td><td><p>INTEGER</p></td><td><p>DEFAULT 0</p></td><td><p>Total downloads</p></td><td><p>5</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>has_thumbnail</strong></p></td><td><p>BOOLEAN</p></td><td><p>DEFAULT false</p></td><td><p>Thumbnail exists</p></td><td><p>false</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>thumbnail_path</strong></p></td><td><p>VARCHAR(1000)</p></td><td><p>Nullable</p></td><td><p>Thumbnail storage path</p></td><td><p>null</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>metadata</strong></p></td><td><p>JSONB</p></td><td><p>DEFAULT '{}'</p></td><td><p>Extensible metadata</p></td><td><p>{}</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>scan_status</strong></p></td><td><p>VARCHAR(50)</p></td><td><p>Nullable</p></td><td><p>Virus scan status (Phase 2)</p></td><td><p>null</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>scanned_at</strong></p></td><td><p>TIMESTAMPTZ</p></td><td><p>Nullable</p></td><td><p>Scan timestamp (Phase 2)</p></td><td><p>null</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>uploaded_by</strong></p></td><td><p>UUID</p></td><td><p>FK → users(id), Nullable</p></td><td><p>Uploader user ID</p></td><td><p>UUID</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>source_module</strong></p></td><td><p>VARCHAR(100)</p></td><td><p>DEFAULT 'platform', INDEX</p></td><td><p>Origin module</p></td><td><p>platform</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>created_at</strong></p></td><td><p>TIMESTAMPTZ</p></td><td><p>DEFAULT NOW()</p></td><td><p>Created timestamp</p></td><td><p>ISO datetime</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>updated_at</strong></p></td><td><p>TIMESTAMPTZ</p></td><td><p>DEFAULT NOW()</p></td><td><p>Updated timestamp</p></td><td><p>ISO datetime</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>deleted_at</strong></p></td><td><p>TIMESTAMPTZ</p></td><td><p>Nullable, INDEX</p></td><td><p>Soft delete timestamp</p></td><td><p>null</p></td></tr></tbody></table><p><em>NOTES: tags is TEXT[] not JSONB. file_size is BIGINT (returns as string from node-pg, parsed to integer in _formatAttachment). is_current_version partial index filters WHERE deleted_at IS NULL AND is_current_version = TRUE. previous_version_id creates a self-referencing linked list for version chains. source_module is auto-derived from auth context, never user-supplied.</em></p><h3>6.2.2 Entity: storage_quotas (10 columns)</h3><table><thead><tr><th><p><strong>Attribute</strong></p></th><th><p><strong>Data Type</strong></p></th><th><p><strong>Constraint / FK</strong></p></th><th><p><strong>Description</strong></p></th><th><p><strong>Example</strong></p></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><p><strong>id</strong></p></td><td><p>UUID</p></td><td><p>PK, DEFAULT gen_random_uuid()</p></td><td><p>Quota record ID</p></td><td><p>UUID</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>tenant_id</strong></p></td><td><p>UUID</p></td><td><p>NOT NULL, FK → tenants(id), UNIQUE</p></td><td><p>Tenant isolation</p></td><td><p>c0000000-...</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>max_storage_bytes</strong></p></td><td><p>BIGINT</p></td><td><p>DEFAULT 10737418240 (10GB)</p></td><td><p>Max total storage</p></td><td><p>10737418240</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>used_storage_bytes</strong></p></td><td><p>BIGINT</p></td><td><p>DEFAULT 0</p></td><td><p>Current usage (trigger-maintained)</p></td><td><p>1536</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>file_count</strong></p></td><td><p>INTEGER</p></td><td><p>DEFAULT 0</p></td><td><p>Active file count (trigger-maintained)</p></td><td><p>15</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>max_file_size_bytes</strong></p></td><td><p>BIGINT</p></td><td><p>DEFAULT 104857600 (100MB)</p></td><td><p>Max single file size</p></td><td><p>104857600</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>allowed_mime_types</strong></p></td><td><p>TEXT[]</p></td><td><p>Nullable</p></td><td><p>Whitelist (null = all allowed)</p></td><td><p>null</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>blocked_extensions</strong></p></td><td><p>TEXT[]</p></td><td><p>Nullable</p></td><td><p>Blacklist</p></td><td><p>{.exe,.bat}</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>created_at</strong></p></td><td><p>TIMESTAMPTZ</p></td><td><p>DEFAULT NOW()</p></td><td><p>Created timestamp</p></td><td><p>ISO datetime</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>updated_at</strong></p></td><td><p>TIMESTAMPTZ</p></td><td><p>DEFAULT NOW()</p></td><td><p>Updated timestamp</p></td><td><p>ISO datetime</p></td></tr></tbody></table><p><em>NOTES: max_storage_bytes and used_storage_bytes are BIGINT. PostgreSQL trigger update_storage_usage() maintains used_storage_bytes and file_count automatically on file_attachments INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE. The trigger uses GREATEST(..., 0) to prevent negative values. Quota auto-creates via service when first upload occurs for a tenant.</em></p><h3>PostgreSQL Trigger Function:</h3><p>CREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION update_storage_usage() --- Handles INSERT (increment), DELETE (decrement), UPDATE with NEW.deleted_at IS NOT NULL AND OLD.deleted_at IS NULL (soft-delete decrement), UPDATE with NEW.deleted_at IS NULL AND OLD.deleted_at IS NOT NULL (restore increment). Uses GREATEST(used_storage_bytes - file_size, 0) and GREATEST(file_count - 1, 0) to prevent negative values.</p><h3>6.2.3 Entity: file_shares (15 columns)</h3><table><thead><tr><th><p><strong>Attribute</strong></p></th><th><p><strong>Data Type</strong></p></th><th><p><strong>Constraint / FK</strong></p></th><th><p><strong>Description</strong></p></th><th><p><strong>Example</strong></p></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><p><strong>id</strong></p></td><td><p>UUID</p></td><td><p>PK, DEFAULT gen_random_uuid()</p></td><td><p>Share record ID</p></td><td><p>UUID</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>tenant_id</strong></p></td><td><p>UUID</p></td><td><p>NOT NULL, FK → tenants(id) ON DELETE CASCADE</p></td><td><p>Tenant isolation</p></td><td><p>c0000000-...</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>file_id</strong></p></td><td><p>UUID</p></td><td><p>NOT NULL, FK → file_attachments(id) ON DELETE CASCADE</p></td><td><p>Shared file</p></td><td><p>UUID</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>share_token</strong></p></td><td><p>VARCHAR(255)</p></td><td><p>NOT NULL, UNIQUE</p></td><td><p>64-char hex token</p></td><td><p>a3f8c2d1...</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>share_type</strong></p></td><td><p>VARCHAR(50)</p></td><td><p>DEFAULT 'link'</p></td><td><p>Share type</p></td><td><p>link</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>shared_with_user_id</strong></p></td><td><p>UUID</p></td><td><p>FK → users(id), Nullable</p></td><td><p>Target user (for 'user' type)</p></td><td><p>null</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>shared_with_email</strong></p></td><td><p>VARCHAR(255)</p></td><td><p>Nullable</p></td><td><p>Target email</p></td><td><p>null</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>permissions</strong></p></td><td><p>VARCHAR(50)</p></td><td><p>DEFAULT 'view'</p></td><td><p>Access level</p></td><td><p>view/download</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>expires_at</strong></p></td><td><p>TIMESTAMPTZ</p></td><td><p>Nullable</p></td><td><p>Expiry timestamp</p></td><td><p>ISO datetime</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>max_downloads</strong></p></td><td><p>INTEGER</p></td><td><p>Nullable</p></td><td><p>Download cap (null = unlimited)</p></td><td><p>5</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>current_downloads</strong></p></td><td><p>INTEGER</p></td><td><p>DEFAULT 0</p></td><td><p>Downloads consumed</p></td><td><p>2</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>is_active</strong></p></td><td><p>BOOLEAN</p></td><td><p>DEFAULT true</p></td><td><p>Active flag (false = revoked)</p></td><td><p>true</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>last_accessed_at</strong></p></td><td><p>TIMESTAMPTZ</p></td><td><p>Nullable</p></td><td><p>Last access time</p></td><td><p>ISO datetime</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>created_by</strong></p></td><td><p>UUID</p></td><td><p>FK → users(id), Nullable</p></td><td><p>Share creator</p></td><td><p>UUID</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>created_at</strong></p></td><td><p>TIMESTAMPTZ</p></td><td><p>DEFAULT NOW()</p></td><td><p>Created timestamp</p></td><td><p>ISO datetime</p></td></tr></tbody></table><p><em>NOTES: share_token is UNIQUE. expires_at can be set directly or auto-calculated from expires_in_hours (service converts: new Date(Date.now() + hours * 3600000).toISOString()). max_downloads null means unlimited. current_downloads is incremented atomically on each access. is_active=false means share has been revoked.</em></p><h3>6.2.4 Entity: file_access_logs (8 columns)</h3><table><thead><tr><th><p><strong>Attribute</strong></p></th><th><p><strong>Data Type</strong></p></th><th><p><strong>Constraint / FK</strong></p></th><th><p><strong>Description</strong></p></th><th><p><strong>Example</strong></p></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><p><strong>id</strong></p></td><td><p>UUID</p></td><td><p>PK, DEFAULT gen_random_uuid()</p></td><td><p>Log record ID</p></td><td><p>UUID</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>tenant_id</strong></p></td><td><p>UUID</p></td><td><p>NOT NULL, FK → tenants(id) ON DELETE CASCADE</p></td><td><p>Tenant isolation</p></td><td><p>UUID</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>file_id</strong></p></td><td><p>UUID</p></td><td><p>NOT NULL, FK → file_attachments(id) ON DELETE CASCADE</p></td><td><p>File accessed</p></td><td><p>UUID</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>user_id</strong></p></td><td><p>UUID</p></td><td><p>NOT NULL, FK → users(id)</p></td><td><p>Who accessed</p></td><td><p>UUID</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>action</strong></p></td><td><p>VARCHAR(50)</p></td><td><p>NOT NULL</p></td><td><p>Action type</p></td><td><p>download/preview/access</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>ip_address</strong></p></td><td><p>INET</p></td><td><p>Nullable</p></td><td><p>Client IP</p></td><td><p>192.168.1.1</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>user_agent</strong></p></td><td><p>TEXT</p></td><td><p>Nullable</p></td><td><p>Browser UA string</p></td><td><p>Mozilla/5.0...</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>created_at</strong></p></td><td><p>TIMESTAMPTZ</p></td><td><p>DEFAULT NOW()</p></td><td><p>When accessed</p></td><td><p>ISO datetime</p></td></tr></tbody></table><p><em>NOTES: Records are insert-only (append-only audit log). ip_address and user_agent are captured from Express request object when available. action values include 'download', 'preview', 'access' for share access.</em></p><h1>7. Qdrant (Vector DB)</h1><p><strong>Phase 1 Status: NOT USED by File Attachment Service.</strong></p><p>Binary file content is stored on the filesystem via LocalStorageProvider. File metadata is stored in PostgreSQL (file_attachments table). No vector embeddings of file content are generated in Phase 1.</p><h2>Planned for Phase 2:</h2><p>Collection Name: file_content_vec_v1</p><p>Vector Dimension: 1536 (OpenAI text-embedding-3-small) or 768 (sentence-transformers)</p><p>Payload Fields: file_id (string), tenant_id (string --- mandatory filter), original_name (string), entity_type (string), entity_id (string), mime_type (string), category (string), source_module (string), created_at (ISO string)</p><p>Payload Indexes: tenant_id, entity_type, mime_type, source_module, created_at</p><p>Use Case: Semantic search across extracted file content (PDF text, document text), similar file discovery, AI-powered content recommendations, duplicate detection via vector similarity rather than checksum matching.</p><p>Prerequisite: Content extraction pipeline (PDF text extraction, OCR for images, document parsing) must be implemented first to generate text from binary files before vector embedding.</p><h1>8. Object Storage</h1><h2>Folder Structure Hierarchy:</h2><p>Root: UPLOAD_DIR (default: ./uploads/, configurable via environment variable)</p><p>Pattern: {UPLOAD_DIR}/{tenant_id}/{entity_type}/{YYYY}/{MM}/{uuid}_{sanitized_name}.ext</p><p><strong>Example hierarchy:</strong></p><p>uploads/</p><p>  c0000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001/   (Tenant: Pharma Corp)</p><p>    documents/   (Entity type)</p><p>      2026/   (Year)</p><p>        02/   (Month)</p><p>          daf3c8ad-3660-4c13-8b57-0daf3cc9aaf0_test-upload.txt</p><p>          d7cbcb77-a8f1-4e22-b901-3dc77f8e22a1_report-v2.pdf</p><p>    capa/</p><p>      2026/</p><p>        02/</p><p>          f1a2b3c4-..._corrective-action-photo.jpg</p><p>    training/</p><p>      2026/</p><p>        01/</p><p>          a9b8c7d6-..._sop-training-material.pdf</p><p>  d0000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000002/   (Tenant: Another Org)</p><p>    documents/</p><p>    suppliers/</p><p>    temp/   (Temporary storage)</p><p>    thumbnails/   (Thumbnail storage)</p><h3>Storage Provider Abstraction:</h3><p>LocalStorageProvider (Phase 1 --- current):</p><p>save(relativePath, buffer) --- Creates directory tree via fs.mkdirSync({recursive: true}), writes buffer via fs.writeFileSync.</p><p>delete(relativePath) --- Removes file via fs.unlinkSync. Silently ignores if file doesn't exist.</p><p>exists(relativePath) --- Checks via fs.existsSync.</p><p>getFullPath(relativePath) --- Joins UPLOAD_DIR + relativePath. Controller uses path.resolve() for absolute path.</p><h3>S3 Migration Path (Phase 2):</h3><p>Implement S3StorageProvider with identical interface (save, delete, exists, getFullPath). S3 key structure maps 1:1 to local folder hierarchy: s3://{bucket}/{tenant_id}/{entity_type}/{YYYY}/{MM}/{uuid}_{name}.ext. No changes to service or controller code required --- swap provider via configuration.</p><h1>SECTION 10 --- REDIS CACHE DETAILS</h1><p>File Attachment Service uses 4 Redis cache key patterns with graceful degradation. The service operates normally if Redis is unavailable --- all cache operations are wrapped in try/catch and failures are logged but never block the main operation.</p><table><thead><tr><th><p><strong>Cache Key Pattern</strong></p></th><th><p><strong>Purpose</strong></p></th><th><p><strong>TTL</strong></p></th><th><p><strong>Invalidation</strong></p></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><p>file:{tenant_id}:{file_id}</p></td><td><p>Single file metadata cache</p></td><td><p>300s (5 min)</p></td><td><p>On update, delete</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>quota:{tenant_id}</p></td><td><p>Tenant storage quota</p></td><td><p>60s (1 min)</p></td><td><p>On upload, delete, quota update</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>share:{share_token}</p></td><td><p>Share token lookup</p></td><td><p>3600s (1 hr)</p></td><td><p>On revoke</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>entity_files:{entity_type}:{entity_id}</p></td><td><p>Files by entity list</p></td><td><p>120s (2 min)</p></td><td><p>On upload, delete to entity</p></td></tr></tbody></table><h3>Redis Service Integration:</h3><p>File: src/infrastructure/cache/redis.service.js --- singleton instance exported as module.exports = new RedisService().</p><p>Methods used: get(key, parseJson=true), set(key, value, ttlSeconds), delete(key). Note: The Redis service uses delete() method, NOT del().</p><p>Cache invalidation method: _invalidateCache(tenantId, fileId, entityType, entityId) --- deletes all relevant cache keys after any write operation (upload, update, delete, share revoke).</p><p>Lazy initialization: getRedis() function returns singleton Redis instance on first call, returns null if Redis is unavailable. Every cache operation checks for null before proceeding.</p><h3>Platform-level Redis usage that benefits File Attachment Service indirectly:</h3><p><strong>Session caching:</strong> JWT session data cached by AuthService for fast auth validation on every request.</p><p><strong>Permission caching:</strong> User permissions cached by PermissionService, used by authorization middleware for files:read/files:write/files:admin checks.</p><h3>Planned Phase 2 enhancements:</h3><p><strong>File content cache:</strong> Large frequently-accessed files cached in Redis for sub-millisecond retrieval.</p><p><strong>Share token bloom filter:</strong> Probabilistic check before DB lookup for invalid/revoked tokens.</p><p><strong>Upload rate limiting:</strong> Per-tenant upload rate tracking via Redis INCR + EXPIRE.</p>	Initial draft	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	4ccf68ad-485c-495a-935b-10d74b173a52	2026-03-30 04:54:38.04009+00	Coc	af82560b-71d5-42cc-9b0d-9a4267b5b4ed
f743ca9d-c130-4b55-bf76-0fdf45537859	1a1f3892-b287-4c08-ba06-0bd2ff6a2679	352d7e21-5155-4ff8-8169-284bf5ece3e7	0.1	0	1	approved	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	SERVICE SPECIFICATION\n\nFile Attachment Services\n\n\n\nQAssure Multi-Tenant SaaS Platform\n\nVersion 1.0   ·   February 2026\n\n\n\n1. Feature of Service\n\nSERVICE NAME: FILE ATTACHMENT SERVICES\n\nCentralised, tenant-isolated, binary file lifecycle engine that governs how files (documents, images, reports, specifications, audit evidence, training materials) are uploaded, stored, versioned, downloaded, previewed, shared, and deleted across multi-tenant regulated environments. All binary file operations across the QAssure platform and module applications are routed through this single service.\n\n\n\nCore capabilities:\n\n\n\n1. File Upload & Storage --- Single and multi-file upload (up to 10 files per request) with multer memory storage, 100MB max file size enforcement, blocked dangerous extensions (.exe, .bat, .cmd, .sh, .ps1, .msi, .dll, .com, .scr, .vbs, .js), SHA-256 checksum calculation via crypto.createHash('sha256'), and automatic tenant-scoped folder hierarchy: {tenant_id}/{entity_type}/{YYYY}/{MM}/{uuid}_{sanitized_name}.ext. Files are linked to any platform entity via entity_type + entity_id (e.g., entity_type='documents', entity_id=document_uuid). Source module is auto-derived from authentication context: module code for combined JWT + API Key mode (e.g., 'coc', 'dhf') where module identity is captured from the API key even though user identity comes from JWT; 'platform' for JWT-only users; moduleCode for API key-only modules (e.g., 'aice', 'dhf'); or 'api_key' as fallback if an API key has no linked module. Priority order: req.module.code (combined mode) → req.user.moduleCode (API key mode) → 'platform' (JWT mode). Duplicate detection prevents re-upload of identical files to the same entity (enforced by checksum + entity match on active records).\n\n2. File Versioning --- Each file supports immutable version history. Uploading a new version via POST /:id/versions marks the previous version as is_current_version=false and creates a new record with incremented version number, linked via previous_version_id. Version history is retrieved via recursive CTE (Common Table Expression) that traverses the chain in both directions, returning complete lineage. Only the current version is returned in standard queries.\n\n3. Download & Preview --- Download endpoint (GET /:id/download) serves files with Content-Disposition: attachment header for browser download prompt. Preview endpoint (GET /:id/preview) serves with Content-Disposition: inline for in-browser rendering. Both use storageProvider.getFullPath() with path.resolve() for absolute path resolution. File access is logged in file_access_logs table with user_id, IP address, user agent, and action type.\n\n4. File Sharing with Token-Based Access --- Create time-limited, download-limited share links via POST /:id/share. Generates 64-character hex token via crypto.randomBytes(32). Supports configurable parameters: expires_at (absolute timestamp), expires_in_hours (convenience --- auto-converted to expires_at), max_downloads (download cap), share_type ('link' or 'user'), shared_with_user_id, shared_with_email, and permissions ('view' or 'download'). Access via GET /shared/:token is a PUBLIC route (no authentication required). The service validates token existence, checks expiry, enforces max_downloads limit, increments current_downloads counter, and serves the file. Share revocation via DELETE /shares/:shareId deactivates the share.\n\n5. Storage Quota Management --- Per-tenant storage quotas tracked in storage_quotas table: max_storage_bytes (default 10GB), used_storage_bytes, file_count, max_file_size_bytes (default 100MB), allowed_mime_types, blocked_extensions. Quota auto-creates on first upload if not present. PostgreSQL trigger (trg_update_storage_usage) automatically increments/decrements used_storage_bytes and file_count on INSERT, DELETE, and UPDATE (soft-delete via SET deleted_at). Quota is checked before every upload: individual file size vs max_file_size_bytes, and total used + new file vs max_storage_bytes. Admin-only PUT /quota endpoint allows updating quota limits (requires files:admin permission).\n\n6. Soft Delete with Hard Delete Option --- Default DELETE /:id performs soft delete (sets deleted_at timestamp). PostgreSQL trigger automatically decrements quota on soft delete. Hard delete via DELETE /:id?hard=true permanently removes the database record AND the physical file from storage via storageProvider.delete(). Orphaned file cleanup: if database INSERT fails after file is saved to disk, the file is automatically cleaned up in the catch block.\n\n7. Storage Provider Abstraction (S3-Ready) --- All file I/O goes through LocalStorageProvider with 4 methods: save(relativePath, buffer), delete(relativePath), exists(relativePath), getFullPath(relativePath). No controller or service code touches the filesystem directly. Migrating to S3 requires implementing an S3StorageProvider with the same interface --- zero code changes in controllers or service logic.\n\n8. Three Authentication Modes --- Every endpoint (except public share access) supports three authentication modes via authenticate middleware: (a) JWT only --- QAssure UI users authenticate via Authorization: Bearer header; tenant derived from JWT claims; user permissions enforced via authorize(); (b) JWT + API Key combined --- module applications acting on behalf of a logged-in user send both Authorization: Bearer (JWT from verify-launch-token) and X-API-Key headers; tenant derived from JWT (X-Tenant-ID not needed); user permissions enforced (not module bypass); source_module set from module identity (e.g., 'coc', 'dhf') for accurate audit attribution; (c) API Key + X-Tenant-ID --- module system/cron operations with no user context send X-API-Key and X-Tenant-ID headers; module gets full access bypass on authorize(). This enables QAssure UI users, module applications acting on behalf of users (with real user identity in audit trails), and module background processes to consume the file attachment service. Routes use authorize() for permission checks: files:read for GET endpoints, files:write for POST/PUT/DELETE endpoints, files:admin for quota management.\n\n9. Multi-Tenant Data Isolation --- All queries are tenant-scoped. Every file, share, quota, and access log belongs to a tenant_id. API Key middleware validates module-tenant assignment via the tenant_module_access table (enabled = true) before granting access. Missing X-Tenant-ID returns error code MISSING_TENANT. Unassigned tenant returns MODULE_NOT_ASSIGNED. In combined mode (JWT + API Key), if the JWT's tenant does not match the API key's assigned tenant, error code TENANT_MISMATCH is returned; if the module is deactivated for the tenant, error code MODULE_NOT_ACTIVE is returned. Users cannot see or access files belonging to other tenants under any circumstance.\n\n10. Security Hardening --- UUID validation middleware on all :id route parameters returns 400 (INVALID_ID_FORMAT) for malformed UUIDs instead of leaking PostgreSQL errors. Error sanitization function (sanitizeErrorResponse) on all 17 controller catch blocks classifies errors into client-safe categories (QUOTA_ERROR, NOT_FOUND, VALIDATION_ERROR, etc.) and returns generic 'An internal error occurred' for unexpected failures --- never exposing constraint names, table structures, or query details. Blocked file extensions prevent executable uploads. Path traversal is impossible because stored names use UUID-based naming, not user-controlled paths.\n\n11. Observability --- Redis caching with graceful degradation (file cache 300s TTL, quota 60s, share 3600s, entity files 120s). Elasticsearch indexing for file searchability. File access logging with IP/user-agent tracking. Storage statistics with per-entity-type and per-module breakdowns. All infrastructure dependencies (Redis, Elasticsearch) fail gracefully --- the service continues operating without caching or indexing if either is unavailable.\n\n\n\n2. Scope\n\nIN SCOPE (Phase 1):\n\nFull file CRUD: upload single file (with auto-generated tenant-scoped storage path, checksum, source_module derivation), upload multiple files (up to 10, parallel via Promise.all), read (single by ID with Redis cache, paginated list with filtering, by entity with pagination), update metadata (description, tags, category, metadata JSONB), soft delete (with quota trigger), hard delete (with physical file removal).\n\nFile versioning: upload new version (marks previous as not current, increments version number, links via previous_version_id), retrieve version history (recursive CTE traversal in both directions).\n\nDownload and preview with correct Content-Disposition headers (attachment for download, inline for preview), file access logging, and path resolution via storageProvider abstraction.\n\nFile sharing: create share link with configurable expiry (expires_at or expires_in_hours), max_downloads cap, share_type (link/user), permissions (view/download). Public access via token (no auth required). Share revocation. Expired/exhausted shares return 404.\n\nStorage quota management: auto-create on first upload, per-tenant limits (max_storage_bytes, max_file_size_bytes, allowed_mime_types, blocked_extensions), quota enforcement on every upload, admin-only quota configuration updates (files:admin permission), PostgreSQL trigger for automatic used_storage_bytes/file_count tracking on INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE.\n\nThree authentication modes (JWT only, JWT + API Key combined, API Key + X-Tenant-ID) on all 16 authenticated endpoints via authenticate middleware (1 public route excluded: GET /shared/:token). Combined mode derives tenant from JWT (X-Tenant-ID not needed), enforces user permissions, and sets source_module from module identity for accurate audit attribution.\n\nMulti-tenant isolation on all read and write operations.\n\nAPI Key middleware validates module-tenant access via tenant_module_access table.\n\nUUID validation middleware on all :id parameters --- returns 400 for invalid UUIDs.\n\nError sanitization on all 17 catch blocks --- never leaks database internals.\n\nFile access audit trail via file_access_logs with user_id, IP address, user agent, action.\n\nStorage statistics with quota summary, file type counts, by_entity and by_module breakdowns.\n\nPagination via limit and offset query parameters (default limit 50). Response is a JSON array of file objects.\n\nFilter support on list: source_module, mime_type, entity_type, category, search (ILIKE on original_name), sort (created_at, file_size, original_name), date range (created_after, created_before).\n\nOrphaned file cleanup: if DB INSERT fails after file save, file is automatically removed from storage.\n\nRedis caching with graceful degradation (operates without Redis).\n\nElasticsearch indexing with graceful degradation (operates without ES).\n\n\n\nOUT OF SCOPE (Phase 1 --- File Attachment Service boundary):\n\nS3/MinIO object storage migration --- LocalStorageProvider is production-ready; S3StorageProvider is a config swap planned for Phase 2. Storage provider abstraction is already in place.\n\nVirus/malware scanning --- scan_status and scanned_at columns exist in file_attachments table but no scanning service is integrated.\n\nImage thumbnail generation --- has_thumbnail, thumbnail_path columns exist but no thumbnail generation pipeline is implemented.\n\nFull-text content extraction from uploaded files (PDF text extraction, OCR).\n\nVector embedding of file content for semantic search via Qdrant.\n\nReal-time file upload progress (WebSocket-based progress tracking).\n\nClient-side chunked upload for files larger than 100MB.\n\nCDN integration for cached file delivery.\n\nFile format conversion (PDF to image, document to PDF, etc.).\n\nWatermarking or digital stamping of downloaded files.\n\nBulk import/export of files.\n\nFile-level access control lists (ACLs) --- access is controlled at entity level via entity_type permissions, not per-file.\n\nRetention policy enforcement --- soft-deleted files remain in database indefinitely until hard-deleted.\n\nEmail/SMS notifications on file events (handled by Notification Service separately).\n\nDocument lifecycle management (SOPs, versioning, review/approval) --- handled by separate Document Service (document.service.js).\n\n\n\n3. Clear Responsibility Split\n\n3.1 SaaS Platform (Owned by Platform Team):\n\nProvide and maintain the File Attachment Service as a centralised, tenant-isolated binary file management engine.\n\nEnforce file upload constraints: 100MB max file size (multer + quota), blocked dangerous extensions, per-tenant storage quotas with auto-creation and trigger-based tracking.\n\nGenerate and manage tenant-scoped storage paths: {tenant_id}/{entity_type}/{YYYY}/{MM}/{uuid}_{sanitized_name}.ext.\n\nCalculate SHA-256 checksums on every upload for integrity verification.\n\nAuto-derive source_module from authentication context with priority: req.module.code for combined JWT + API Key mode (e.g., 'coc', 'dhf'), req.user.moduleCode for API key-only modules, 'platform' for JWT-only users, 'api_key' fallback for API keys without linked modules. This ensures files uploaded by modules on behalf of users are correctly attributed to the originating module in storage statistics.\n\nManage file versioning: version number increment, previous_version_id linking, is_current_version flagging, recursive CTE version history retrieval.\n\nMaintain storage provider abstraction (LocalStorageProvider) with save, delete, exists, getFullPath methods for future S3 migration.\n\nEnforce tenant isolation on all queries via tenant_id filtering. No cross-tenant file access is possible.\n\nProvide three authentication modes on all 16 authenticated endpoints via authenticate middleware (1 public route excluded: GET /shared/:token): (a) JWT only for QAssure UI users, (b) JWT + API Key combined for module applications acting on behalf of logged-in users (tenant from JWT, user permissions enforced, source_module from module identity), (c) API Key + X-Tenant-ID for module system/cron operations.\n\nValidate module-tenant access via tenant_module_access table (enabled = true check). Return MISSING_TENANT or MODULE_NOT_ASSIGNED errors for invalid API Key requests. In combined mode, return TENANT_MISMATCH if JWT tenant differs from API key's assigned tenant, and MODULE_NOT_ACTIVE if the module is deactivated for the tenant.\n\nProvide UUID validation middleware for all route parameters. Return 400 INVALID_ID_FORMAT for malformed UUIDs.\n\nSanitise all error responses to prevent database internal leakage.\n\nProvide file sharing infrastructure: token generation, expiry enforcement, max_downloads enforcement, public access route.\n\nProvide storage statistics with per-entity-type and per-module breakdowns.\n\nLog all file access events (download, preview, checkout, checkin) in file_access_logs.\n\nProvide Redis caching and Elasticsearch indexing with graceful degradation.\n\nExpose admin-only quota management endpoint (files:admin permission).\n\n\n\n3.2 Module Applications (Residing on Platform; Owned by App Teams --- LTTS):\n\nUse the File Attachment Service API with the appropriate authentication mode:\n\nFor user-context operations (user uploads a file via module UI): Send both Authorization: Bearer (JWT obtained from verify-launch-token) and X-API-Key headers. X-Tenant-ID is NOT needed --- tenant is derived from JWT. User permissions (files:read, files:write) are enforced. uploaded_by and file_access_logs.user_id record the real user identity. source_module records the module identity (e.g., 'coc').\n\nFor system/cron operations (no user context): Send X-API-Key + X-Tenant-ID headers. Module gets full access bypass. source_module records the module identity from API key.\n\nSpecify entity_type and entity_id when uploading files to link them to the correct module entity (e.g., entity_type='documents', entity_id=<document_uuid> for Document Service; entity_type='capa', entity_id=<capa_uuid> for CAPA module).\n\nProvide required fields on upload: file (multipart), entity_type (string), entity_id (UUID). Optional fields: description, category, tags, metadata (JSONB).\n\nUse the version upload endpoint (POST /:id/versions) for updating files rather than deleting and re-uploading. This preserves version history and audit trail.\n\nUse the share endpoint to generate time-limited, download-limited links for external stakeholders (e.g., auditors, suppliers) without granting platform login access.\n\nDo NOT implement custom file storage logic --- always delegate to platform's file attachment service.\n\nDo NOT store file metadata in the module's own database --- use platform as the system of record for all file attachments. Use the entity link (entity_type + entity_id) to associate files with module records.\n\nDo NOT construct file paths manually or access the uploads directory directly --- always use service API endpoints for file access.\n\nUse SSO launch token flow to obtain both tenant context and user session JWT dynamically in production: call POST /modules/verify-launch-token with X-API-Key and the launch token to receive a JWT (24h expiry), user details (including department, designation, user category), tenant details, and permissions. Use this JWT in Authorization: Bearer header alongside X-API-Key for all user-context platform calls. Use .env TENANT_ID + X-Tenant-ID header for development and system/cron operations only.\n\n\n\n4. API List (Refer to Excel Sheet shared)\n\n\n\n#\n\nMethod\n\nEndpoint\n\nAuth\n\nDescription\n\n1\n\nPOST\n\n/api/v1/files/upload\n\nauthenticate + files:write\n\nUpload single file (multipart)\n\n2\n\nPOST\n\n/api/v1/files/upload/multiple\n\nauthenticate + files:write\n\nUpload multiple files (up to 10)\n\n3\n\nGET\n\n/api/v1/files\n\nauthenticate + files:read\n\nList files (paginated, filterable)\n\n4\n\nGET\n\n/api/v1/files/:id\n\nauthenticate + files:read + validateUUID\n\nGet single file by ID\n\n5\n\nPUT\n\n/api/v1/files/:id\n\nauthenticate + files:write + validateUUID\n\nUpdate file metadata\n\n6\n\nDELETE\n\n/api/v1/files/:id\n\nauthenticate + files:write + validateUUID\n\nDelete file (soft; ?hard=true for permanent)\n\n7\n\nGET\n\n/api/v1/files/:id/download\n\nauthenticate + files:read + validateUUID\n\nDownload file (Content-Disposition: attachment)\n\n8\n\nGET\n\n/api/v1/files/:id/preview\n\nauthenticate + files:read + validateUUID\n\nPreview file (Content-Disposition: inline)\n\n9\n\nPOST\n\n/api/v1/files/:id/versions\n\nauthenticate + files:write + validateUUID\n\nUpload new version of existing file\n\n10\n\nGET\n\n/api/v1/files/:id/versions\n\nauthenticate + files:read + validateUUID\n\nGet version history (recursive CTE)\n\n11\n\nPOST\n\n/api/v1/files/:id/share\n\nauthenticate + files:write + validateUUID\n\nCreate share link (token, expiry, max_downloads)\n\n12\n\nDELETE\n\n/api/v1/files/shares/:shareId\n\nauthenticate + files:write + validateUUID\n\nRevoke share link\n\n13\n\nGET\n\n/api/v1/files/shared/:token\n\nPUBLIC (no auth)\n\nAccess shared file via token\n\n14\n\nGET\n\n/api/v1/files/entity/:entity_type/:entity_id\n\nauthenticate + files:read + validateUUID\n\nGet files by entity (paginated)\n\n15\n\nGET\n\n/api/v1/files/stats\n\nauthenticate + files:read\n\nGet storage statistics (by entity, by module)\n\n16\n\nGET\n\n/api/v1/files/quota\n\nauthenticate + files:read\n\nGet tenant storage quota\n\n17\n\nPUT\n\n/api/v1/files/quota\n\nauthenticate + files:admin\n\nUpdate tenant storage quota (admin only)\n\n\n\nRoute ordering note: /stats, /quota, /entity/:type/:id, /shared/:token are registered BEFORE /:id to prevent Express matching static segments as UUID parameters.\n\n\n\n5. Typical Workflow\n\n5.1 Write Path (File Upload & Management):\n\n1. Upload Single File: Tenant user or module calls POST /api/v1/files/upload with multipart form data: file (required, binary), entity_type (required, e.g., 'documents'), entity_id (required, UUID of parent entity), and optional: description, category, tags (comma-separated string or JSON array), metadata (JSONB). The API validates: file presence (returns VALIDATION_ERROR 'No file uploaded'), entity_type and entity_id presence (returns VALIDATION_ERROR 'entity_type and entity_id are required'), file extension not in blocked list (.exe, .bat, .cmd, .sh, .ps1, .msi, .dll, .com, .scr, .vbs, .js --- returns error 'File type .exe is not allowed'), file size under 100MB (returns FILE_TOO_LARGE 'File size exceeds 100MB limit'), tenant quota not exceeded (returns QUOTA_ERROR for both individual file and total storage).\n\nPlatform generates: stored_name as {uuid}_{sanitized_original_name}.ext, storage path as {tenant_id}/{entity_type}/{YYYY}/{MM}/{stored_name}, SHA-256 checksum via crypto.createHash('sha256').update(buffer).digest('hex'), source_module derived from auth context with priority: req.module.code for combined mode (e.g., 'coc', 'dhf'), 'platform' for JWT-only users, req.user.moduleCode for API key-only modules (e.g., 'aice', 'dhf'), or 'api_key' fallback if API key has no linked module. File is saved to storage via storageProvider.save(relativePath, buffer), then database record is inserted into file_attachments. If DB INSERT fails, file is automatically cleaned up from storage (orphan prevention). Redis cache is invalidated for entity files list. Elasticsearch index is updated via IndexingHooks. Returns full file object with 201 status.\n\n2. Upload Multiple Files: POST /api/v1/files/upload/multiple accepts up to 10 files via multer array ('files' field, max 10). All files are uploaded in parallel via Promise.all, sharing the same entity_type and entity_id. Returns array of results with per-file success/error status. Quota is checked per file individually.\n\n3. Update Metadata: PUT /api/v1/files/:id updates allowed fields: description (text), category (varchar), tags (cast as text[]), metadata (cast as JSONB). File content changes go through versioning (POST /:id/versions), not metadata update. Sets updated_at timestamp. Returns 'No valid fields to update' error if no recognized fields provided. Returns full file object with 200 status.\n\n4. Soft Delete: DELETE /api/v1/files/:id sets deleted_at = NOW() timestamp. PostgreSQL trigger (trg_update_storage_usage) automatically decrements used_storage_bytes and file_count in storage_quotas using GREATEST(..., 0) to prevent negative values. File remains on disk for potential recovery. Redis cache invalidated. Elasticsearch index updated. Returns { success: true, message: 'File deleted' }.\n\n5. Hard Delete: DELETE /api/v1/files/:id?hard=true permanently removes: database record (DELETE FROM file_attachments WHERE id = $1 AND tenant_id = $2), physical file from storage via storageProvider.delete(file_path), thumbnail if exists via storageProvider.delete(thumbnail_path). Returns 404 on subsequent GET. Irreversible. Quota adjusts via DELETE trigger.\n\n\n\n5.2 Versioning:\n\nMinor Versioning: Default behavior. Every POST /api/v1/files/:id/versions with new file (multipart) performs: fetches current record by ID and tenant_id, marks it as is_current_version=false via UPDATE, inserts new record with version = previous_version + 1, is_current_version=true, previous_version_id = old record ID. SHA-256 checksum is calculated for the new file. Both old and new records remain in database --- version history is immutable and never deleted.\n\nVersion History Retrieval: GET /api/v1/files/:id/versions uses a recursive CTE (Common Table Expression) that traverses the previous_version_id chain in both directions (forward via self-join on previous_version_id, backward via matching previous_version_id = id). Returns complete version lineage with: id, version, file_size, checksum, original_name, uploaded_by_email (resolved via LEFT JOIN users), created_at for each version. Ordered by version number ascending.\n\nVersion Chain Structure: file_attachments.previous_version_id creates a self-referencing linked list. Version 1 has previous_version_id=null. Version 2 points to Version 1. Version 3 points to Version 2. Only the latest version has is_current_version=true. Standard queries (list, get by entity) only return current versions (WHERE is_current_version = true AND deleted_at IS NULL).\n\n\n\n5.3 Read/Search Path:\n\nGet by ID: GET /api/v1/files/:id --- Redis cached (300s TTL). Returns full file object with uploaded_by_email resolved via LEFT JOIN users. Filters by tenant_id for isolation.\n\nList Files: GET /api/v1/files?limit=50&offset=0 --- returns a JSON array of file objects. Supports limit and offset query parameters for pagination. Supports filters: source_module, mime_type, entity_type, category, search (ILIKE on original_name), sort_by (created_at|file_size|original_name), sort_order (ASC|DESC), created_after, created_before. Only returns active files (deleted_at IS NULL, is_current_version = true).\n\nGet by Entity: GET /api/v1/files/entity/:entity_type/:entity_id --- returns a JSON array of all files linked to a specific entity. Used by Document Service, CAPA module, Training module, etc. to retrieve their linked files.\n\nStorage Stats: GET /api/v1/files/stats --- returns four sections: quota (max_storage, used_storage, available, usage_percent), files (total, images, pdfs, documents counts), by_entity (count + size per entity_type), and by_module (count + size per source_module e.g., 'platform', 'coc').\n\nStorage Quota: GET /api/v1/files/quota --- returns current quota status: max_storage_bytes, used_storage_bytes, file_count, max_file_size_bytes, allowed_mime_types, blocked_extensions, usage_percentage.\n\n\n\n5.4 File Sharing Path:\n\nCreate Share: POST /api/v1/files/:id/share with body: share_type ('link'|'user'), permissions ('view'|'download'), expires_at (ISO timestamp) or expires_in_hours (number, auto-converted), max_downloads (integer), shared_with_user_id, shared_with_email. Service generates 64-char hex token via crypto.randomBytes(32). Returns share object with share_token, share_url, expires_at, max_downloads. Redis cached (3600s TTL).\n\nAccess Shared File: GET /api/v1/files/shared/:token --- PUBLIC route (no authentication). Service: looks up share by token (Redis cached), validates expiry (expires_at > NOW()), validates download count (current_downloads < max_downloads), increments current_downloads, resolves file path via storageProvider.getFullPath(), serves file with correct Content-Type and Content-Disposition headers. Returns 404 if share not found, expired, or download limit reached.\n\nRevoke Share: DELETE /api/v1/files/shares/:shareId --- sets is_active=false. Invalidates Redis cache for the share token.\n\n\n\n5.5 Retention/Deletion:\n\nPhase 1: No automated retention policy enforcement. Soft-deleted files remain in database with deleted_at timestamp set. Hard delete permanently removes file and database record.\n\nQuota Recovery: Soft delete triggers automatic quota decrement via PostgreSQL trigger. Restoring a soft-deleted file (clearing deleted_at) would trigger quota increment.\n\nPlanned Phase 2: Configurable retention periods per entity_type. Scheduled cleanup job to hard-delete files past retention period. Archived storage tier for long-term retention at reduced cost.\n\n\n\n5.6 Audit Trail:\n\nfile_access_logs records (inserted on download, preview, and any file access): file_id, tenant_id, user_id, action ('download'|'preview'|'access'), ip_address (from req.ip), user_agent (from req.headers), created_at.\n\nStorage quota changes tracked via PostgreSQL trigger --- every INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE on file_attachments automatically adjusts used_storage_bytes and file_count.\n\nShare access tracked via current_downloads counter and last_accessed_at timestamp on file_shares.\n\nAuthentication mode impacts audit attribution: In combined JWT + API Key mode, uploaded_by and file_access_logs.user_id record the real user identity (from JWT), while source_module records the originating module (from API key). This provides GxP-compliant traceability --- auditors can see both who performed the action and which module facilitated it. In API Key-only mode, uploaded_by records the module service account and source_module records the module code. In JWT-only mode, uploaded_by records the platform user and source_module is set to 'platform'.\n\n\n\n6. PostgreSQL DB --- MODEL\n\nThe File Attachment Service uses 4 PostgreSQL tables: file_attachments (master record with binary file metadata, entity linking, versioning, and storage path), storage_quotas (per-tenant storage limits with trigger-maintained usage counters), file_shares (token-based file sharing with expiry and download limits), and file_access_logs (audit trail for file access events). All tables enforce multi-tenant isolation via tenant_id foreign key with ON DELETE CASCADE. The file_attachments table serves as the central entity. A PostgreSQL trigger function (update_storage_usage) automatically maintains used_storage_bytes and file_count in storage_quotas on INSERT, UPDATE (soft-delete/restore), and DELETE operations.\n\n6.1 ER Diagram:\n\n\n\nFigure 6.1: Entity Relationship Diagram --- File Attachment Service\n\nfile_attachments.tenant_id → tenants.id (ON DELETE CASCADE)\n\nfile_attachments.uploaded_by → users.id\n\nfile_attachments.previous_version_id → file_attachments.id (self-referencing version chain)\n\nstorage_quotas.tenant_id → tenants.id (UNIQUE --- one quota per tenant)\n\nfile_shares.tenant_id → tenants.id, file_shares.file_id → file_attachments.id\n\nfile_access_logs.file_id → file_attachments.id, file_access_logs.user_id → users.id\n\n6.2 Entities & Attributes:\n\n6.2.1 Entity: file_attachments (32 columns)\n\n\n\nAttribute\n\nData Type\n\nConstraint / FK\n\nDescription\n\nExample\n\nid\n\nUUID\n\nPK, DEFAULT gen_random_uuid()\n\nUnique file identifier\n\ndaf3c8ad-...\n\ntenant_id\n\nUUID\n\nNOT NULL, FK → tenants(id) ON DELETE CASCADE, INDEX\n\nTenant isolation\n\nc0000000-...\n\noriginal_name\n\nVARCHAR(500)\n\nNOT NULL\n\nUser-uploaded filename\n\ntest-upload.txt\n\nstored_name\n\nVARCHAR(500)\n\nNOT NULL\n\nUUID-based stored name\n\ndaf3c8ad_test-upload.txt\n\nfile_path\n\nVARCHAR(1000)\n\nNOT NULL\n\nRelative storage path\n\nc000.../documents/2026/02/daf3...txt\n\nmime_type\n\nVARCHAR(255)\n\nNOT NULL\n\nMIME type\n\ntext/plain\n\nfile_size\n\nBIGINT\n\nNOT NULL\n\nFile size in bytes\n\n18\n\nchecksum\n\nVARCHAR(128)\n\nNullable\n\nSHA-256 hash\n\na591a6d40...\n\nstorage_provider\n\nVARCHAR(50)\n\nDEFAULT 'local'\n\nStorage backend\n\nlocal\n\nstorage_bucket\n\nVARCHAR(255)\n\nNullable\n\nS3 bucket (Phase 2)\n\nnull\n\nstorage_region\n\nVARCHAR(100)\n\nNullable\n\nS3 region (Phase 2)\n\nnull\n\nentity_type\n\nVARCHAR(100)\n\nNOT NULL, INDEX\n\nParent entity type\n\ndocuments\n\nentity_id\n\nUUID\n\nNOT NULL, INDEX\n\nParent entity ID\n\nUUID\n\ncategory\n\nVARCHAR(100)\n\nNullable\n\nFile category\n\nspecification\n\ndescription\n\nTEXT\n\nNullable\n\nFile description\n\nTest file upload\n\ntags\n\nTEXT[]\n\nNullable\n\nPostgreSQL text array\n\n{sop,quality}\n\nversion\n\nINTEGER\n\nDEFAULT 1\n\nVersion number\n\n1\n\nis_current_version\n\nBOOLEAN\n\nDEFAULT true, INDEX (partial)\n\nActive version flag\n\ntrue\n\nprevious_version_id\n\nUUID\n\nFK → file_attachments(id), Nullable\n\nVersion chain link\n\nUUID or null\n\nis_public\n\nBOOLEAN\n\nDEFAULT false\n\nPublic access flag\n\nfalse\n\naccess_level\n\nVARCHAR(50)\n\nDEFAULT 'private'\n\nAccess level\n\nprivate\n\ndownload_count\n\nINTEGER\n\nDEFAULT 0\n\nTotal downloads\n\n5\n\nhas_thumbnail\n\nBOOLEAN\n\nDEFAULT false\n\nThumbnail exists\n\nfalse\n\nthumbnail_path\n\nVARCHAR(1000)\n\nNullable\n\nThumbnail storage path\n\nnull\n\nmetadata\n\nJSONB\n\nDEFAULT '{}'\n\nExtensible metadata\n\n{}\n\nscan_status\n\nVARCHAR(50)\n\nNullable\n\nVirus scan status (Phase 2)\n\nnull\n\nscanned_at\n\nTIMESTAMPTZ\n\nNullable\n\nScan timestamp (Phase 2)\n\nnull\n\nuploaded_by\n\nUUID\n\nFK → users(id), Nullable\n\nUploader user ID\n\nUUID\n\nsource_module\n\nVARCHAR(100)\n\nDEFAULT 'platform', INDEX\n\nOrigin module\n\nplatform\n\ncreated_at\n\nTIMESTAMPTZ\n\nDEFAULT NOW()\n\nCreated timestamp\n\nISO datetime\n\nupdated_at\n\nTIMESTAMPTZ\n\nDEFAULT NOW()\n\nUpdated timestamp\n\nISO datetime\n\ndeleted_at\n\nTIMESTAMPTZ\n\nNullable, INDEX\n\nSoft delete timestamp\n\nnull\n\n\n\nNOTES: tags is TEXT[] not JSONB. file_size is BIGINT (returns as string from node-pg, parsed to integer in _formatAttachment). is_current_version partial index filters WHERE deleted_at IS NULL AND is_current_version = TRUE. previous_version_id creates a self-referencing linked list for version chains. source_module is auto-derived from auth context, never user-supplied.\n\n\n\n6.2.2 Entity: storage_quotas (10 columns)\n\n\n\nAttribute\n\nData Type\n\nConstraint / FK\n\nDescription\n\nExample\n\nid\n\nUUID\n\nPK, DEFAULT gen_random_uuid()\n\nQuota record ID\n\nUUID\n\ntenant_id\n\nUUID\n\nNOT NULL, FK → tenants(id), UNIQUE\n\nTenant isolation\n\nc0000000-...\n\nmax_storage_bytes\n\nBIGINT\n\nDEFAULT 10737418240 (10GB)\n\nMax total storage\n\n10737418240\n\nused_storage_bytes\n\nBIGINT\n\nDEFAULT 0\n\nCurrent usage (trigger-maintained)\n\n1536\n\nfile_count\n\nINTEGER\n\nDEFAULT 0\n\nActive file count (trigger-maintained)\n\n15\n\nmax_file_size_bytes\n\nBIGINT\n\nDEFAULT 104857600 (100MB)\n\nMax single file size\n\n104857600\n\nallowed_mime_types\n\nTEXT[]\n\nNullable\n\nWhitelist (null = all allowed)\n\nnull\n\nblocked_extensions\n\nTEXT[]\n\nNullable\n\nBlacklist\n\n{.exe,.bat}\n\ncreated_at\n\nTIMESTAMPTZ\n\nDEFAULT NOW()\n\nCreated timestamp\n\nISO datetime\n\nupdated_at\n\nTIMESTAMPTZ\n\nDEFAULT NOW()\n\nUpdated timestamp\n\nISO datetime\n\n\n\nNOTES: max_storage_bytes and used_storage_bytes are BIGINT. PostgreSQL trigger update_storage_usage() maintains used_storage_bytes and file_count automatically on file_attachments INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE. The trigger uses GREATEST(..., 0) to prevent negative values. Quota auto-creates via service when first upload occurs for a tenant.\n\n\n\nPostgreSQL Trigger Function:\n\nCREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION update_storage_usage() --- Handles INSERT (increment), DELETE (decrement), UPDATE with NEW.deleted_at IS NOT NULL AND OLD.deleted_at IS NULL (soft-delete decrement), UPDATE with NEW.deleted_at IS NULL AND OLD.deleted_at IS NOT NULL (restore increment). Uses GREATEST(used_storage_bytes - file_size, 0) and GREATEST(file_count - 1, 0) to prevent negative values.\n\n6.2.3 Entity: file_shares (15 columns)\n\n\n\nAttribute\n\nData Type\n\nConstraint / FK\n\nDescription\n\nExample\n\nid\n\nUUID\n\nPK, DEFAULT gen_random_uuid()\n\nShare record ID\n\nUUID\n\ntenant_id\n\nUUID\n\nNOT NULL, FK → tenants(id) ON DELETE CASCADE\n\nTenant isolation\n\nc0000000-...\n\nfile_id\n\nUUID\n\nNOT NULL, FK → file_attachments(id) ON DELETE CASCADE\n\nShared file\n\nUUID\n\nshare_token\n\nVARCHAR(255)\n\nNOT NULL, UNIQUE\n\n64-char hex token\n\na3f8c2d1...\n\nshare_type\n\nVARCHAR(50)\n\nDEFAULT 'link'\n\nShare type\n\nlink\n\nshared_with_user_id\n\nUUID\n\nFK → users(id), Nullable\n\nTarget user (for 'user' type)\n\nnull\n\nshared_with_email\n\nVARCHAR(255)\n\nNullable\n\nTarget email\n\nnull\n\npermissions\n\nVARCHAR(50)\n\nDEFAULT 'view'\n\nAccess level\n\nview/download\n\nexpires_at\n\nTIMESTAMPTZ\n\nNullable\n\nExpiry timestamp\n\nISO datetime\n\nmax_downloads\n\nINTEGER\n\nNullable\n\nDownload cap (null = unlimited)\n\n5\n\ncurrent_downloads\n\nINTEGER\n\nDEFAULT 0\n\nDownloads consumed\n\n2\n\nis_active\n\nBOOLEAN\n\nDEFAULT true\n\nActive flag (false = revoked)\n\ntrue\n\nlast_accessed_at\n\nTIMESTAMPTZ\n\nNullable\n\nLast access time\n\nISO datetime\n\ncreated_by\n\nUUID\n\nFK → users(id), Nullable\n\nShare creator\n\nUUID\n\ncreated_at\n\nTIMESTAMPTZ\n\nDEFAULT NOW()\n\nCreated timestamp\n\nISO datetime\n\n\n\nNOTES: share_token is UNIQUE. expires_at can be set directly or auto-calculated from expires_in_hours (service converts: new Date(Date.now() + hours * 3600000).toISOString()). max_downloads null means unlimited. current_downloads is incremented atomically on each access. is_active=false means share has been revoked.\n\n\n\n6.2.4 Entity: file_access_logs (8 columns)\n\n\n\nAttribute\n\nData Type\n\nConstraint / FK\n\nDescription\n\nExample\n\nid\n\nUUID\n\nPK, DEFAULT gen_random_uuid()\n\nLog record ID\n\nUUID\n\ntenant_id\n\nUUID\n\nNOT NULL, FK → tenants(id) ON DELETE CASCADE\n\nTenant isolation\n\nUUID\n\nfile_id\n\nUUID\n\nNOT NULL, FK → file_attachments(id) ON DELETE CASCADE\n\nFile accessed\n\nUUID\n\nuser_id\n\nUUID\n\nNOT NULL, FK → users(id)\n\nWho accessed\n\nUUID\n\naction\n\nVARCHAR(50)\n\nNOT NULL\n\nAction type\n\ndownload/preview/access\n\nip_address\n\nINET\n\nNullable\n\nClient IP\n\n192.168.1.1\n\nuser_agent\n\nTEXT\n\nNullable\n\nBrowser UA string\n\nMozilla/5.0...\n\ncreated_at\n\nTIMESTAMPTZ\n\nDEFAULT NOW()\n\nWhen accessed\n\nISO datetime\n\n\n\nNOTES: Records are insert-only (append-only audit log). ip_address and user_agent are captured from Express request object when available. action values include 'download', 'preview', 'access' for share access.\n\n\n\n7. Qdrant (Vector DB)\n\nPhase 1 Status: NOT USED by File Attachment Service.\n\nBinary file content is stored on the filesystem via LocalStorageProvider. File metadata is stored in PostgreSQL (file_attachments table). No vector embeddings of file content are generated in Phase 1.\n\n\n\nPlanned for Phase 2:\n\nCollection Name: file_content_vec_v1\n\nVector Dimension: 1536 (OpenAI text-embedding-3-small) or 768 (sentence-transformers)\n\nPayload Fields: file_id (string), tenant_id (string --- mandatory filter), original_name (string), entity_type (string), entity_id (string), mime_type (string), category (string), source_module (string), created_at (ISO string)\n\nPayload Indexes: tenant_id, entity_type, mime_type, source_module, created_at\n\nUse Case: Semantic search across extracted file content (PDF text, document text), similar file discovery, AI-powered content recommendations, duplicate detection via vector similarity rather than checksum matching.\n\nPrerequisite: Content extraction pipeline (PDF text extraction, OCR for images, document parsing) must be implemented first to generate text from binary files before vector embedding.\n\n\n\n8. Object Storage\n\nFolder Structure Hierarchy:\n\nRoot: UPLOAD_DIR (default: ./uploads/, configurable via environment variable)\n\nPattern: {UPLOAD_DIR}/{tenant_id}/{entity_type}/{YYYY}/{MM}/{uuid}_{sanitized_name}.ext\n\n\n\nExample hierarchy:\n\nuploads/\n\n  c0000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001/   (Tenant: Pharma Corp)\n\n    documents/   (Entity type)\n\n      2026/   (Year)\n\n        02/   (Month)\n\n          daf3c8ad-3660-4c13-8b57-0daf3cc9aaf0_test-upload.txt\n\n          d7cbcb77-a8f1-4e22-b901-3dc77f8e22a1_report-v2.pdf\n\n    capa/\n\n      2026/\n\n        02/\n\n          f1a2b3c4-..._corrective-action-photo.jpg\n\n    training/\n\n      2026/\n\n        01/\n\n          a9b8c7d6-..._sop-training-material.pdf\n\n  d0000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000002/   (Tenant: Another Org)\n\n    documents/\n\n    suppliers/\n\n    temp/   (Temporary storage)\n\n    thumbnails/   (Thumbnail storage)\n\n\n\nStorage Provider Abstraction:\n\nLocalStorageProvider (Phase 1 --- current):\n\nsave(relativePath, buffer) --- Creates directory tree via fs.mkdirSync({recursive: true}), writes buffer via fs.writeFileSync.\n\ndelete(relativePath) --- Removes file via fs.unlinkSync. Silently ignores if file doesn't exist.\n\nexists(relativePath) --- Checks via fs.existsSync.\n\ngetFullPath(relativePath) --- Joins UPLOAD_DIR + relativePath. Controller uses path.resolve() for absolute path.\n\nS3 Migration Path (Phase 2):\n\nImplement S3StorageProvider with identical interface (save, delete, exists, getFullPath). S3 key structure maps 1:1 to local folder hierarchy: s3://{bucket}/{tenant_id}/{entity_type}/{YYYY}/{MM}/{uuid}_{name}.ext. No changes to service or controller code required --- swap provider via configuration.\n\n\n\nSECTION 10 --- REDIS CACHE DETAILS\n\nFile Attachment Service uses 4 Redis cache key patterns with graceful degradation. The service operates normally if Redis is unavailable --- all cache operations are wrapped in try/catch and failures are logged but never block the main operation.\n\n\n\nCache Key Pattern\n\nPurpose\n\nTTL\n\nInvalidation\n\nfile:{tenant_id}:{file_id}\n\nSingle file metadata cache\n\n300s (5 min)\n\nOn update, delete\n\nquota:{tenant_id}\n\nTenant storage quota\n\n60s (1 min)\n\nOn upload, delete, quota update\n\nshare:{share_token}\n\nShare token lookup\n\n3600s (1 hr)\n\nOn revoke\n\nentity_files:{entity_type}:{entity_id}\n\nFiles by entity list\n\n120s (2 min)\n\nOn upload, delete to entity\n\n\n\nRedis Service Integration:\n\nFile: src/infrastructure/cache/redis.service.js --- singleton instance exported as module.exports = new RedisService().\n\nMethods used: get(key, parseJson=true), set(key, value, ttlSeconds), delete(key). Note: The Redis service uses delete() method, NOT del().\n\nCache invalidation method: _invalidateCache(tenantId, fileId, entityType, entityId) --- deletes all relevant cache keys after any write operation (upload, update, delete, share revoke).\n\nLazy initialization: getRedis() function returns singleton Redis instance on first call, returns null if Redis is unavailable. Every cache operation checks for null before proceeding.\n\n\n\nPlatform-level Redis usage that benefits File Attachment Service indirectly:\n\nSession caching: JWT session data cached by AuthService for fast auth validation on every request.\n\nPermission caching: User permissions cached by PermissionService, used by authorization middleware for files:read/files:write/files:admin checks.\n\n\n\nPlanned Phase 2 enhancements:\n\nFile content cache: Large frequently-accessed files cached in Redis for sub-millisecond retrieval.\n\nShare token bloom filter: Probabilistic check before DB lookup for invalid/revoked tokens.\n\nUpload rate limiting: Per-tenant upload rate tracking via Redis INCR + EXPIRE.	<p>SERVICE SPECIFICATION</p><p><strong>File Attachment Services</strong></p><p>QAssure Multi-Tenant SaaS Platform</p><p>Version 1.0   ·   February 2026</p><h1>1. Feature of Service</h1><h3>SERVICE NAME: FILE ATTACHMENT SERVICES</h3><p>Centralised, tenant-isolated, binary file lifecycle engine that governs how files (documents, images, reports, specifications, audit evidence, training materials) are uploaded, stored, versioned, downloaded, previewed, shared, and deleted across multi-tenant regulated environments. All binary file operations across the QAssure platform and module applications are routed through this single service.</p><p><strong>Core capabilities:</strong></p><p><strong>1. File Upload &amp; Storage</strong> --- Single and multi-file upload (up to 10 files per request) with multer memory storage, 100MB max file size enforcement, blocked dangerous extensions (.exe, .bat, .cmd, .sh, .ps1, .msi, .dll, .com, .scr, .vbs, .js), SHA-256 checksum calculation via crypto.createHash('sha256'), and automatic tenant-scoped folder hierarchy: {tenant_id}/{entity_type}/{YYYY}/{MM}/{uuid}_{sanitized_name}.ext. Files are linked to any platform entity via entity_type + entity_id (e.g., entity_type='documents', entity_id=document_uuid). Source module is auto-derived from authentication context: module code for combined JWT + API Key mode (e.g., 'coc', 'dhf') where module identity is captured from the API key even though user identity comes from JWT; 'platform' for JWT-only users; moduleCode for API key-only modules (e.g., 'aice', 'dhf'); or 'api_key' as fallback if an API key has no linked module. Priority order: req.module.code (combined mode) → req.user.moduleCode (API key mode) → 'platform' (JWT mode). Duplicate detection prevents re-upload of identical files to the same entity (enforced by checksum + entity match on active records).</p><p><strong>2. File Versioning</strong> --- Each file supports immutable version history. Uploading a new version via POST /:id/versions marks the previous version as is_current_version=false and creates a new record with incremented version number, linked via previous_version_id. Version history is retrieved via recursive CTE (Common Table Expression) that traverses the chain in both directions, returning complete lineage. Only the current version is returned in standard queries.</p><p><strong>3. Download &amp; Preview</strong> --- Download endpoint (GET /:id/download) serves files with Content-Disposition: attachment header for browser download prompt. Preview endpoint (GET /:id/preview) serves with Content-Disposition: inline for in-browser rendering. Both use storageProvider.getFullPath() with path.resolve() for absolute path resolution. File access is logged in file_access_logs table with user_id, IP address, user agent, and action type.</p><p><strong>4. File Sharing with Token-Based Access</strong> --- Create time-limited, download-limited share links via POST /:id/share. Generates 64-character hex token via crypto.randomBytes(32). Supports configurable parameters: expires_at (absolute timestamp), expires_in_hours (convenience --- auto-converted to expires_at), max_downloads (download cap), share_type ('link' or 'user'), shared_with_user_id, shared_with_email, and permissions ('view' or 'download'). Access via GET /shared/:token is a PUBLIC route (no authentication required). The service validates token existence, checks expiry, enforces max_downloads limit, increments current_downloads counter, and serves the file. Share revocation via DELETE /shares/:shareId deactivates the share.</p><p><strong>5. Storage Quota Management</strong> --- Per-tenant storage quotas tracked in storage_quotas table: max_storage_bytes (default 10GB), used_storage_bytes, file_count, max_file_size_bytes (default 100MB), allowed_mime_types, blocked_extensions. Quota auto-creates on first upload if not present. PostgreSQL trigger (trg_update_storage_usage) automatically increments/decrements used_storage_bytes and file_count on INSERT, DELETE, and UPDATE (soft-delete via SET deleted_at). Quota is checked before every upload: individual file size vs max_file_size_bytes, and total used + new file vs max_storage_bytes. Admin-only PUT /quota endpoint allows updating quota limits (requires files:admin permission).</p><p><strong>6. Soft Delete with Hard Delete Option</strong> --- Default DELETE /:id performs soft delete (sets deleted_at timestamp). PostgreSQL trigger automatically decrements quota on soft delete. Hard delete via DELETE /:id?hard=true permanently removes the database record AND the physical file from storage via storageProvider.delete(). Orphaned file cleanup: if database INSERT fails after file is saved to disk, the file is automatically cleaned up in the catch block.</p><p><strong>7. Storage Provider Abstraction (S3-Ready)</strong> --- All file I/O goes through LocalStorageProvider with 4 methods: save(relativePath, buffer), delete(relativePath), exists(relativePath), getFullPath(relativePath). No controller or service code touches the filesystem directly. Migrating to S3 requires implementing an S3StorageProvider with the same interface --- zero code changes in controllers or service logic.</p><p><strong>8. Three Authentication Modes</strong> --- Every endpoint (except public share access) supports three authentication modes via authenticate middleware: (a) JWT only --- QAssure UI users authenticate via Authorization: Bearer header; tenant derived from JWT claims; user permissions enforced via authorize(); (b) JWT + API Key combined --- module applications acting on behalf of a logged-in user send both Authorization: Bearer (JWT from verify-launch-token) and X-API-Key headers; tenant derived from JWT (X-Tenant-ID not needed); user permissions enforced (not module bypass); source_module set from module identity (e.g., 'coc', 'dhf') for accurate audit attribution; (c) API Key + X-Tenant-ID --- module system/cron operations with no user context send X-API-Key and X-Tenant-ID headers; module gets full access bypass on authorize(). This enables QAssure UI users, module applications acting on behalf of users (with real user identity in audit trails), and module background processes to consume the file attachment service. Routes use authorize() for permission checks: files:read for GET endpoints, files:write for POST/PUT/DELETE endpoints, files:admin for quota management.</p><p><strong>9. Multi-Tenant Data Isolation</strong> --- All queries are tenant-scoped. Every file, share, quota, and access log belongs to a tenant_id. API Key middleware validates module-tenant assignment via the tenant_module_access table (enabled = true) before granting access. Missing X-Tenant-ID returns error code MISSING_TENANT. Unassigned tenant returns MODULE_NOT_ASSIGNED. In combined mode (JWT + API Key), if the JWT's tenant does not match the API key's assigned tenant, error code TENANT_MISMATCH is returned; if the module is deactivated for the tenant, error code MODULE_NOT_ACTIVE is returned. Users cannot see or access files belonging to other tenants under any circumstance.</p><p><strong>10. Security Hardening</strong> --- UUID validation middleware on all :id route parameters returns 400 (INVALID_ID_FORMAT) for malformed UUIDs instead of leaking PostgreSQL errors. Error sanitization function (sanitizeErrorResponse) on all 17 controller catch blocks classifies errors into client-safe categories (QUOTA_ERROR, NOT_FOUND, VALIDATION_ERROR, etc.) and returns generic 'An internal error occurred' for unexpected failures --- never exposing constraint names, table structures, or query details. Blocked file extensions prevent executable uploads. Path traversal is impossible because stored names use UUID-based naming, not user-controlled paths.</p><p><strong>11. Observability</strong> --- Redis caching with graceful degradation (file cache 300s TTL, quota 60s, share 3600s, entity files 120s). Elasticsearch indexing for file searchability. File access logging with IP/user-agent tracking. Storage statistics with per-entity-type and per-module breakdowns. All infrastructure dependencies (Redis, Elasticsearch) fail gracefully --- the service continues operating without caching or indexing if either is unavailable.</p><h1>2. Scope</h1><h2>IN SCOPE (Phase 1):</h2><p><strong>Full file CRUD:</strong> upload single file (with auto-generated tenant-scoped storage path, checksum, source_module derivation), upload multiple files (up to 10, parallel via Promise.all), read (single by ID with Redis cache, paginated list with filtering, by entity with pagination), update metadata (description, tags, category, metadata JSONB), soft delete (with quota trigger), hard delete (with physical file removal).</p><p><strong>File versioning:</strong> upload new version (marks previous as not current, increments version number, links via previous_version_id), retrieve version history (recursive CTE traversal in both directions).</p><p><strong>Download and preview</strong> with correct Content-Disposition headers (attachment for download, inline for preview), file access logging, and path resolution via storageProvider abstraction.</p><p><strong>File sharing:</strong> create share link with configurable expiry (expires_at or expires_in_hours), max_downloads cap, share_type (link/user), permissions (view/download). Public access via token (no auth required). Share revocation. Expired/exhausted shares return 404.</p><p><strong>Storage quota management:</strong> auto-create on first upload, per-tenant limits (max_storage_bytes, max_file_size_bytes, allowed_mime_types, blocked_extensions), quota enforcement on every upload, admin-only quota configuration updates (files:admin permission), PostgreSQL trigger for automatic used_storage_bytes/file_count tracking on INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE.</p><p><strong>Three authentication modes</strong> (JWT only, JWT + API Key combined, API Key + X-Tenant-ID) on all 16 authenticated endpoints via authenticate middleware (1 public route excluded: GET /shared/:token). Combined mode derives tenant from JWT (X-Tenant-ID not needed), enforces user permissions, and sets source_module from module identity for accurate audit attribution.</p><p>Multi-tenant isolation on all read and write operations.</p><p>API Key middleware validates module-tenant access via tenant_module_access table.</p><p>UUID validation middleware on all :id parameters --- returns 400 for invalid UUIDs.</p><p>Error sanitization on all 17 catch blocks --- never leaks database internals.</p><p>File access audit trail via file_access_logs with user_id, IP address, user agent, action.</p><p>Storage statistics with quota summary, file type counts, by_entity and by_module breakdowns.</p><p>Pagination via limit and offset query parameters (default limit 50). Response is a JSON array of file objects.</p><p>Filter support on list: source_module, mime_type, entity_type, category, search (ILIKE on original_name), sort (created_at, file_size, original_name), date range (created_after, created_before).</p><p>Orphaned file cleanup: if DB INSERT fails after file save, file is automatically removed from storage.</p><p>Redis caching with graceful degradation (operates without Redis).</p><p>Elasticsearch indexing with graceful degradation (operates without ES).</p><h2>OUT OF SCOPE (Phase 1 --- File Attachment Service boundary):</h2><p><strong>S3/MinIO object storage migration</strong> --- LocalStorageProvider is production-ready; S3StorageProvider is a config swap planned for Phase 2. Storage provider abstraction is already in place.</p><p><strong>Virus/malware scanning</strong> --- scan_status and scanned_at columns exist in file_attachments table but no scanning service is integrated.</p><p><strong>Image thumbnail generation</strong> --- has_thumbnail, thumbnail_path columns exist but no thumbnail generation pipeline is implemented.</p><p>Full-text content extraction from uploaded files (PDF text extraction, OCR).</p><p>Vector embedding of file content for semantic search via Qdrant.</p><p>Real-time file upload progress (WebSocket-based progress tracking).</p><p>Client-side chunked upload for files larger than 100MB.</p><p>CDN integration for cached file delivery.</p><p>File format conversion (PDF to image, document to PDF, etc.).</p><p>Watermarking or digital stamping of downloaded files.</p><p>Bulk import/export of files.</p><p><strong>File-level access control lists (ACLs)</strong> --- access is controlled at entity level via entity_type permissions, not per-file.</p><p><strong>Retention policy enforcement</strong> --- soft-deleted files remain in database indefinitely until hard-deleted.</p><p><strong>Email/SMS notifications on file events</strong> (handled by Notification Service separately).</p><p><strong>Document lifecycle management</strong> (SOPs, versioning, review/approval) --- handled by separate Document Service (document.service.js).</p><h1>3. Clear Responsibility Split</h1><h2>3.1 SaaS Platform (Owned by Platform Team):</h2><p>Provide and maintain the File Attachment Service as a centralised, tenant-isolated binary file management engine.</p><p>Enforce file upload constraints: 100MB max file size (multer + quota), blocked dangerous extensions, per-tenant storage quotas with auto-creation and trigger-based tracking.</p><p>Generate and manage tenant-scoped storage paths: {tenant_id}/{entity_type}/{YYYY}/{MM}/{uuid}_{sanitized_name}.ext.</p><p>Calculate SHA-256 checksums on every upload for integrity verification.</p><p>Auto-derive source_module from authentication context with priority: req.module.code for combined JWT + API Key mode (e.g., 'coc', 'dhf'), req.user.moduleCode for API key-only modules, 'platform' for JWT-only users, 'api_key' fallback for API keys without linked modules. This ensures files uploaded by modules on behalf of users are correctly attributed to the originating module in storage statistics.</p><p>Manage file versioning: version number increment, previous_version_id linking, is_current_version flagging, recursive CTE version history retrieval.</p><p>Maintain storage provider abstraction (LocalStorageProvider) with save, delete, exists, getFullPath methods for future S3 migration.</p><p>Enforce tenant isolation on all queries via tenant_id filtering. No cross-tenant file access is possible.</p><p>Provide three authentication modes on all 16 authenticated endpoints via authenticate middleware (1 public route excluded: GET /shared/:token): (a) JWT only for QAssure UI users, (b) JWT + API Key combined for module applications acting on behalf of logged-in users (tenant from JWT, user permissions enforced, source_module from module identity), (c) API Key + X-Tenant-ID for module system/cron operations.</p><p>Validate module-tenant access via tenant_module_access table (enabled = true check). Return MISSING_TENANT or MODULE_NOT_ASSIGNED errors for invalid API Key requests. In combined mode, return TENANT_MISMATCH if JWT tenant differs from API key's assigned tenant, and MODULE_NOT_ACTIVE if the module is deactivated for the tenant.</p><p>Provide UUID validation middleware for all route parameters. Return 400 INVALID_ID_FORMAT for malformed UUIDs.</p><p>Sanitise all error responses to prevent database internal leakage.</p><p>Provide file sharing infrastructure: token generation, expiry enforcement, max_downloads enforcement, public access route.</p><p>Provide storage statistics with per-entity-type and per-module breakdowns.</p><p>Log all file access events (download, preview, checkout, checkin) in file_access_logs.</p><p>Provide Redis caching and Elasticsearch indexing with graceful degradation.</p><p>Expose admin-only quota management endpoint (files:admin permission).</p><h2>3.2 Module Applications (Residing on Platform; Owned by App Teams --- LTTS):</h2><p>Use the File Attachment Service API with the appropriate authentication mode:</p><p><strong>For user-context operations (user uploads a file via module UI):</strong> Send both Authorization: Bearer (JWT obtained from verify-launch-token) and X-API-Key headers. X-Tenant-ID is NOT needed --- tenant is derived from JWT. User permissions (files:read, files:write) are enforced. uploaded_by and file_access_logs.user_id record the real user identity. source_module records the module identity (e.g., 'coc').</p><p><strong>For system/cron operations (no user context):</strong> Send X-API-Key + X-Tenant-ID headers. Module gets full access bypass. source_module records the module identity from API key.</p><p>Specify entity_type and entity_id when uploading files to link them to the correct module entity (e.g., entity_type='documents', entity_id=&lt;document_uuid&gt; for Document Service; entity_type='capa', entity_id=&lt;capa_uuid&gt; for CAPA module).</p><p>Provide required fields on upload: file (multipart), entity_type (string), entity_id (UUID). Optional fields: description, category, tags, metadata (JSONB).</p><p>Use the version upload endpoint (POST /:id/versions) for updating files rather than deleting and re-uploading. This preserves version history and audit trail.</p><p>Use the share endpoint to generate time-limited, download-limited links for external stakeholders (e.g., auditors, suppliers) without granting platform login access.</p><p>Do NOT implement custom file storage logic --- always delegate to platform's file attachment service.</p><p>Do NOT store file metadata in the module's own database --- use platform as the system of record for all file attachments. Use the entity link (entity_type + entity_id) to associate files with module records.</p><p>Do NOT construct file paths manually or access the uploads directory directly --- always use service API endpoints for file access.</p><p>Use SSO launch token flow to obtain both tenant context and user session JWT dynamically in production: call POST /modules/verify-launch-token with X-API-Key and the launch token to receive a JWT (24h expiry), user details (including department, designation, user category), tenant details, and permissions. Use this JWT in Authorization: Bearer header alongside X-API-Key for all user-context platform calls. Use .env TENANT_ID + X-Tenant-ID header for development and system/cron operations only.</p><h1>4. API List (Refer to Excel Sheet shared)</h1><table><thead><tr><th><p><strong>#</strong></p></th><th><p><strong>Method</strong></p></th><th><p><strong>Endpoint</strong></p></th><th><p><strong>Auth</strong></p></th><th><p><strong>Description</strong></p></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><p><strong>1</strong></p></td><td><p><strong>POST</strong></p></td><td><p>/api/v1/files/upload</p></td><td><p>authenticate + files:write</p></td><td><p>Upload single file (multipart)</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>2</strong></p></td><td><p><strong>POST</strong></p></td><td><p>/api/v1/files/upload/multiple</p></td><td><p>authenticate + files:write</p></td><td><p>Upload multiple files (up to 10)</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>3</strong></p></td><td><p><strong>GET</strong></p></td><td><p>/api/v1/files</p></td><td><p>authenticate + files:read</p></td><td><p>List files (paginated, filterable)</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>4</strong></p></td><td><p><strong>GET</strong></p></td><td><p>/api/v1/files/:id</p></td><td><p>authenticate + files:read + validateUUID</p></td><td><p>Get single file by ID</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>5</strong></p></td><td><p><strong>PUT</strong></p></td><td><p>/api/v1/files/:id</p></td><td><p>authenticate + files:write + validateUUID</p></td><td><p>Update file metadata</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>6</strong></p></td><td><p><strong>DELETE</strong></p></td><td><p>/api/v1/files/:id</p></td><td><p>authenticate + files:write + validateUUID</p></td><td><p>Delete file (soft; ?hard=true for permanent)</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>7</strong></p></td><td><p><strong>GET</strong></p></td><td><p>/api/v1/files/:id/download</p></td><td><p>authenticate + files:read + validateUUID</p></td><td><p>Download file (Content-Disposition: attachment)</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>8</strong></p></td><td><p><strong>GET</strong></p></td><td><p>/api/v1/files/:id/preview</p></td><td><p>authenticate + files:read + validateUUID</p></td><td><p>Preview file (Content-Disposition: inline)</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>9</strong></p></td><td><p><strong>POST</strong></p></td><td><p>/api/v1/files/:id/versions</p></td><td><p>authenticate + files:write + validateUUID</p></td><td><p>Upload new version of existing file</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>10</strong></p></td><td><p><strong>GET</strong></p></td><td><p>/api/v1/files/:id/versions</p></td><td><p>authenticate + files:read + validateUUID</p></td><td><p>Get version history (recursive CTE)</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>11</strong></p></td><td><p><strong>POST</strong></p></td><td><p>/api/v1/files/:id/share</p></td><td><p>authenticate + files:write + validateUUID</p></td><td><p>Create share link (token, expiry, max_downloads)</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>12</strong></p></td><td><p><strong>DELETE</strong></p></td><td><p>/api/v1/files/shares/:shareId</p></td><td><p>authenticate + files:write + validateUUID</p></td><td><p>Revoke share link</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>13</strong></p></td><td><p><strong>GET</strong></p></td><td><p>/api/v1/files/shared/:token</p></td><td><p>PUBLIC (no auth)</p></td><td><p>Access shared file via token</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>14</strong></p></td><td><p><strong>GET</strong></p></td><td><p>/api/v1/files/entity/:entity_type/:entity_id</p></td><td><p>authenticate + files:read + validateUUID</p></td><td><p>Get files by entity (paginated)</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>15</strong></p></td><td><p><strong>GET</strong></p></td><td><p>/api/v1/files/stats</p></td><td><p>authenticate + files:read</p></td><td><p>Get storage statistics (by entity, by module)</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>16</strong></p></td><td><p><strong>GET</strong></p></td><td><p>/api/v1/files/quota</p></td><td><p>authenticate + files:read</p></td><td><p>Get tenant storage quota</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>17</strong></p></td><td><p><strong>PUT</strong></p></td><td><p>/api/v1/files/quota</p></td><td><p>authenticate + files:admin</p></td><td><p>Update tenant storage quota (admin only)</p></td></tr></tbody></table><p><em>Route ordering note: /stats, /quota, /entity/:type/:id, /shared/:token are registered BEFORE /:id to prevent Express matching static segments as UUID parameters.</em></p><h1>5. Typical Workflow</h1><h2>5.1 Write Path (File Upload &amp; Management):</h2><p><strong>1. Upload Single File:</strong> Tenant user or module calls POST /api/v1/files/upload with multipart form data: file (required, binary), entity_type (required, e.g., 'documents'), entity_id (required, UUID of parent entity), and optional: description, category, tags (comma-separated string or JSON array), metadata (JSONB). The API validates: file presence (returns VALIDATION_ERROR 'No file uploaded'), entity_type and entity_id presence (returns VALIDATION_ERROR 'entity_type and entity_id are required'), file extension not in blocked list (.exe, .bat, .cmd, .sh, .ps1, .msi, .dll, .com, .scr, .vbs, .js --- returns error 'File type .exe is not allowed'), file size under 100MB (returns FILE_TOO_LARGE 'File size exceeds 100MB limit'), tenant quota not exceeded (returns QUOTA_ERROR for both individual file and total storage).</p><p>Platform generates: stored_name as {uuid}_{sanitized_original_name}.ext, storage path as {tenant_id}/{entity_type}/{YYYY}/{MM}/{stored_name}, SHA-256 checksum via crypto.createHash('sha256').update(buffer).digest('hex'), source_module derived from auth context with priority: req.module.code for combined mode (e.g., 'coc', 'dhf'), 'platform' for JWT-only users, req.user.moduleCode for API key-only modules (e.g., 'aice', 'dhf'), or 'api_key' fallback if API key has no linked module. File is saved to storage via storageProvider.save(relativePath, buffer), then database record is inserted into file_attachments. If DB INSERT fails, file is automatically cleaned up from storage (orphan prevention). Redis cache is invalidated for entity files list. Elasticsearch index is updated via IndexingHooks. Returns full file object with 201 status.</p><p><strong>2. Upload Multiple Files:</strong> POST /api/v1/files/upload/multiple accepts up to 10 files via multer array ('files' field, max 10). All files are uploaded in parallel via Promise.all, sharing the same entity_type and entity_id. Returns array of results with per-file success/error status. Quota is checked per file individually.</p><p><strong>3. Update Metadata:</strong> PUT /api/v1/files/:id updates allowed fields: description (text), category (varchar), tags (cast as text[]), metadata (cast as JSONB). File content changes go through versioning (POST /:id/versions), not metadata update. Sets updated_at timestamp. Returns 'No valid fields to update' error if no recognized fields provided. Returns full file object with 200 status.</p><p><strong>4. Soft Delete:</strong> DELETE /api/v1/files/:id sets deleted_at = NOW() timestamp. PostgreSQL trigger (trg_update_storage_usage) automatically decrements used_storage_bytes and file_count in storage_quotas using GREATEST(..., 0) to prevent negative values. File remains on disk for potential recovery. Redis cache invalidated. Elasticsearch index updated. Returns { success: true, message: 'File deleted' }.</p><p><strong>5. Hard Delete:</strong> DELETE /api/v1/files/:id?hard=true permanently removes: database record (DELETE FROM file_attachments WHERE id = $1 AND tenant_id = $2), physical file from storage via storageProvider.delete(file_path), thumbnail if exists via storageProvider.delete(thumbnail_path). Returns 404 on subsequent GET. Irreversible. Quota adjusts via DELETE trigger.</p><h2>5.2 Versioning:</h2><p><strong>Minor Versioning:</strong> Default behavior. Every POST /api/v1/files/:id/versions with new file (multipart) performs: fetches current record by ID and tenant_id, marks it as is_current_version=false via UPDATE, inserts new record with version = previous_version + 1, is_current_version=true, previous_version_id = old record ID. SHA-256 checksum is calculated for the new file. Both old and new records remain in database --- version history is immutable and never deleted.</p><p><strong>Version History Retrieval:</strong> GET /api/v1/files/:id/versions uses a recursive CTE (Common Table Expression) that traverses the previous_version_id chain in both directions (forward via self-join on previous_version_id, backward via matching previous_version_id = id). Returns complete version lineage with: id, version, file_size, checksum, original_name, uploaded_by_email (resolved via LEFT JOIN users), created_at for each version. Ordered by version number ascending.</p><p><strong>Version Chain Structure:</strong> file_attachments.previous_version_id creates a self-referencing linked list. Version 1 has previous_version_id=null. Version 2 points to Version 1. Version 3 points to Version 2. Only the latest version has is_current_version=true. Standard queries (list, get by entity) only return current versions (WHERE is_current_version = true AND deleted_at IS NULL).</p><h2>5.3 Read/Search Path:</h2><p><strong>Get by ID:</strong> GET /api/v1/files/:id --- Redis cached (300s TTL). Returns full file object with uploaded_by_email resolved via LEFT JOIN users. Filters by tenant_id for isolation.</p><p><strong>List Files:</strong> GET /api/v1/files?limit=50&amp;offset=0 --- returns a JSON array of file objects. Supports limit and offset query parameters for pagination. Supports filters: source_module, mime_type, entity_type, category, search (ILIKE on original_name), sort_by (created_at|file_size|original_name), sort_order (ASC|DESC), created_after, created_before. Only returns active files (deleted_at IS NULL, is_current_version = true).</p><p><strong>Get by Entity:</strong> GET /api/v1/files/entity/:entity_type/:entity_id --- returns a JSON array of all files linked to a specific entity. Used by Document Service, CAPA module, Training module, etc. to retrieve their linked files.</p><p><strong>Storage Stats:</strong> GET /api/v1/files/stats --- returns four sections: quota (max_storage, used_storage, available, usage_percent), files (total, images, pdfs, documents counts), by_entity (count + size per entity_type), and by_module (count + size per source_module e.g., 'platform', 'coc').</p><p><strong>Storage Quota:</strong> GET /api/v1/files/quota --- returns current quota status: max_storage_bytes, used_storage_bytes, file_count, max_file_size_bytes, allowed_mime_types, blocked_extensions, usage_percentage.</p><h2>5.4 File Sharing Path:</h2><p><strong>Create Share:</strong> POST /api/v1/files/:id/share with body: share_type ('link'|'user'), permissions ('view'|'download'), expires_at (ISO timestamp) or expires_in_hours (number, auto-converted), max_downloads (integer), shared_with_user_id, shared_with_email. Service generates 64-char hex token via crypto.randomBytes(32). Returns share object with share_token, share_url, expires_at, max_downloads. Redis cached (3600s TTL).</p><p><strong>Access Shared File:</strong> GET /api/v1/files/shared/:token --- PUBLIC route (no authentication). Service: looks up share by token (Redis cached), validates expiry (expires_at &gt; NOW()), validates download count (current_downloads &lt; max_downloads), increments current_downloads, resolves file path via storageProvider.getFullPath(), serves file with correct Content-Type and Content-Disposition headers. Returns 404 if share not found, expired, or download limit reached.</p><p><strong>Revoke Share:</strong> DELETE /api/v1/files/shares/:shareId --- sets is_active=false. Invalidates Redis cache for the share token.</p><h2>5.5 Retention/Deletion:</h2><p>Phase 1: No automated retention policy enforcement. Soft-deleted files remain in database with deleted_at timestamp set. Hard delete permanently removes file and database record.</p><p><strong>Quota Recovery:</strong> Soft delete triggers automatic quota decrement via PostgreSQL trigger. Restoring a soft-deleted file (clearing deleted_at) would trigger quota increment.</p><p><strong>Planned Phase 2:</strong> Configurable retention periods per entity_type. Scheduled cleanup job to hard-delete files past retention period. Archived storage tier for long-term retention at reduced cost.</p><h2>5.6 Audit Trail:</h2><p>file_access_logs records (inserted on download, preview, and any file access): file_id, tenant_id, user_id, action ('download'|'preview'|'access'), ip_address (from req.ip), user_agent (from req.headers), created_at.</p><p>Storage quota changes tracked via PostgreSQL trigger --- every INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE on file_attachments automatically adjusts used_storage_bytes and file_count.</p><p>Share access tracked via current_downloads counter and last_accessed_at timestamp on file_shares.</p><p><strong>Authentication mode impacts audit attribution:</strong> In combined JWT + API Key mode, uploaded_by and file_access_logs.user_id record the real user identity (from JWT), while source_module records the originating module (from API key). This provides GxP-compliant traceability --- auditors can see both who performed the action and which module facilitated it. In API Key-only mode, uploaded_by records the module service account and source_module records the module code. In JWT-only mode, uploaded_by records the platform user and source_module is set to 'platform'.</p><h1>6. PostgreSQL DB --- MODEL</h1><p>The File Attachment Service uses 4 PostgreSQL tables: file_attachments (master record with binary file metadata, entity linking, versioning, and storage path), storage_quotas (per-tenant storage limits with trigger-maintained usage counters), file_shares (token-based file sharing with expiry and download limits), and file_access_logs (audit trail for file access events). All tables enforce multi-tenant isolation via tenant_id foreign key with ON DELETE CASCADE. The file_attachments table serves as the central entity. A PostgreSQL trigger function (update_storage_usage) automatically maintains used_storage_bytes and file_count in storage_quotas on INSERT, UPDATE (soft-delete/restore), and DELETE operations.</p><h2>6.1 ER Diagram:</h2><p><img src="data:image/png;base64," /></p><p><em>Figure 6.1: Entity Relationship Diagram --- File Attachment Service</em></p><p>file_attachments.tenant_id → tenants.id (ON DELETE CASCADE)</p><p>file_attachments.uploaded_by → users.id</p><p>file_attachments.previous_version_id → file_attachments.id (self-referencing version chain)</p><p>storage_quotas.tenant_id → tenants.id (UNIQUE --- one quota per tenant)</p><p>file_shares.tenant_id → tenants.id, file_shares.file_id → file_attachments.id</p><p>file_access_logs.file_id → file_attachments.id, file_access_logs.user_id → users.id</p><h2>6.2 Entities &amp; Attributes:</h2><h3>6.2.1 Entity: file_attachments (32 columns)</h3><table><thead><tr><th><p><strong>Attribute</strong></p></th><th><p><strong>Data Type</strong></p></th><th><p><strong>Constraint / FK</strong></p></th><th><p><strong>Description</strong></p></th><th><p><strong>Example</strong></p></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><p><strong>id</strong></p></td><td><p>UUID</p></td><td><p>PK, DEFAULT gen_random_uuid()</p></td><td><p>Unique file identifier</p></td><td><p>daf3c8ad-...</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>tenant_id</strong></p></td><td><p>UUID</p></td><td><p>NOT NULL, FK → tenants(id) ON DELETE CASCADE, INDEX</p></td><td><p>Tenant isolation</p></td><td><p>c0000000-...</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>original_name</strong></p></td><td><p>VARCHAR(500)</p></td><td><p>NOT NULL</p></td><td><p>User-uploaded filename</p></td><td><p>test-upload.txt</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>stored_name</strong></p></td><td><p>VARCHAR(500)</p></td><td><p>NOT NULL</p></td><td><p>UUID-based stored name</p></td><td><p>daf3c8ad_test-upload.txt</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>file_path</strong></p></td><td><p>VARCHAR(1000)</p></td><td><p>NOT NULL</p></td><td><p>Relative storage path</p></td><td><p>c000.../documents/2026/02/daf3...txt</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>mime_type</strong></p></td><td><p>VARCHAR(255)</p></td><td><p>NOT NULL</p></td><td><p>MIME type</p></td><td><p>text/plain</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>file_size</strong></p></td><td><p>BIGINT</p></td><td><p>NOT NULL</p></td><td><p>File size in bytes</p></td><td><p>18</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>checksum</strong></p></td><td><p>VARCHAR(128)</p></td><td><p>Nullable</p></td><td><p>SHA-256 hash</p></td><td><p>a591a6d40...</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>storage_provider</strong></p></td><td><p>VARCHAR(50)</p></td><td><p>DEFAULT 'local'</p></td><td><p>Storage backend</p></td><td><p>local</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>storage_bucket</strong></p></td><td><p>VARCHAR(255)</p></td><td><p>Nullable</p></td><td><p>S3 bucket (Phase 2)</p></td><td><p>null</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>storage_region</strong></p></td><td><p>VARCHAR(100)</p></td><td><p>Nullable</p></td><td><p>S3 region (Phase 2)</p></td><td><p>null</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>entity_type</strong></p></td><td><p>VARCHAR(100)</p></td><td><p>NOT NULL, INDEX</p></td><td><p>Parent entity type</p></td><td><p>documents</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>entity_id</strong></p></td><td><p>UUID</p></td><td><p>NOT NULL, INDEX</p></td><td><p>Parent entity ID</p></td><td><p>UUID</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>category</strong></p></td><td><p>VARCHAR(100)</p></td><td><p>Nullable</p></td><td><p>File category</p></td><td><p>specification</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>description</strong></p></td><td><p>TEXT</p></td><td><p>Nullable</p></td><td><p>File description</p></td><td><p>Test file upload</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>tags</strong></p></td><td><p>TEXT[]</p></td><td><p>Nullable</p></td><td><p>PostgreSQL text array</p></td><td><p>{sop,quality}</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>version</strong></p></td><td><p>INTEGER</p></td><td><p>DEFAULT 1</p></td><td><p>Version number</p></td><td><p>1</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>is_current_version</strong></p></td><td><p>BOOLEAN</p></td><td><p>DEFAULT true, INDEX (partial)</p></td><td><p>Active version flag</p></td><td><p>true</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>previous_version_id</strong></p></td><td><p>UUID</p></td><td><p>FK → file_attachments(id), Nullable</p></td><td><p>Version chain link</p></td><td><p>UUID or null</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>is_public</strong></p></td><td><p>BOOLEAN</p></td><td><p>DEFAULT false</p></td><td><p>Public access flag</p></td><td><p>false</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>access_level</strong></p></td><td><p>VARCHAR(50)</p></td><td><p>DEFAULT 'private'</p></td><td><p>Access level</p></td><td><p>private</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>download_count</strong></p></td><td><p>INTEGER</p></td><td><p>DEFAULT 0</p></td><td><p>Total downloads</p></td><td><p>5</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>has_thumbnail</strong></p></td><td><p>BOOLEAN</p></td><td><p>DEFAULT false</p></td><td><p>Thumbnail exists</p></td><td><p>false</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>thumbnail_path</strong></p></td><td><p>VARCHAR(1000)</p></td><td><p>Nullable</p></td><td><p>Thumbnail storage path</p></td><td><p>null</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>metadata</strong></p></td><td><p>JSONB</p></td><td><p>DEFAULT '{}'</p></td><td><p>Extensible metadata</p></td><td><p>{}</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>scan_status</strong></p></td><td><p>VARCHAR(50)</p></td><td><p>Nullable</p></td><td><p>Virus scan status (Phase 2)</p></td><td><p>null</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>scanned_at</strong></p></td><td><p>TIMESTAMPTZ</p></td><td><p>Nullable</p></td><td><p>Scan timestamp (Phase 2)</p></td><td><p>null</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>uploaded_by</strong></p></td><td><p>UUID</p></td><td><p>FK → users(id), Nullable</p></td><td><p>Uploader user ID</p></td><td><p>UUID</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>source_module</strong></p></td><td><p>VARCHAR(100)</p></td><td><p>DEFAULT 'platform', INDEX</p></td><td><p>Origin module</p></td><td><p>platform</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>created_at</strong></p></td><td><p>TIMESTAMPTZ</p></td><td><p>DEFAULT NOW()</p></td><td><p>Created timestamp</p></td><td><p>ISO datetime</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>updated_at</strong></p></td><td><p>TIMESTAMPTZ</p></td><td><p>DEFAULT NOW()</p></td><td><p>Updated timestamp</p></td><td><p>ISO datetime</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>deleted_at</strong></p></td><td><p>TIMESTAMPTZ</p></td><td><p>Nullable, INDEX</p></td><td><p>Soft delete timestamp</p></td><td><p>null</p></td></tr></tbody></table><p><em>NOTES: tags is TEXT[] not JSONB. file_size is BIGINT (returns as string from node-pg, parsed to integer in _formatAttachment). is_current_version partial index filters WHERE deleted_at IS NULL AND is_current_version = TRUE. previous_version_id creates a self-referencing linked list for version chains. source_module is auto-derived from auth context, never user-supplied.</em></p><h3>6.2.2 Entity: storage_quotas (10 columns)</h3><table><thead><tr><th><p><strong>Attribute</strong></p></th><th><p><strong>Data Type</strong></p></th><th><p><strong>Constraint / FK</strong></p></th><th><p><strong>Description</strong></p></th><th><p><strong>Example</strong></p></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><p><strong>id</strong></p></td><td><p>UUID</p></td><td><p>PK, DEFAULT gen_random_uuid()</p></td><td><p>Quota record ID</p></td><td><p>UUID</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>tenant_id</strong></p></td><td><p>UUID</p></td><td><p>NOT NULL, FK → tenants(id), UNIQUE</p></td><td><p>Tenant isolation</p></td><td><p>c0000000-...</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>max_storage_bytes</strong></p></td><td><p>BIGINT</p></td><td><p>DEFAULT 10737418240 (10GB)</p></td><td><p>Max total storage</p></td><td><p>10737418240</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>used_storage_bytes</strong></p></td><td><p>BIGINT</p></td><td><p>DEFAULT 0</p></td><td><p>Current usage (trigger-maintained)</p></td><td><p>1536</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>file_count</strong></p></td><td><p>INTEGER</p></td><td><p>DEFAULT 0</p></td><td><p>Active file count (trigger-maintained)</p></td><td><p>15</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>max_file_size_bytes</strong></p></td><td><p>BIGINT</p></td><td><p>DEFAULT 104857600 (100MB)</p></td><td><p>Max single file size</p></td><td><p>104857600</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>allowed_mime_types</strong></p></td><td><p>TEXT[]</p></td><td><p>Nullable</p></td><td><p>Whitelist (null = all allowed)</p></td><td><p>null</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>blocked_extensions</strong></p></td><td><p>TEXT[]</p></td><td><p>Nullable</p></td><td><p>Blacklist</p></td><td><p>{.exe,.bat}</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>created_at</strong></p></td><td><p>TIMESTAMPTZ</p></td><td><p>DEFAULT NOW()</p></td><td><p>Created timestamp</p></td><td><p>ISO datetime</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>updated_at</strong></p></td><td><p>TIMESTAMPTZ</p></td><td><p>DEFAULT NOW()</p></td><td><p>Updated timestamp</p></td><td><p>ISO datetime</p></td></tr></tbody></table><p><em>NOTES: max_storage_bytes and used_storage_bytes are BIGINT. PostgreSQL trigger update_storage_usage() maintains used_storage_bytes and file_count automatically on file_attachments INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE. The trigger uses GREATEST(..., 0) to prevent negative values. Quota auto-creates via service when first upload occurs for a tenant.</em></p><h3>PostgreSQL Trigger Function:</h3><p>CREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION update_storage_usage() --- Handles INSERT (increment), DELETE (decrement), UPDATE with NEW.deleted_at IS NOT NULL AND OLD.deleted_at IS NULL (soft-delete decrement), UPDATE with NEW.deleted_at IS NULL AND OLD.deleted_at IS NOT NULL (restore increment). Uses GREATEST(used_storage_bytes - file_size, 0) and GREATEST(file_count - 1, 0) to prevent negative values.</p><h3>6.2.3 Entity: file_shares (15 columns)</h3><table><thead><tr><th><p><strong>Attribute</strong></p></th><th><p><strong>Data Type</strong></p></th><th><p><strong>Constraint / FK</strong></p></th><th><p><strong>Description</strong></p></th><th><p><strong>Example</strong></p></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><p><strong>id</strong></p></td><td><p>UUID</p></td><td><p>PK, DEFAULT gen_random_uuid()</p></td><td><p>Share record ID</p></td><td><p>UUID</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>tenant_id</strong></p></td><td><p>UUID</p></td><td><p>NOT NULL, FK → tenants(id) ON DELETE CASCADE</p></td><td><p>Tenant isolation</p></td><td><p>c0000000-...</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>file_id</strong></p></td><td><p>UUID</p></td><td><p>NOT NULL, FK → file_attachments(id) ON DELETE CASCADE</p></td><td><p>Shared file</p></td><td><p>UUID</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>share_token</strong></p></td><td><p>VARCHAR(255)</p></td><td><p>NOT NULL, UNIQUE</p></td><td><p>64-char hex token</p></td><td><p>a3f8c2d1...</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>share_type</strong></p></td><td><p>VARCHAR(50)</p></td><td><p>DEFAULT 'link'</p></td><td><p>Share type</p></td><td><p>link</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>shared_with_user_id</strong></p></td><td><p>UUID</p></td><td><p>FK → users(id), Nullable</p></td><td><p>Target user (for 'user' type)</p></td><td><p>null</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>shared_with_email</strong></p></td><td><p>VARCHAR(255)</p></td><td><p>Nullable</p></td><td><p>Target email</p></td><td><p>null</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>permissions</strong></p></td><td><p>VARCHAR(50)</p></td><td><p>DEFAULT 'view'</p></td><td><p>Access level</p></td><td><p>view/download</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>expires_at</strong></p></td><td><p>TIMESTAMPTZ</p></td><td><p>Nullable</p></td><td><p>Expiry timestamp</p></td><td><p>ISO datetime</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>max_downloads</strong></p></td><td><p>INTEGER</p></td><td><p>Nullable</p></td><td><p>Download cap (null = unlimited)</p></td><td><p>5</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>current_downloads</strong></p></td><td><p>INTEGER</p></td><td><p>DEFAULT 0</p></td><td><p>Downloads consumed</p></td><td><p>2</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>is_active</strong></p></td><td><p>BOOLEAN</p></td><td><p>DEFAULT true</p></td><td><p>Active flag (false = revoked)</p></td><td><p>true</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>last_accessed_at</strong></p></td><td><p>TIMESTAMPTZ</p></td><td><p>Nullable</p></td><td><p>Last access time</p></td><td><p>ISO datetime</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>created_by</strong></p></td><td><p>UUID</p></td><td><p>FK → users(id), Nullable</p></td><td><p>Share creator</p></td><td><p>UUID</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>created_at</strong></p></td><td><p>TIMESTAMPTZ</p></td><td><p>DEFAULT NOW()</p></td><td><p>Created timestamp</p></td><td><p>ISO datetime</p></td></tr></tbody></table><p><em>NOTES: share_token is UNIQUE. expires_at can be set directly or auto-calculated from expires_in_hours (service converts: new Date(Date.now() + hours * 3600000).toISOString()). max_downloads null means unlimited. current_downloads is incremented atomically on each access. is_active=false means share has been revoked.</em></p><h3>6.2.4 Entity: file_access_logs (8 columns)</h3><table><thead><tr><th><p><strong>Attribute</strong></p></th><th><p><strong>Data Type</strong></p></th><th><p><strong>Constraint / FK</strong></p></th><th><p><strong>Description</strong></p></th><th><p><strong>Example</strong></p></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><p><strong>id</strong></p></td><td><p>UUID</p></td><td><p>PK, DEFAULT gen_random_uuid()</p></td><td><p>Log record ID</p></td><td><p>UUID</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>tenant_id</strong></p></td><td><p>UUID</p></td><td><p>NOT NULL, FK → tenants(id) ON DELETE CASCADE</p></td><td><p>Tenant isolation</p></td><td><p>UUID</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>file_id</strong></p></td><td><p>UUID</p></td><td><p>NOT NULL, FK → file_attachments(id) ON DELETE CASCADE</p></td><td><p>File accessed</p></td><td><p>UUID</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>user_id</strong></p></td><td><p>UUID</p></td><td><p>NOT NULL, FK → users(id)</p></td><td><p>Who accessed</p></td><td><p>UUID</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>action</strong></p></td><td><p>VARCHAR(50)</p></td><td><p>NOT NULL</p></td><td><p>Action type</p></td><td><p>download/preview/access</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>ip_address</strong></p></td><td><p>INET</p></td><td><p>Nullable</p></td><td><p>Client IP</p></td><td><p>192.168.1.1</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>user_agent</strong></p></td><td><p>TEXT</p></td><td><p>Nullable</p></td><td><p>Browser UA string</p></td><td><p>Mozilla/5.0...</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>created_at</strong></p></td><td><p>TIMESTAMPTZ</p></td><td><p>DEFAULT NOW()</p></td><td><p>When accessed</p></td><td><p>ISO datetime</p></td></tr></tbody></table><p><em>NOTES: Records are insert-only (append-only audit log). ip_address and user_agent are captured from Express request object when available. action values include 'download', 'preview', 'access' for share access.</em></p><h1>7. Qdrant (Vector DB)</h1><p><strong>Phase 1 Status: NOT USED by File Attachment Service.</strong></p><p>Binary file content is stored on the filesystem via LocalStorageProvider. File metadata is stored in PostgreSQL (file_attachments table). No vector embeddings of file content are generated in Phase 1.</p><h2>Planned for Phase 2:</h2><p>Collection Name: file_content_vec_v1</p><p>Vector Dimension: 1536 (OpenAI text-embedding-3-small) or 768 (sentence-transformers)</p><p>Payload Fields: file_id (string), tenant_id (string --- mandatory filter), original_name (string), entity_type (string), entity_id (string), mime_type (string), category (string), source_module (string), created_at (ISO string)</p><p>Payload Indexes: tenant_id, entity_type, mime_type, source_module, created_at</p><p>Use Case: Semantic search across extracted file content (PDF text, document text), similar file discovery, AI-powered content recommendations, duplicate detection via vector similarity rather than checksum matching.</p><p>Prerequisite: Content extraction pipeline (PDF text extraction, OCR for images, document parsing) must be implemented first to generate text from binary files before vector embedding.</p><h1>8. Object Storage</h1><h2>Folder Structure Hierarchy:</h2><p>Root: UPLOAD_DIR (default: ./uploads/, configurable via environment variable)</p><p>Pattern: {UPLOAD_DIR}/{tenant_id}/{entity_type}/{YYYY}/{MM}/{uuid}_{sanitized_name}.ext</p><p><strong>Example hierarchy:</strong></p><p>uploads/</p><p>  c0000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001/   (Tenant: Pharma Corp)</p><p>    documents/   (Entity type)</p><p>      2026/   (Year)</p><p>        02/   (Month)</p><p>          daf3c8ad-3660-4c13-8b57-0daf3cc9aaf0_test-upload.txt</p><p>          d7cbcb77-a8f1-4e22-b901-3dc77f8e22a1_report-v2.pdf</p><p>    capa/</p><p>      2026/</p><p>        02/</p><p>          f1a2b3c4-..._corrective-action-photo.jpg</p><p>    training/</p><p>      2026/</p><p>        01/</p><p>          a9b8c7d6-..._sop-training-material.pdf</p><p>  d0000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000002/   (Tenant: Another Org)</p><p>    documents/</p><p>    suppliers/</p><p>    temp/   (Temporary storage)</p><p>    thumbnails/   (Thumbnail storage)</p><h3>Storage Provider Abstraction:</h3><p>LocalStorageProvider (Phase 1 --- current):</p><p>save(relativePath, buffer) --- Creates directory tree via fs.mkdirSync({recursive: true}), writes buffer via fs.writeFileSync.</p><p>delete(relativePath) --- Removes file via fs.unlinkSync. Silently ignores if file doesn't exist.</p><p>exists(relativePath) --- Checks via fs.existsSync.</p><p>getFullPath(relativePath) --- Joins UPLOAD_DIR + relativePath. Controller uses path.resolve() for absolute path.</p><h3>S3 Migration Path (Phase 2):</h3><p>Implement S3StorageProvider with identical interface (save, delete, exists, getFullPath). S3 key structure maps 1:1 to local folder hierarchy: s3://{bucket}/{tenant_id}/{entity_type}/{YYYY}/{MM}/{uuid}_{name}.ext. No changes to service or controller code required --- swap provider via configuration.</p><h1>SECTION 10 --- REDIS CACHE DETAILS</h1><p>File Attachment Service uses 4 Redis cache key patterns with graceful degradation. The service operates normally if Redis is unavailable --- all cache operations are wrapped in try/catch and failures are logged but never block the main operation.</p><table><thead><tr><th><p><strong>Cache Key Pattern</strong></p></th><th><p><strong>Purpose</strong></p></th><th><p><strong>TTL</strong></p></th><th><p><strong>Invalidation</strong></p></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><p>file:{tenant_id}:{file_id}</p></td><td><p>Single file metadata cache</p></td><td><p>300s (5 min)</p></td><td><p>On update, delete</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>quota:{tenant_id}</p></td><td><p>Tenant storage quota</p></td><td><p>60s (1 min)</p></td><td><p>On upload, delete, quota update</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>share:{share_token}</p></td><td><p>Share token lookup</p></td><td><p>3600s (1 hr)</p></td><td><p>On revoke</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>entity_files:{entity_type}:{entity_id}</p></td><td><p>Files by entity list</p></td><td><p>120s (2 min)</p></td><td><p>On upload, delete to entity</p></td></tr></tbody></table><h3>Redis Service Integration:</h3><p>File: src/infrastructure/cache/redis.service.js --- singleton instance exported as module.exports = new RedisService().</p><p>Methods used: get(key, parseJson=true), set(key, value, ttlSeconds), delete(key). Note: The Redis service uses delete() method, NOT del().</p><p>Cache invalidation method: _invalidateCache(tenantId, fileId, entityType, entityId) --- deletes all relevant cache keys after any write operation (upload, update, delete, share revoke).</p><p>Lazy initialization: getRedis() function returns singleton Redis instance on first call, returns null if Redis is unavailable. Every cache operation checks for null before proceeding.</p><h3>Platform-level Redis usage that benefits File Attachment Service indirectly:</h3><p><strong>Session caching:</strong> JWT session data cached by AuthService for fast auth validation on every request.</p><p><strong>Permission caching:</strong> User permissions cached by PermissionService, used by authorization middleware for files:read/files:write/files:admin checks.</p><h3>Planned Phase 2 enhancements:</h3><p><strong>File content cache:</strong> Large frequently-accessed files cached in Redis for sub-millisecond retrieval.</p><p><strong>Share token bloom filter:</strong> Probabilistic check before DB lookup for invalid/revoked tokens.</p><p><strong>Upload rate limiting:</strong> Per-tenant upload rate tracking via Redis INCR + EXPIRE.</p>	Initial draft	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	4ccf68ad-485c-495a-935b-10d74b173a52	2026-03-27 14:44:30.143306+00	platform	\N
03f7cb03-8046-4829-8b2f-ca0ffe51c768	64845e91-9075-4d0b-a4a6-649daa218471	047c228e-7a07-4437-81da-3f9bc82d8569	0.1	0	1	draft	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	bfghgdgfdfgd\nhgfhgf\nhgfhgf	<p>bfghgdgfdfgd<br>hgfhgf<br>hgfhgf</p>	Initial draft	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	a8e9f655-f14f-4d9c-8bbb-5a0d7cf31a0d	2026-03-30 12:40:52.899315+00	platform	\N
b5555a11-9853-4a71-8427-9ea8832b1136	63450062-d961-43f8-8b9b-87b9894766bc	352d7e21-5155-4ff8-8169-284bf5ece3e7	0.1	0	1	in_review	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	SERVICE SPECIFICATION\n\nFile Attachment Services\n\n\n\nQAssure Multi-Tenant SaaS Platform\n\nVersion 1.0   ·   February 2026\n\n\n\n1. Feature of Service\n\nSERVICE NAME: FILE ATTACHMENT SERVICES\n\nCentralised, tenant-isolated, binary file lifecycle engine that governs how files (documents, images, reports, specifications, audit evidence, training materials) are uploaded, stored, versioned, downloaded, previewed, shared, and deleted across multi-tenant regulated environments. All binary file operations across the QAssure platform and module applications are routed through this single service.\n\n\n\nCore capabilities:\n\n\n\n1. File Upload & Storage --- Single and multi-file upload (up to 10 files per request) with multer memory storage, 100MB max file size enforcement, blocked dangerous extensions (.exe, .bat, .cmd, .sh, .ps1, .msi, .dll, .com, .scr, .vbs, .js), SHA-256 checksum calculation via crypto.createHash('sha256'), and automatic tenant-scoped folder hierarchy: {tenant_id}/{entity_type}/{YYYY}/{MM}/{uuid}_{sanitized_name}.ext. Files are linked to any platform entity via entity_type + entity_id (e.g., entity_type='documents', entity_id=document_uuid). Source module is auto-derived from authentication context: module code for combined JWT + API Key mode (e.g., 'coc', 'dhf') where module identity is captured from the API key even though user identity comes from JWT; 'platform' for JWT-only users; moduleCode for API key-only modules (e.g., 'aice', 'dhf'); or 'api_key' as fallback if an API key has no linked module. Priority order: req.module.code (combined mode) → req.user.moduleCode (API key mode) → 'platform' (JWT mode). Duplicate detection prevents re-upload of identical files to the same entity (enforced by checksum + entity match on active records).\n\n2. File Versioning --- Each file supports immutable version history. Uploading a new version via POST /:id/versions marks the previous version as is_current_version=false and creates a new record with incremented version number, linked via previous_version_id. Version history is retrieved via recursive CTE (Common Table Expression) that traverses the chain in both directions, returning complete lineage. Only the current version is returned in standard queries.\n\n3. Download & Preview --- Download endpoint (GET /:id/download) serves files with Content-Disposition: attachment header for browser download prompt. Preview endpoint (GET /:id/preview) serves with Content-Disposition: inline for in-browser rendering. Both use storageProvider.getFullPath() with path.resolve() for absolute path resolution. File access is logged in file_access_logs table with user_id, IP address, user agent, and action type.\n\n4. File Sharing with Token-Based Access --- Create time-limited, download-limited share links via POST /:id/share. Generates 64-character hex token via crypto.randomBytes(32). Supports configurable parameters: expires_at (absolute timestamp), expires_in_hours (convenience --- auto-converted to expires_at), max_downloads (download cap), share_type ('link' or 'user'), shared_with_user_id, shared_with_email, and permissions ('view' or 'download'). Access via GET /shared/:token is a PUBLIC route (no authentication required). The service validates token existence, checks expiry, enforces max_downloads limit, increments current_downloads counter, and serves the file. Share revocation via DELETE /shares/:shareId deactivates the share.\n\n5. Storage Quota Management --- Per-tenant storage quotas tracked in storage_quotas table: max_storage_bytes (default 10GB), used_storage_bytes, file_count, max_file_size_bytes (default 100MB), allowed_mime_types, blocked_extensions. Quota auto-creates on first upload if not present. PostgreSQL trigger (trg_update_storage_usage) automatically increments/decrements used_storage_bytes and file_count on INSERT, DELETE, and UPDATE (soft-delete via SET deleted_at). Quota is checked before every upload: individual file size vs max_file_size_bytes, and total used + new file vs max_storage_bytes. Admin-only PUT /quota endpoint allows updating quota limits (requires files:admin permission).\n\n6. Soft Delete with Hard Delete Option --- Default DELETE /:id performs soft delete (sets deleted_at timestamp). PostgreSQL trigger automatically decrements quota on soft delete. Hard delete via DELETE /:id?hard=true permanently removes the database record AND the physical file from storage via storageProvider.delete(). Orphaned file cleanup: if database INSERT fails after file is saved to disk, the file is automatically cleaned up in the catch block.\n\n7. Storage Provider Abstraction (S3-Ready) --- All file I/O goes through LocalStorageProvider with 4 methods: save(relativePath, buffer), delete(relativePath), exists(relativePath), getFullPath(relativePath). No controller or service code touches the filesystem directly. Migrating to S3 requires implementing an S3StorageProvider with the same interface --- zero code changes in controllers or service logic.\n\n8. Three Authentication Modes --- Every endpoint (except public share access) supports three authentication modes via authenticate middleware: (a) JWT only --- QAssure UI users authenticate via Authorization: Bearer header; tenant derived from JWT claims; user permissions enforced via authorize(); (b) JWT + API Key combined --- module applications acting on behalf of a logged-in user send both Authorization: Bearer (JWT from verify-launch-token) and X-API-Key headers; tenant derived from JWT (X-Tenant-ID not needed); user permissions enforced (not module bypass); source_module set from module identity (e.g., 'coc', 'dhf') for accurate audit attribution; (c) API Key + X-Tenant-ID --- module system/cron operations with no user context send X-API-Key and X-Tenant-ID headers; module gets full access bypass on authorize(). This enables QAssure UI users, module applications acting on behalf of users (with real user identity in audit trails), and module background processes to consume the file attachment service. Routes use authorize() for permission checks: files:read for GET endpoints, files:write for POST/PUT/DELETE endpoints, files:admin for quota management.\n\n9. Multi-Tenant Data Isolation --- All queries are tenant-scoped. Every file, share, quota, and access log belongs to a tenant_id. API Key middleware validates module-tenant assignment via the tenant_module_access table (enabled = true) before granting access. Missing X-Tenant-ID returns error code MISSING_TENANT. Unassigned tenant returns MODULE_NOT_ASSIGNED. In combined mode (JWT + API Key), if the JWT's tenant does not match the API key's assigned tenant, error code TENANT_MISMATCH is returned; if the module is deactivated for the tenant, error code MODULE_NOT_ACTIVE is returned. Users cannot see or access files belonging to other tenants under any circumstance.\n\n10. Security Hardening --- UUID validation middleware on all :id route parameters returns 400 (INVALID_ID_FORMAT) for malformed UUIDs instead of leaking PostgreSQL errors. Error sanitization function (sanitizeErrorResponse) on all 17 controller catch blocks classifies errors into client-safe categories (QUOTA_ERROR, NOT_FOUND, VALIDATION_ERROR, etc.) and returns generic 'An internal error occurred' for unexpected failures --- never exposing constraint names, table structures, or query details. Blocked file extensions prevent executable uploads. Path traversal is impossible because stored names use UUID-based naming, not user-controlled paths.\n\n11. Observability --- Redis caching with graceful degradation (file cache 300s TTL, quota 60s, share 3600s, entity files 120s). Elasticsearch indexing for file searchability. File access logging with IP/user-agent tracking. Storage statistics with per-entity-type and per-module breakdowns. All infrastructure dependencies (Redis, Elasticsearch) fail gracefully --- the service continues operating without caching or indexing if either is unavailable.\n\n\n\n2. Scope\n\nIN SCOPE (Phase 1):\n\nFull file CRUD: upload single file (with auto-generated tenant-scoped storage path, checksum, source_module derivation), upload multiple files (up to 10, parallel via Promise.all), read (single by ID with Redis cache, paginated list with filtering, by entity with pagination), update metadata (description, tags, category, metadata JSONB), soft delete (with quota trigger), hard delete (with physical file removal).\n\nFile versioning: upload new version (marks previous as not current, increments version number, links via previous_version_id), retrieve version history (recursive CTE traversal in both directions).\n\nDownload and preview with correct Content-Disposition headers (attachment for download, inline for preview), file access logging, and path resolution via storageProvider abstraction.\n\nFile sharing: create share link with configurable expiry (expires_at or expires_in_hours), max_downloads cap, share_type (link/user), permissions (view/download). Public access via token (no auth required). Share revocation. Expired/exhausted shares return 404.\n\nStorage quota management: auto-create on first upload, per-tenant limits (max_storage_bytes, max_file_size_bytes, allowed_mime_types, blocked_extensions), quota enforcement on every upload, admin-only quota configuration updates (files:admin permission), PostgreSQL trigger for automatic used_storage_bytes/file_count tracking on INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE.\n\nThree authentication modes (JWT only, JWT + API Key combined, API Key + X-Tenant-ID) on all 16 authenticated endpoints via authenticate middleware (1 public route excluded: GET /shared/:token). Combined mode derives tenant from JWT (X-Tenant-ID not needed), enforces user permissions, and sets source_module from module identity for accurate audit attribution.\n\nMulti-tenant isolation on all read and write operations.\n\nAPI Key middleware validates module-tenant access via tenant_module_access table.\n\nUUID validation middleware on all :id parameters --- returns 400 for invalid UUIDs.\n\nError sanitization on all 17 catch blocks --- never leaks database internals.\n\nFile access audit trail via file_access_logs with user_id, IP address, user agent, action.\n\nStorage statistics with quota summary, file type counts, by_entity and by_module breakdowns.\n\nPagination via limit and offset query parameters (default limit 50). Response is a JSON array of file objects.\n\nFilter support on list: source_module, mime_type, entity_type, category, search (ILIKE on original_name), sort (created_at, file_size, original_name), date range (created_after, created_before).\n\nOrphaned file cleanup: if DB INSERT fails after file save, file is automatically removed from storage.\n\nRedis caching with graceful degradation (operates without Redis).\n\nElasticsearch indexing with graceful degradation (operates without ES).\n\n\n\nOUT OF SCOPE (Phase 1 --- File Attachment Service boundary):\n\nS3/MinIO object storage migration --- LocalStorageProvider is production-ready; S3StorageProvider is a config swap planned for Phase 2. Storage provider abstraction is already in place.\n\nVirus/malware scanning --- scan_status and scanned_at columns exist in file_attachments table but no scanning service is integrated.\n\nImage thumbnail generation --- has_thumbnail, thumbnail_path columns exist but no thumbnail generation pipeline is implemented.\n\nFull-text content extraction from uploaded files (PDF text extraction, OCR).\n\nVector embedding of file content for semantic search via Qdrant.\n\nReal-time file upload progress (WebSocket-based progress tracking).\n\nClient-side chunked upload for files larger than 100MB.\n\nCDN integration for cached file delivery.\n\nFile format conversion (PDF to image, document to PDF, etc.).\n\nWatermarking or digital stamping of downloaded files.\n\nBulk import/export of files.\n\nFile-level access control lists (ACLs) --- access is controlled at entity level via entity_type permissions, not per-file.\n\nRetention policy enforcement --- soft-deleted files remain in database indefinitely until hard-deleted.\n\nEmail/SMS notifications on file events (handled by Notification Service separately).\n\nDocument lifecycle management (SOPs, versioning, review/approval) --- handled by separate Document Service (document.service.js).\n\n\n\n3. Clear Responsibility Split\n\n3.1 SaaS Platform (Owned by Platform Team):\n\nProvide and maintain the File Attachment Service as a centralised, tenant-isolated binary file management engine.\n\nEnforce file upload constraints: 100MB max file size (multer + quota), blocked dangerous extensions, per-tenant storage quotas with auto-creation and trigger-based tracking.\n\nGenerate and manage tenant-scoped storage paths: {tenant_id}/{entity_type}/{YYYY}/{MM}/{uuid}_{sanitized_name}.ext.\n\nCalculate SHA-256 checksums on every upload for integrity verification.\n\nAuto-derive source_module from authentication context with priority: req.module.code for combined JWT + API Key mode (e.g., 'coc', 'dhf'), req.user.moduleCode for API key-only modules, 'platform' for JWT-only users, 'api_key' fallback for API keys without linked modules. This ensures files uploaded by modules on behalf of users are correctly attributed to the originating module in storage statistics.\n\nManage file versioning: version number increment, previous_version_id linking, is_current_version flagging, recursive CTE version history retrieval.\n\nMaintain storage provider abstraction (LocalStorageProvider) with save, delete, exists, getFullPath methods for future S3 migration.\n\nEnforce tenant isolation on all queries via tenant_id filtering. No cross-tenant file access is possible.\n\nProvide three authentication modes on all 16 authenticated endpoints via authenticate middleware (1 public route excluded: GET /shared/:token): (a) JWT only for QAssure UI users, (b) JWT + API Key combined for module applications acting on behalf of logged-in users (tenant from JWT, user permissions enforced, source_module from module identity), (c) API Key + X-Tenant-ID for module system/cron operations.\n\nValidate module-tenant access via tenant_module_access table (enabled = true check). Return MISSING_TENANT or MODULE_NOT_ASSIGNED errors for invalid API Key requests. In combined mode, return TENANT_MISMATCH if JWT tenant differs from API key's assigned tenant, and MODULE_NOT_ACTIVE if the module is deactivated for the tenant.\n\nProvide UUID validation middleware for all route parameters. Return 400 INVALID_ID_FORMAT for malformed UUIDs.\n\nSanitise all error responses to prevent database internal leakage.\n\nProvide file sharing infrastructure: token generation, expiry enforcement, max_downloads enforcement, public access route.\n\nProvide storage statistics with per-entity-type and per-module breakdowns.\n\nLog all file access events (download, preview, checkout, checkin) in file_access_logs.\n\nProvide Redis caching and Elasticsearch indexing with graceful degradation.\n\nExpose admin-only quota management endpoint (files:admin permission).\n\n\n\n3.2 Module Applications (Residing on Platform; Owned by App Teams --- LTTS):\n\nUse the File Attachment Service API with the appropriate authentication mode:\n\nFor user-context operations (user uploads a file via module UI): Send both Authorization: Bearer (JWT obtained from verify-launch-token) and X-API-Key headers. X-Tenant-ID is NOT needed --- tenant is derived from JWT. User permissions (files:read, files:write) are enforced. uploaded_by and file_access_logs.user_id record the real user identity. source_module records the module identity (e.g., 'coc').\n\nFor system/cron operations (no user context): Send X-API-Key + X-Tenant-ID headers. Module gets full access bypass. source_module records the module identity from API key.\n\nSpecify entity_type and entity_id when uploading files to link them to the correct module entity (e.g., entity_type='documents', entity_id=<document_uuid> for Document Service; entity_type='capa', entity_id=<capa_uuid> for CAPA module).\n\nProvide required fields on upload: file (multipart), entity_type (string), entity_id (UUID). Optional fields: description, category, tags, metadata (JSONB).\n\nUse the version upload endpoint (POST /:id/versions) for updating files rather than deleting and re-uploading. This preserves version history and audit trail.\n\nUse the share endpoint to generate time-limited, download-limited links for external stakeholders (e.g., auditors, suppliers) without granting platform login access.\n\nDo NOT implement custom file storage logic --- always delegate to platform's file attachment service.\n\nDo NOT store file metadata in the module's own database --- use platform as the system of record for all file attachments. Use the entity link (entity_type + entity_id) to associate files with module records.\n\nDo NOT construct file paths manually or access the uploads directory directly --- always use service API endpoints for file access.\n\nUse SSO launch token flow to obtain both tenant context and user session JWT dynamically in production: call POST /modules/verify-launch-token with X-API-Key and the launch token to receive a JWT (24h expiry), user details (including department, designation, user category), tenant details, and permissions. Use this JWT in Authorization: Bearer header alongside X-API-Key for all user-context platform calls. Use .env TENANT_ID + X-Tenant-ID header for development and system/cron operations only.\n\n\n\n4. API List (Refer to Excel Sheet shared)\n\n\n\n#\n\nMethod\n\nEndpoint\n\nAuth\n\nDescription\n\n1\n\nPOST\n\n/api/v1/files/upload\n\nauthenticate + files:write\n\nUpload single file (multipart)\n\n2\n\nPOST\n\n/api/v1/files/upload/multiple\n\nauthenticate + files:write\n\nUpload multiple files (up to 10)\n\n3\n\nGET\n\n/api/v1/files\n\nauthenticate + files:read\n\nList files (paginated, filterable)\n\n4\n\nGET\n\n/api/v1/files/:id\n\nauthenticate + files:read + validateUUID\n\nGet single file by ID\n\n5\n\nPUT\n\n/api/v1/files/:id\n\nauthenticate + files:write + validateUUID\n\nUpdate file metadata\n\n6\n\nDELETE\n\n/api/v1/files/:id\n\nauthenticate + files:write + validateUUID\n\nDelete file (soft; ?hard=true for permanent)\n\n7\n\nGET\n\n/api/v1/files/:id/download\n\nauthenticate + files:read + validateUUID\n\nDownload file (Content-Disposition: attachment)\n\n8\n\nGET\n\n/api/v1/files/:id/preview\n\nauthenticate + files:read + validateUUID\n\nPreview file (Content-Disposition: inline)\n\n9\n\nPOST\n\n/api/v1/files/:id/versions\n\nauthenticate + files:write + validateUUID\n\nUpload new version of existing file\n\n10\n\nGET\n\n/api/v1/files/:id/versions\n\nauthenticate + files:read + validateUUID\n\nGet version history (recursive CTE)\n\n11\n\nPOST\n\n/api/v1/files/:id/share\n\nauthenticate + files:write + validateUUID\n\nCreate share link (token, expiry, max_downloads)\n\n12\n\nDELETE\n\n/api/v1/files/shares/:shareId\n\nauthenticate + files:write + validateUUID\n\nRevoke share link\n\n13\n\nGET\n\n/api/v1/files/shared/:token\n\nPUBLIC (no auth)\n\nAccess shared file via token\n\n14\n\nGET\n\n/api/v1/files/entity/:entity_type/:entity_id\n\nauthenticate + files:read + validateUUID\n\nGet files by entity (paginated)\n\n15\n\nGET\n\n/api/v1/files/stats\n\nauthenticate + files:read\n\nGet storage statistics (by entity, by module)\n\n16\n\nGET\n\n/api/v1/files/quota\n\nauthenticate + files:read\n\nGet tenant storage quota\n\n17\n\nPUT\n\n/api/v1/files/quota\n\nauthenticate + files:admin\n\nUpdate tenant storage quota (admin only)\n\n\n\nRoute ordering note: /stats, /quota, /entity/:type/:id, /shared/:token are registered BEFORE /:id to prevent Express matching static segments as UUID parameters.\n\n\n\n5. Typical Workflow\n\n5.1 Write Path (File Upload & Management):\n\n1. Upload Single File: Tenant user or module calls POST /api/v1/files/upload with multipart form data: file (required, binary), entity_type (required, e.g., 'documents'), entity_id (required, UUID of parent entity), and optional: description, category, tags (comma-separated string or JSON array), metadata (JSONB). The API validates: file presence (returns VALIDATION_ERROR 'No file uploaded'), entity_type and entity_id presence (returns VALIDATION_ERROR 'entity_type and entity_id are required'), file extension not in blocked list (.exe, .bat, .cmd, .sh, .ps1, .msi, .dll, .com, .scr, .vbs, .js --- returns error 'File type .exe is not allowed'), file size under 100MB (returns FILE_TOO_LARGE 'File size exceeds 100MB limit'), tenant quota not exceeded (returns QUOTA_ERROR for both individual file and total storage).\n\nPlatform generates: stored_name as {uuid}_{sanitized_original_name}.ext, storage path as {tenant_id}/{entity_type}/{YYYY}/{MM}/{stored_name}, SHA-256 checksum via crypto.createHash('sha256').update(buffer).digest('hex'), source_module derived from auth context with priority: req.module.code for combined mode (e.g., 'coc', 'dhf'), 'platform' for JWT-only users, req.user.moduleCode for API key-only modules (e.g., 'aice', 'dhf'), or 'api_key' fallback if API key has no linked module. File is saved to storage via storageProvider.save(relativePath, buffer), then database record is inserted into file_attachments. If DB INSERT fails, file is automatically cleaned up from storage (orphan prevention). Redis cache is invalidated for entity files list. Elasticsearch index is updated via IndexingHooks. Returns full file object with 201 status.\n\n2. Upload Multiple Files: POST /api/v1/files/upload/multiple accepts up to 10 files via multer array ('files' field, max 10). All files are uploaded in parallel via Promise.all, sharing the same entity_type and entity_id. Returns array of results with per-file success/error status. Quota is checked per file individually.\n\n3. Update Metadata: PUT /api/v1/files/:id updates allowed fields: description (text), category (varchar), tags (cast as text[]), metadata (cast as JSONB). File content changes go through versioning (POST /:id/versions), not metadata update. Sets updated_at timestamp. Returns 'No valid fields to update' error if no recognized fields provided. Returns full file object with 200 status.\n\n4. Soft Delete: DELETE /api/v1/files/:id sets deleted_at = NOW() timestamp. PostgreSQL trigger (trg_update_storage_usage) automatically decrements used_storage_bytes and file_count in storage_quotas using GREATEST(..., 0) to prevent negative values. File remains on disk for potential recovery. Redis cache invalidated. Elasticsearch index updated. Returns { success: true, message: 'File deleted' }.\n\n5. Hard Delete: DELETE /api/v1/files/:id?hard=true permanently removes: database record (DELETE FROM file_attachments WHERE id = $1 AND tenant_id = $2), physical file from storage via storageProvider.delete(file_path), thumbnail if exists via storageProvider.delete(thumbnail_path). Returns 404 on subsequent GET. Irreversible. Quota adjusts via DELETE trigger.\n\n\n\n5.2 Versioning:\n\nMinor Versioning: Default behavior. Every POST /api/v1/files/:id/versions with new file (multipart) performs: fetches current record by ID and tenant_id, marks it as is_current_version=false via UPDATE, inserts new record with version = previous_version + 1, is_current_version=true, previous_version_id = old record ID. SHA-256 checksum is calculated for the new file. Both old and new records remain in database --- version history is immutable and never deleted.\n\nVersion History Retrieval: GET /api/v1/files/:id/versions uses a recursive CTE (Common Table Expression) that traverses the previous_version_id chain in both directions (forward via self-join on previous_version_id, backward via matching previous_version_id = id). Returns complete version lineage with: id, version, file_size, checksum, original_name, uploaded_by_email (resolved via LEFT JOIN users), created_at for each version. Ordered by version number ascending.\n\nVersion Chain Structure: file_attachments.previous_version_id creates a self-referencing linked list. Version 1 has previous_version_id=null. Version 2 points to Version 1. Version 3 points to Version 2. Only the latest version has is_current_version=true. Standard queries (list, get by entity) only return current versions (WHERE is_current_version = true AND deleted_at IS NULL).\n\n\n\n5.3 Read/Search Path:\n\nGet by ID: GET /api/v1/files/:id --- Redis cached (300s TTL). Returns full file object with uploaded_by_email resolved via LEFT JOIN users. Filters by tenant_id for isolation.\n\nList Files: GET /api/v1/files?limit=50&offset=0 --- returns a JSON array of file objects. Supports limit and offset query parameters for pagination. Supports filters: source_module, mime_type, entity_type, category, search (ILIKE on original_name), sort_by (created_at|file_size|original_name), sort_order (ASC|DESC), created_after, created_before. Only returns active files (deleted_at IS NULL, is_current_version = true).\n\nGet by Entity: GET /api/v1/files/entity/:entity_type/:entity_id --- returns a JSON array of all files linked to a specific entity. Used by Document Service, CAPA module, Training module, etc. to retrieve their linked files.\n\nStorage Stats: GET /api/v1/files/stats --- returns four sections: quota (max_storage, used_storage, available, usage_percent), files (total, images, pdfs, documents counts), by_entity (count + size per entity_type), and by_module (count + size per source_module e.g., 'platform', 'coc').\n\nStorage Quota: GET /api/v1/files/quota --- returns current quota status: max_storage_bytes, used_storage_bytes, file_count, max_file_size_bytes, allowed_mime_types, blocked_extensions, usage_percentage.\n\n\n\n5.4 File Sharing Path:\n\nCreate Share: POST /api/v1/files/:id/share with body: share_type ('link'|'user'), permissions ('view'|'download'), expires_at (ISO timestamp) or expires_in_hours (number, auto-converted), max_downloads (integer), shared_with_user_id, shared_with_email. Service generates 64-char hex token via crypto.randomBytes(32). Returns share object with share_token, share_url, expires_at, max_downloads. Redis cached (3600s TTL).\n\nAccess Shared File: GET /api/v1/files/shared/:token --- PUBLIC route (no authentication). Service: looks up share by token (Redis cached), validates expiry (expires_at > NOW()), validates download count (current_downloads < max_downloads), increments current_downloads, resolves file path via storageProvider.getFullPath(), serves file with correct Content-Type and Content-Disposition headers. Returns 404 if share not found, expired, or download limit reached.\n\nRevoke Share: DELETE /api/v1/files/shares/:shareId --- sets is_active=false. Invalidates Redis cache for the share token.\n\n\n\n5.5 Retention/Deletion:\n\nPhase 1: No automated retention policy enforcement. Soft-deleted files remain in database with deleted_at timestamp set. Hard delete permanently removes file and database record.\n\nQuota Recovery: Soft delete triggers automatic quota decrement via PostgreSQL trigger. Restoring a soft-deleted file (clearing deleted_at) would trigger quota increment.\n\nPlanned Phase 2: Configurable retention periods per entity_type. Scheduled cleanup job to hard-delete files past retention period. Archived storage tier for long-term retention at reduced cost.\n\n\n\n5.6 Audit Trail:\n\nfile_access_logs records (inserted on download, preview, and any file access): file_id, tenant_id, user_id, action ('download'|'preview'|'access'), ip_address (from req.ip), user_agent (from req.headers), created_at.\n\nStorage quota changes tracked via PostgreSQL trigger --- every INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE on file_attachments automatically adjusts used_storage_bytes and file_count.\n\nShare access tracked via current_downloads counter and last_accessed_at timestamp on file_shares.\n\nAuthentication mode impacts audit attribution: In combined JWT + API Key mode, uploaded_by and file_access_logs.user_id record the real user identity (from JWT), while source_module records the originating module (from API key). This provides GxP-compliant traceability --- auditors can see both who performed the action and which module facilitated it. In API Key-only mode, uploaded_by records the module service account and source_module records the module code. In JWT-only mode, uploaded_by records the platform user and source_module is set to 'platform'.\n\n\n\n6. PostgreSQL DB --- MODEL\n\nThe File Attachment Service uses 4 PostgreSQL tables: file_attachments (master record with binary file metadata, entity linking, versioning, and storage path), storage_quotas (per-tenant storage limits with trigger-maintained usage counters), file_shares (token-based file sharing with expiry and download limits), and file_access_logs (audit trail for file access events). All tables enforce multi-tenant isolation via tenant_id foreign key with ON DELETE CASCADE. The file_attachments table serves as the central entity. A PostgreSQL trigger function (update_storage_usage) automatically maintains used_storage_bytes and file_count in storage_quotas on INSERT, UPDATE (soft-delete/restore), and DELETE operations.\n\n6.1 ER Diagram:\n\n\n\nFigure 6.1: Entity Relationship Diagram --- File Attachment Service\n\nfile_attachments.tenant_id → tenants.id (ON DELETE CASCADE)\n\nfile_attachments.uploaded_by → users.id\n\nfile_attachments.previous_version_id → file_attachments.id (self-referencing version chain)\n\nstorage_quotas.tenant_id → tenants.id (UNIQUE --- one quota per tenant)\n\nfile_shares.tenant_id → tenants.id, file_shares.file_id → file_attachments.id\n\nfile_access_logs.file_id → file_attachments.id, file_access_logs.user_id → users.id\n\n6.2 Entities & Attributes:\n\n6.2.1 Entity: file_attachments (32 columns)\n\n\n\nAttribute\n\nData Type\n\nConstraint / FK\n\nDescription\n\nExample\n\nid\n\nUUID\n\nPK, DEFAULT gen_random_uuid()\n\nUnique file identifier\n\ndaf3c8ad-...\n\ntenant_id\n\nUUID\n\nNOT NULL, FK → tenants(id) ON DELETE CASCADE, INDEX\n\nTenant isolation\n\nc0000000-...\n\noriginal_name\n\nVARCHAR(500)\n\nNOT NULL\n\nUser-uploaded filename\n\ntest-upload.txt\n\nstored_name\n\nVARCHAR(500)\n\nNOT NULL\n\nUUID-based stored name\n\ndaf3c8ad_test-upload.txt\n\nfile_path\n\nVARCHAR(1000)\n\nNOT NULL\n\nRelative storage path\n\nc000.../documents/2026/02/daf3...txt\n\nmime_type\n\nVARCHAR(255)\n\nNOT NULL\n\nMIME type\n\ntext/plain\n\nfile_size\n\nBIGINT\n\nNOT NULL\n\nFile size in bytes\n\n18\n\nchecksum\n\nVARCHAR(128)\n\nNullable\n\nSHA-256 hash\n\na591a6d40...\n\nstorage_provider\n\nVARCHAR(50)\n\nDEFAULT 'local'\n\nStorage backend\n\nlocal\n\nstorage_bucket\n\nVARCHAR(255)\n\nNullable\n\nS3 bucket (Phase 2)\n\nnull\n\nstorage_region\n\nVARCHAR(100)\n\nNullable\n\nS3 region (Phase 2)\n\nnull\n\nentity_type\n\nVARCHAR(100)\n\nNOT NULL, INDEX\n\nParent entity type\n\ndocuments\n\nentity_id\n\nUUID\n\nNOT NULL, INDEX\n\nParent entity ID\n\nUUID\n\ncategory\n\nVARCHAR(100)\n\nNullable\n\nFile category\n\nspecification\n\ndescription\n\nTEXT\n\nNullable\n\nFile description\n\nTest file upload\n\ntags\n\nTEXT[]\n\nNullable\n\nPostgreSQL text array\n\n{sop,quality}\n\nversion\n\nINTEGER\n\nDEFAULT 1\n\nVersion number\n\n1\n\nis_current_version\n\nBOOLEAN\n\nDEFAULT true, INDEX (partial)\n\nActive version flag\n\ntrue\n\nprevious_version_id\n\nUUID\n\nFK → file_attachments(id), Nullable\n\nVersion chain link\n\nUUID or null\n\nis_public\n\nBOOLEAN\n\nDEFAULT false\n\nPublic access flag\n\nfalse\n\naccess_level\n\nVARCHAR(50)\n\nDEFAULT 'private'\n\nAccess level\n\nprivate\n\ndownload_count\n\nINTEGER\n\nDEFAULT 0\n\nTotal downloads\n\n5\n\nhas_thumbnail\n\nBOOLEAN\n\nDEFAULT false\n\nThumbnail exists\n\nfalse\n\nthumbnail_path\n\nVARCHAR(1000)\n\nNullable\n\nThumbnail storage path\n\nnull\n\nmetadata\n\nJSONB\n\nDEFAULT '{}'\n\nExtensible metadata\n\n{}\n\nscan_status\n\nVARCHAR(50)\n\nNullable\n\nVirus scan status (Phase 2)\n\nnull\n\nscanned_at\n\nTIMESTAMPTZ\n\nNullable\n\nScan timestamp (Phase 2)\n\nnull\n\nuploaded_by\n\nUUID\n\nFK → users(id), Nullable\n\nUploader user ID\n\nUUID\n\nsource_module\n\nVARCHAR(100)\n\nDEFAULT 'platform', INDEX\n\nOrigin module\n\nplatform\n\ncreated_at\n\nTIMESTAMPTZ\n\nDEFAULT NOW()\n\nCreated timestamp\n\nISO datetime\n\nupdated_at\n\nTIMESTAMPTZ\n\nDEFAULT NOW()\n\nUpdated timestamp\n\nISO datetime\n\ndeleted_at\n\nTIMESTAMPTZ\n\nNullable, INDEX\n\nSoft delete timestamp\n\nnull\n\n\n\nNOTES: tags is TEXT[] not JSONB. file_size is BIGINT (returns as string from node-pg, parsed to integer in _formatAttachment). is_current_version partial index filters WHERE deleted_at IS NULL AND is_current_version = TRUE. previous_version_id creates a self-referencing linked list for version chains. source_module is auto-derived from auth context, never user-supplied.\n\n\n\n6.2.2 Entity: storage_quotas (10 columns)\n\n\n\nAttribute\n\nData Type\n\nConstraint / FK\n\nDescription\n\nExample\n\nid\n\nUUID\n\nPK, DEFAULT gen_random_uuid()\n\nQuota record ID\n\nUUID\n\ntenant_id\n\nUUID\n\nNOT NULL, FK → tenants(id), UNIQUE\n\nTenant isolation\n\nc0000000-...\n\nmax_storage_bytes\n\nBIGINT\n\nDEFAULT 10737418240 (10GB)\n\nMax total storage\n\n10737418240\n\nused_storage_bytes\n\nBIGINT\n\nDEFAULT 0\n\nCurrent usage (trigger-maintained)\n\n1536\n\nfile_count\n\nINTEGER\n\nDEFAULT 0\n\nActive file count (trigger-maintained)\n\n15\n\nmax_file_size_bytes\n\nBIGINT\n\nDEFAULT 104857600 (100MB)\n\nMax single file size\n\n104857600\n\nallowed_mime_types\n\nTEXT[]\n\nNullable\n\nWhitelist (null = all allowed)\n\nnull\n\nblocked_extensions\n\nTEXT[]\n\nNullable\n\nBlacklist\n\n{.exe,.bat}\n\ncreated_at\n\nTIMESTAMPTZ\n\nDEFAULT NOW()\n\nCreated timestamp\n\nISO datetime\n\nupdated_at\n\nTIMESTAMPTZ\n\nDEFAULT NOW()\n\nUpdated timestamp\n\nISO datetime\n\n\n\nNOTES: max_storage_bytes and used_storage_bytes are BIGINT. PostgreSQL trigger update_storage_usage() maintains used_storage_bytes and file_count automatically on file_attachments INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE. The trigger uses GREATEST(..., 0) to prevent negative values. Quota auto-creates via service when first upload occurs for a tenant.\n\n\n\nPostgreSQL Trigger Function:\n\nCREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION update_storage_usage() --- Handles INSERT (increment), DELETE (decrement), UPDATE with NEW.deleted_at IS NOT NULL AND OLD.deleted_at IS NULL (soft-delete decrement), UPDATE with NEW.deleted_at IS NULL AND OLD.deleted_at IS NOT NULL (restore increment). Uses GREATEST(used_storage_bytes - file_size, 0) and GREATEST(file_count - 1, 0) to prevent negative values.\n\n6.2.3 Entity: file_shares (15 columns)\n\n\n\nAttribute\n\nData Type\n\nConstraint / FK\n\nDescription\n\nExample\n\nid\n\nUUID\n\nPK, DEFAULT gen_random_uuid()\n\nShare record ID\n\nUUID\n\ntenant_id\n\nUUID\n\nNOT NULL, FK → tenants(id) ON DELETE CASCADE\n\nTenant isolation\n\nc0000000-...\n\nfile_id\n\nUUID\n\nNOT NULL, FK → file_attachments(id) ON DELETE CASCADE\n\nShared file\n\nUUID\n\nshare_token\n\nVARCHAR(255)\n\nNOT NULL, UNIQUE\n\n64-char hex token\n\na3f8c2d1...\n\nshare_type\n\nVARCHAR(50)\n\nDEFAULT 'link'\n\nShare type\n\nlink\n\nshared_with_user_id\n\nUUID\n\nFK → users(id), Nullable\n\nTarget user (for 'user' type)\n\nnull\n\nshared_with_email\n\nVARCHAR(255)\n\nNullable\n\nTarget email\n\nnull\n\npermissions\n\nVARCHAR(50)\n\nDEFAULT 'view'\n\nAccess level\n\nview/download\n\nexpires_at\n\nTIMESTAMPTZ\n\nNullable\n\nExpiry timestamp\n\nISO datetime\n\nmax_downloads\n\nINTEGER\n\nNullable\n\nDownload cap (null = unlimited)\n\n5\n\ncurrent_downloads\n\nINTEGER\n\nDEFAULT 0\n\nDownloads consumed\n\n2\n\nis_active\n\nBOOLEAN\n\nDEFAULT true\n\nActive flag (false = revoked)\n\ntrue\n\nlast_accessed_at\n\nTIMESTAMPTZ\n\nNullable\n\nLast access time\n\nISO datetime\n\ncreated_by\n\nUUID\n\nFK → users(id), Nullable\n\nShare creator\n\nUUID\n\ncreated_at\n\nTIMESTAMPTZ\n\nDEFAULT NOW()\n\nCreated timestamp\n\nISO datetime\n\n\n\nNOTES: share_token is UNIQUE. expires_at can be set directly or auto-calculated from expires_in_hours (service converts: new Date(Date.now() + hours * 3600000).toISOString()). max_downloads null means unlimited. current_downloads is incremented atomically on each access. is_active=false means share has been revoked.\n\n\n\n6.2.4 Entity: file_access_logs (8 columns)\n\n\n\nAttribute\n\nData Type\n\nConstraint / FK\n\nDescription\n\nExample\n\nid\n\nUUID\n\nPK, DEFAULT gen_random_uuid()\n\nLog record ID\n\nUUID\n\ntenant_id\n\nUUID\n\nNOT NULL, FK → tenants(id) ON DELETE CASCADE\n\nTenant isolation\n\nUUID\n\nfile_id\n\nUUID\n\nNOT NULL, FK → file_attachments(id) ON DELETE CASCADE\n\nFile accessed\n\nUUID\n\nuser_id\n\nUUID\n\nNOT NULL, FK → users(id)\n\nWho accessed\n\nUUID\n\naction\n\nVARCHAR(50)\n\nNOT NULL\n\nAction type\n\ndownload/preview/access\n\nip_address\n\nINET\n\nNullable\n\nClient IP\n\n192.168.1.1\n\nuser_agent\n\nTEXT\n\nNullable\n\nBrowser UA string\n\nMozilla/5.0...\n\ncreated_at\n\nTIMESTAMPTZ\n\nDEFAULT NOW()\n\nWhen accessed\n\nISO datetime\n\n\n\nNOTES: Records are insert-only (append-only audit log). ip_address and user_agent are captured from Express request object when available. action values include 'download', 'preview', 'access' for share access.\n\n\n\n7. Qdrant (Vector DB)\n\nPhase 1 Status: NOT USED by File Attachment Service.\n\nBinary file content is stored on the filesystem via LocalStorageProvider. File metadata is stored in PostgreSQL (file_attachments table). No vector embeddings of file content are generated in Phase 1.\n\n\n\nPlanned for Phase 2:\n\nCollection Name: file_content_vec_v1\n\nVector Dimension: 1536 (OpenAI text-embedding-3-small) or 768 (sentence-transformers)\n\nPayload Fields: file_id (string), tenant_id (string --- mandatory filter), original_name (string), entity_type (string), entity_id (string), mime_type (string), category (string), source_module (string), created_at (ISO string)\n\nPayload Indexes: tenant_id, entity_type, mime_type, source_module, created_at\n\nUse Case: Semantic search across extracted file content (PDF text, document text), similar file discovery, AI-powered content recommendations, duplicate detection via vector similarity rather than checksum matching.\n\nPrerequisite: Content extraction pipeline (PDF text extraction, OCR for images, document parsing) must be implemented first to generate text from binary files before vector embedding.\n\n\n\n8. Object Storage\n\nFolder Structure Hierarchy:\n\nRoot: UPLOAD_DIR (default: ./uploads/, configurable via environment variable)\n\nPattern: {UPLOAD_DIR}/{tenant_id}/{entity_type}/{YYYY}/{MM}/{uuid}_{sanitized_name}.ext\n\n\n\nExample hierarchy:\n\nuploads/\n\n  c0000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001/   (Tenant: Pharma Corp)\n\n    documents/   (Entity type)\n\n      2026/   (Year)\n\n        02/   (Month)\n\n          daf3c8ad-3660-4c13-8b57-0daf3cc9aaf0_test-upload.txt\n\n          d7cbcb77-a8f1-4e22-b901-3dc77f8e22a1_report-v2.pdf\n\n    capa/\n\n      2026/\n\n        02/\n\n          f1a2b3c4-..._corrective-action-photo.jpg\n\n    training/\n\n      2026/\n\n        01/\n\n          a9b8c7d6-..._sop-training-material.pdf\n\n  d0000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000002/   (Tenant: Another Org)\n\n    documents/\n\n    suppliers/\n\n    temp/   (Temporary storage)\n\n    thumbnails/   (Thumbnail storage)\n\n\n\nStorage Provider Abstraction:\n\nLocalStorageProvider (Phase 1 --- current):\n\nsave(relativePath, buffer) --- Creates directory tree via fs.mkdirSync({recursive: true}), writes buffer via fs.writeFileSync.\n\ndelete(relativePath) --- Removes file via fs.unlinkSync. Silently ignores if file doesn't exist.\n\nexists(relativePath) --- Checks via fs.existsSync.\n\ngetFullPath(relativePath) --- Joins UPLOAD_DIR + relativePath. Controller uses path.resolve() for absolute path.\n\nS3 Migration Path (Phase 2):\n\nImplement S3StorageProvider with identical interface (save, delete, exists, getFullPath). S3 key structure maps 1:1 to local folder hierarchy: s3://{bucket}/{tenant_id}/{entity_type}/{YYYY}/{MM}/{uuid}_{name}.ext. No changes to service or controller code required --- swap provider via configuration.\n\n\n\nSECTION 10 --- REDIS CACHE DETAILS\n\nFile Attachment Service uses 4 Redis cache key patterns with graceful degradation. The service operates normally if Redis is unavailable --- all cache operations are wrapped in try/catch and failures are logged but never block the main operation.\n\n\n\nCache Key Pattern\n\nPurpose\n\nTTL\n\nInvalidation\n\nfile:{tenant_id}:{file_id}\n\nSingle file metadata cache\n\n300s (5 min)\n\nOn update, delete\n\nquota:{tenant_id}\n\nTenant storage quota\n\n60s (1 min)\n\nOn upload, delete, quota update\n\nshare:{share_token}\n\nShare token lookup\n\n3600s (1 hr)\n\nOn revoke\n\nentity_files:{entity_type}:{entity_id}\n\nFiles by entity list\n\n120s (2 min)\n\nOn upload, delete to entity\n\n\n\nRedis Service Integration:\n\nFile: src/infrastructure/cache/redis.service.js --- singleton instance exported as module.exports = new RedisService().\n\nMethods used: get(key, parseJson=true), set(key, value, ttlSeconds), delete(key). Note: The Redis service uses delete() method, NOT del().\n\nCache invalidation method: _invalidateCache(tenantId, fileId, entityType, entityId) --- deletes all relevant cache keys after any write operation (upload, update, delete, share revoke).\n\nLazy initialization: getRedis() function returns singleton Redis instance on first call, returns null if Redis is unavailable. Every cache operation checks for null before proceeding.\n\n\n\nPlatform-level Redis usage that benefits File Attachment Service indirectly:\n\nSession caching: JWT session data cached by AuthService for fast auth validation on every request.\n\nPermission caching: User permissions cached by PermissionService, used by authorization middleware for files:read/files:write/files:admin checks.\n\n\n\nPlanned Phase 2 enhancements:\n\nFile content cache: Large frequently-accessed files cached in Redis for sub-millisecond retrieval.\n\nShare token bloom filter: Probabilistic check before DB lookup for invalid/revoked tokens.\n\nUpload rate limiting: Per-tenant upload rate tracking via Redis INCR + EXPIRE.	<p>SERVICE SPECIFICATION</p><p><strong>File Attachment Services</strong></p><p>QAssure Multi-Tenant SaaS Platform</p><p>Version 1.0   ·   February 2026</p><h1>1. Feature of Service</h1><h3>SERVICE NAME: FILE ATTACHMENT SERVICES</h3><p>Centralised, tenant-isolated, binary file lifecycle engine that governs how files (documents, images, reports, specifications, audit evidence, training materials) are uploaded, stored, versioned, downloaded, previewed, shared, and deleted across multi-tenant regulated environments. All binary file operations across the QAssure platform and module applications are routed through this single service.</p><p><strong>Core capabilities:</strong></p><p><strong>1. File Upload &amp; Storage</strong> --- Single and multi-file upload (up to 10 files per request) with multer memory storage, 100MB max file size enforcement, blocked dangerous extensions (.exe, .bat, .cmd, .sh, .ps1, .msi, .dll, .com, .scr, .vbs, .js), SHA-256 checksum calculation via crypto.createHash('sha256'), and automatic tenant-scoped folder hierarchy: {tenant_id}/{entity_type}/{YYYY}/{MM}/{uuid}_{sanitized_name}.ext. Files are linked to any platform entity via entity_type + entity_id (e.g., entity_type='documents', entity_id=document_uuid). Source module is auto-derived from authentication context: module code for combined JWT + API Key mode (e.g., 'coc', 'dhf') where module identity is captured from the API key even though user identity comes from JWT; 'platform' for JWT-only users; moduleCode for API key-only modules (e.g., 'aice', 'dhf'); or 'api_key' as fallback if an API key has no linked module. Priority order: req.module.code (combined mode) → req.user.moduleCode (API key mode) → 'platform' (JWT mode). Duplicate detection prevents re-upload of identical files to the same entity (enforced by checksum + entity match on active records).</p><p><strong>2. File Versioning</strong> --- Each file supports immutable version history. Uploading a new version via POST /:id/versions marks the previous version as is_current_version=false and creates a new record with incremented version number, linked via previous_version_id. Version history is retrieved via recursive CTE (Common Table Expression) that traverses the chain in both directions, returning complete lineage. Only the current version is returned in standard queries.</p><p><strong>3. Download &amp; Preview</strong> --- Download endpoint (GET /:id/download) serves files with Content-Disposition: attachment header for browser download prompt. Preview endpoint (GET /:id/preview) serves with Content-Disposition: inline for in-browser rendering. Both use storageProvider.getFullPath() with path.resolve() for absolute path resolution. File access is logged in file_access_logs table with user_id, IP address, user agent, and action type.</p><p><strong>4. File Sharing with Token-Based Access</strong> --- Create time-limited, download-limited share links via POST /:id/share. Generates 64-character hex token via crypto.randomBytes(32). Supports configurable parameters: expires_at (absolute timestamp), expires_in_hours (convenience --- auto-converted to expires_at), max_downloads (download cap), share_type ('link' or 'user'), shared_with_user_id, shared_with_email, and permissions ('view' or 'download'). Access via GET /shared/:token is a PUBLIC route (no authentication required). The service validates token existence, checks expiry, enforces max_downloads limit, increments current_downloads counter, and serves the file. Share revocation via DELETE /shares/:shareId deactivates the share.</p><p><strong>5. Storage Quota Management</strong> --- Per-tenant storage quotas tracked in storage_quotas table: max_storage_bytes (default 10GB), used_storage_bytes, file_count, max_file_size_bytes (default 100MB), allowed_mime_types, blocked_extensions. Quota auto-creates on first upload if not present. PostgreSQL trigger (trg_update_storage_usage) automatically increments/decrements used_storage_bytes and file_count on INSERT, DELETE, and UPDATE (soft-delete via SET deleted_at). Quota is checked before every upload: individual file size vs max_file_size_bytes, and total used + new file vs max_storage_bytes. Admin-only PUT /quota endpoint allows updating quota limits (requires files:admin permission).</p><p><strong>6. Soft Delete with Hard Delete Option</strong> --- Default DELETE /:id performs soft delete (sets deleted_at timestamp). PostgreSQL trigger automatically decrements quota on soft delete. Hard delete via DELETE /:id?hard=true permanently removes the database record AND the physical file from storage via storageProvider.delete(). Orphaned file cleanup: if database INSERT fails after file is saved to disk, the file is automatically cleaned up in the catch block.</p><p><strong>7. Storage Provider Abstraction (S3-Ready)</strong> --- All file I/O goes through LocalStorageProvider with 4 methods: save(relativePath, buffer), delete(relativePath), exists(relativePath), getFullPath(relativePath). No controller or service code touches the filesystem directly. Migrating to S3 requires implementing an S3StorageProvider with the same interface --- zero code changes in controllers or service logic.</p><p><strong>8. Three Authentication Modes</strong> --- Every endpoint (except public share access) supports three authentication modes via authenticate middleware: (a) JWT only --- QAssure UI users authenticate via Authorization: Bearer header; tenant derived from JWT claims; user permissions enforced via authorize(); (b) JWT + API Key combined --- module applications acting on behalf of a logged-in user send both Authorization: Bearer (JWT from verify-launch-token) and X-API-Key headers; tenant derived from JWT (X-Tenant-ID not needed); user permissions enforced (not module bypass); source_module set from module identity (e.g., 'coc', 'dhf') for accurate audit attribution; (c) API Key + X-Tenant-ID --- module system/cron operations with no user context send X-API-Key and X-Tenant-ID headers; module gets full access bypass on authorize(). This enables QAssure UI users, module applications acting on behalf of users (with real user identity in audit trails), and module background processes to consume the file attachment service. Routes use authorize() for permission checks: files:read for GET endpoints, files:write for POST/PUT/DELETE endpoints, files:admin for quota management.</p><p><strong>9. Multi-Tenant Data Isolation</strong> --- All queries are tenant-scoped. Every file, share, quota, and access log belongs to a tenant_id. API Key middleware validates module-tenant assignment via the tenant_module_access table (enabled = true) before granting access. Missing X-Tenant-ID returns error code MISSING_TENANT. Unassigned tenant returns MODULE_NOT_ASSIGNED. In combined mode (JWT + API Key), if the JWT's tenant does not match the API key's assigned tenant, error code TENANT_MISMATCH is returned; if the module is deactivated for the tenant, error code MODULE_NOT_ACTIVE is returned. Users cannot see or access files belonging to other tenants under any circumstance.</p><p><strong>10. Security Hardening</strong> --- UUID validation middleware on all :id route parameters returns 400 (INVALID_ID_FORMAT) for malformed UUIDs instead of leaking PostgreSQL errors. Error sanitization function (sanitizeErrorResponse) on all 17 controller catch blocks classifies errors into client-safe categories (QUOTA_ERROR, NOT_FOUND, VALIDATION_ERROR, etc.) and returns generic 'An internal error occurred' for unexpected failures --- never exposing constraint names, table structures, or query details. Blocked file extensions prevent executable uploads. Path traversal is impossible because stored names use UUID-based naming, not user-controlled paths.</p><p><strong>11. Observability</strong> --- Redis caching with graceful degradation (file cache 300s TTL, quota 60s, share 3600s, entity files 120s). Elasticsearch indexing for file searchability. File access logging with IP/user-agent tracking. Storage statistics with per-entity-type and per-module breakdowns. All infrastructure dependencies (Redis, Elasticsearch) fail gracefully --- the service continues operating without caching or indexing if either is unavailable.</p><h1>2. Scope</h1><h2>IN SCOPE (Phase 1):</h2><p><strong>Full file CRUD:</strong> upload single file (with auto-generated tenant-scoped storage path, checksum, source_module derivation), upload multiple files (up to 10, parallel via Promise.all), read (single by ID with Redis cache, paginated list with filtering, by entity with pagination), update metadata (description, tags, category, metadata JSONB), soft delete (with quota trigger), hard delete (with physical file removal).</p><p><strong>File versioning:</strong> upload new version (marks previous as not current, increments version number, links via previous_version_id), retrieve version history (recursive CTE traversal in both directions).</p><p><strong>Download and preview</strong> with correct Content-Disposition headers (attachment for download, inline for preview), file access logging, and path resolution via storageProvider abstraction.</p><p><strong>File sharing:</strong> create share link with configurable expiry (expires_at or expires_in_hours), max_downloads cap, share_type (link/user), permissions (view/download). Public access via token (no auth required). Share revocation. Expired/exhausted shares return 404.</p><p><strong>Storage quota management:</strong> auto-create on first upload, per-tenant limits (max_storage_bytes, max_file_size_bytes, allowed_mime_types, blocked_extensions), quota enforcement on every upload, admin-only quota configuration updates (files:admin permission), PostgreSQL trigger for automatic used_storage_bytes/file_count tracking on INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE.</p><p><strong>Three authentication modes</strong> (JWT only, JWT + API Key combined, API Key + X-Tenant-ID) on all 16 authenticated endpoints via authenticate middleware (1 public route excluded: GET /shared/:token). Combined mode derives tenant from JWT (X-Tenant-ID not needed), enforces user permissions, and sets source_module from module identity for accurate audit attribution.</p><p>Multi-tenant isolation on all read and write operations.</p><p>API Key middleware validates module-tenant access via tenant_module_access table.</p><p>UUID validation middleware on all :id parameters --- returns 400 for invalid UUIDs.</p><p>Error sanitization on all 17 catch blocks --- never leaks database internals.</p><p>File access audit trail via file_access_logs with user_id, IP address, user agent, action.</p><p>Storage statistics with quota summary, file type counts, by_entity and by_module breakdowns.</p><p>Pagination via limit and offset query parameters (default limit 50). Response is a JSON array of file objects.</p><p>Filter support on list: source_module, mime_type, entity_type, category, search (ILIKE on original_name), sort (created_at, file_size, original_name), date range (created_after, created_before).</p><p>Orphaned file cleanup: if DB INSERT fails after file save, file is automatically removed from storage.</p><p>Redis caching with graceful degradation (operates without Redis).</p><p>Elasticsearch indexing with graceful degradation (operates without ES).</p><h2>OUT OF SCOPE (Phase 1 --- File Attachment Service boundary):</h2><p><strong>S3/MinIO object storage migration</strong> --- LocalStorageProvider is production-ready; S3StorageProvider is a config swap planned for Phase 2. Storage provider abstraction is already in place.</p><p><strong>Virus/malware scanning</strong> --- scan_status and scanned_at columns exist in file_attachments table but no scanning service is integrated.</p><p><strong>Image thumbnail generation</strong> --- has_thumbnail, thumbnail_path columns exist but no thumbnail generation pipeline is implemented.</p><p>Full-text content extraction from uploaded files (PDF text extraction, OCR).</p><p>Vector embedding of file content for semantic search via Qdrant.</p><p>Real-time file upload progress (WebSocket-based progress tracking).</p><p>Client-side chunked upload for files larger than 100MB.</p><p>CDN integration for cached file delivery.</p><p>File format conversion (PDF to image, document to PDF, etc.).</p><p>Watermarking or digital stamping of downloaded files.</p><p>Bulk import/export of files.</p><p><strong>File-level access control lists (ACLs)</strong> --- access is controlled at entity level via entity_type permissions, not per-file.</p><p><strong>Retention policy enforcement</strong> --- soft-deleted files remain in database indefinitely until hard-deleted.</p><p><strong>Email/SMS notifications on file events</strong> (handled by Notification Service separately).</p><p><strong>Document lifecycle management</strong> (SOPs, versioning, review/approval) --- handled by separate Document Service (document.service.js).</p><h1>3. Clear Responsibility Split</h1><h2>3.1 SaaS Platform (Owned by Platform Team):</h2><p>Provide and maintain the File Attachment Service as a centralised, tenant-isolated binary file management engine.</p><p>Enforce file upload constraints: 100MB max file size (multer + quota), blocked dangerous extensions, per-tenant storage quotas with auto-creation and trigger-based tracking.</p><p>Generate and manage tenant-scoped storage paths: {tenant_id}/{entity_type}/{YYYY}/{MM}/{uuid}_{sanitized_name}.ext.</p><p>Calculate SHA-256 checksums on every upload for integrity verification.</p><p>Auto-derive source_module from authentication context with priority: req.module.code for combined JWT + API Key mode (e.g., 'coc', 'dhf'), req.user.moduleCode for API key-only modules, 'platform' for JWT-only users, 'api_key' fallback for API keys without linked modules. This ensures files uploaded by modules on behalf of users are correctly attributed to the originating module in storage statistics.</p><p>Manage file versioning: version number increment, previous_version_id linking, is_current_version flagging, recursive CTE version history retrieval.</p><p>Maintain storage provider abstraction (LocalStorageProvider) with save, delete, exists, getFullPath methods for future S3 migration.</p><p>Enforce tenant isolation on all queries via tenant_id filtering. No cross-tenant file access is possible.</p><p>Provide three authentication modes on all 16 authenticated endpoints via authenticate middleware (1 public route excluded: GET /shared/:token): (a) JWT only for QAssure UI users, (b) JWT + API Key combined for module applications acting on behalf of logged-in users (tenant from JWT, user permissions enforced, source_module from module identity), (c) API Key + X-Tenant-ID for module system/cron operations.</p><p>Validate module-tenant access via tenant_module_access table (enabled = true check). Return MISSING_TENANT or MODULE_NOT_ASSIGNED errors for invalid API Key requests. In combined mode, return TENANT_MISMATCH if JWT tenant differs from API key's assigned tenant, and MODULE_NOT_ACTIVE if the module is deactivated for the tenant.</p><p>Provide UUID validation middleware for all route parameters. Return 400 INVALID_ID_FORMAT for malformed UUIDs.</p><p>Sanitise all error responses to prevent database internal leakage.</p><p>Provide file sharing infrastructure: token generation, expiry enforcement, max_downloads enforcement, public access route.</p><p>Provide storage statistics with per-entity-type and per-module breakdowns.</p><p>Log all file access events (download, preview, checkout, checkin) in file_access_logs.</p><p>Provide Redis caching and Elasticsearch indexing with graceful degradation.</p><p>Expose admin-only quota management endpoint (files:admin permission).</p><h2>3.2 Module Applications (Residing on Platform; Owned by App Teams --- LTTS):</h2><p>Use the File Attachment Service API with the appropriate authentication mode:</p><p><strong>For user-context operations (user uploads a file via module UI):</strong> Send both Authorization: Bearer (JWT obtained from verify-launch-token) and X-API-Key headers. X-Tenant-ID is NOT needed --- tenant is derived from JWT. User permissions (files:read, files:write) are enforced. uploaded_by and file_access_logs.user_id record the real user identity. source_module records the module identity (e.g., 'coc').</p><p><strong>For system/cron operations (no user context):</strong> Send X-API-Key + X-Tenant-ID headers. Module gets full access bypass. source_module records the module identity from API key.</p><p>Specify entity_type and entity_id when uploading files to link them to the correct module entity (e.g., entity_type='documents', entity_id=&lt;document_uuid&gt; for Document Service; entity_type='capa', entity_id=&lt;capa_uuid&gt; for CAPA module).</p><p>Provide required fields on upload: file (multipart), entity_type (string), entity_id (UUID). Optional fields: description, category, tags, metadata (JSONB).</p><p>Use the version upload endpoint (POST /:id/versions) for updating files rather than deleting and re-uploading. This preserves version history and audit trail.</p><p>Use the share endpoint to generate time-limited, download-limited links for external stakeholders (e.g., auditors, suppliers) without granting platform login access.</p><p>Do NOT implement custom file storage logic --- always delegate to platform's file attachment service.</p><p>Do NOT store file metadata in the module's own database --- use platform as the system of record for all file attachments. Use the entity link (entity_type + entity_id) to associate files with module records.</p><p>Do NOT construct file paths manually or access the uploads directory directly --- always use service API endpoints for file access.</p><p>Use SSO launch token flow to obtain both tenant context and user session JWT dynamically in production: call POST /modules/verify-launch-token with X-API-Key and the launch token to receive a JWT (24h expiry), user details (including department, designation, user category), tenant details, and permissions. Use this JWT in Authorization: Bearer header alongside X-API-Key for all user-context platform calls. Use .env TENANT_ID + X-Tenant-ID header for development and system/cron operations only.</p><h1>4. API List (Refer to Excel Sheet shared)</h1><table><thead><tr><th><p><strong>#</strong></p></th><th><p><strong>Method</strong></p></th><th><p><strong>Endpoint</strong></p></th><th><p><strong>Auth</strong></p></th><th><p><strong>Description</strong></p></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><p><strong>1</strong></p></td><td><p><strong>POST</strong></p></td><td><p>/api/v1/files/upload</p></td><td><p>authenticate + files:write</p></td><td><p>Upload single file (multipart)</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>2</strong></p></td><td><p><strong>POST</strong></p></td><td><p>/api/v1/files/upload/multiple</p></td><td><p>authenticate + files:write</p></td><td><p>Upload multiple files (up to 10)</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>3</strong></p></td><td><p><strong>GET</strong></p></td><td><p>/api/v1/files</p></td><td><p>authenticate + files:read</p></td><td><p>List files (paginated, filterable)</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>4</strong></p></td><td><p><strong>GET</strong></p></td><td><p>/api/v1/files/:id</p></td><td><p>authenticate + files:read + validateUUID</p></td><td><p>Get single file by ID</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>5</strong></p></td><td><p><strong>PUT</strong></p></td><td><p>/api/v1/files/:id</p></td><td><p>authenticate + files:write + validateUUID</p></td><td><p>Update file metadata</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>6</strong></p></td><td><p><strong>DELETE</strong></p></td><td><p>/api/v1/files/:id</p></td><td><p>authenticate + files:write + validateUUID</p></td><td><p>Delete file (soft; ?hard=true for permanent)</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>7</strong></p></td><td><p><strong>GET</strong></p></td><td><p>/api/v1/files/:id/download</p></td><td><p>authenticate + files:read + validateUUID</p></td><td><p>Download file (Content-Disposition: attachment)</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>8</strong></p></td><td><p><strong>GET</strong></p></td><td><p>/api/v1/files/:id/preview</p></td><td><p>authenticate + files:read + validateUUID</p></td><td><p>Preview file (Content-Disposition: inline)</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>9</strong></p></td><td><p><strong>POST</strong></p></td><td><p>/api/v1/files/:id/versions</p></td><td><p>authenticate + files:write + validateUUID</p></td><td><p>Upload new version of existing file</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>10</strong></p></td><td><p><strong>GET</strong></p></td><td><p>/api/v1/files/:id/versions</p></td><td><p>authenticate + files:read + validateUUID</p></td><td><p>Get version history (recursive CTE)</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>11</strong></p></td><td><p><strong>POST</strong></p></td><td><p>/api/v1/files/:id/share</p></td><td><p>authenticate + files:write + validateUUID</p></td><td><p>Create share link (token, expiry, max_downloads)</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>12</strong></p></td><td><p><strong>DELETE</strong></p></td><td><p>/api/v1/files/shares/:shareId</p></td><td><p>authenticate + files:write + validateUUID</p></td><td><p>Revoke share link</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>13</strong></p></td><td><p><strong>GET</strong></p></td><td><p>/api/v1/files/shared/:token</p></td><td><p>PUBLIC (no auth)</p></td><td><p>Access shared file via token</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>14</strong></p></td><td><p><strong>GET</strong></p></td><td><p>/api/v1/files/entity/:entity_type/:entity_id</p></td><td><p>authenticate + files:read + validateUUID</p></td><td><p>Get files by entity (paginated)</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>15</strong></p></td><td><p><strong>GET</strong></p></td><td><p>/api/v1/files/stats</p></td><td><p>authenticate + files:read</p></td><td><p>Get storage statistics (by entity, by module)</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>16</strong></p></td><td><p><strong>GET</strong></p></td><td><p>/api/v1/files/quota</p></td><td><p>authenticate + files:read</p></td><td><p>Get tenant storage quota</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>17</strong></p></td><td><p><strong>PUT</strong></p></td><td><p>/api/v1/files/quota</p></td><td><p>authenticate + files:admin</p></td><td><p>Update tenant storage quota (admin only)</p></td></tr></tbody></table><p><em>Route ordering note: /stats, /quota, /entity/:type/:id, /shared/:token are registered BEFORE /:id to prevent Express matching static segments as UUID parameters.</em></p><h1>5. Typical Workflow</h1><h2>5.1 Write Path (File Upload &amp; Management):</h2><p><strong>1. Upload Single File:</strong> Tenant user or module calls POST /api/v1/files/upload with multipart form data: file (required, binary), entity_type (required, e.g., 'documents'), entity_id (required, UUID of parent entity), and optional: description, category, tags (comma-separated string or JSON array), metadata (JSONB). The API validates: file presence (returns VALIDATION_ERROR 'No file uploaded'), entity_type and entity_id presence (returns VALIDATION_ERROR 'entity_type and entity_id are required'), file extension not in blocked list (.exe, .bat, .cmd, .sh, .ps1, .msi, .dll, .com, .scr, .vbs, .js --- returns error 'File type .exe is not allowed'), file size under 100MB (returns FILE_TOO_LARGE 'File size exceeds 100MB limit'), tenant quota not exceeded (returns QUOTA_ERROR for both individual file and total storage).</p><p>Platform generates: stored_name as {uuid}_{sanitized_original_name}.ext, storage path as {tenant_id}/{entity_type}/{YYYY}/{MM}/{stored_name}, SHA-256 checksum via crypto.createHash('sha256').update(buffer).digest('hex'), source_module derived from auth context with priority: req.module.code for combined mode (e.g., 'coc', 'dhf'), 'platform' for JWT-only users, req.user.moduleCode for API key-only modules (e.g., 'aice', 'dhf'), or 'api_key' fallback if API key has no linked module. File is saved to storage via storageProvider.save(relativePath, buffer), then database record is inserted into file_attachments. If DB INSERT fails, file is automatically cleaned up from storage (orphan prevention). Redis cache is invalidated for entity files list. Elasticsearch index is updated via IndexingHooks. Returns full file object with 201 status.</p><p><strong>2. Upload Multiple Files:</strong> POST /api/v1/files/upload/multiple accepts up to 10 files via multer array ('files' field, max 10). All files are uploaded in parallel via Promise.all, sharing the same entity_type and entity_id. Returns array of results with per-file success/error status. Quota is checked per file individually.</p><p><strong>3. Update Metadata:</strong> PUT /api/v1/files/:id updates allowed fields: description (text), category (varchar), tags (cast as text[]), metadata (cast as JSONB). File content changes go through versioning (POST /:id/versions), not metadata update. Sets updated_at timestamp. Returns 'No valid fields to update' error if no recognized fields provided. Returns full file object with 200 status.</p><p><strong>4. Soft Delete:</strong> DELETE /api/v1/files/:id sets deleted_at = NOW() timestamp. PostgreSQL trigger (trg_update_storage_usage) automatically decrements used_storage_bytes and file_count in storage_quotas using GREATEST(..., 0) to prevent negative values. File remains on disk for potential recovery. Redis cache invalidated. Elasticsearch index updated. Returns { success: true, message: 'File deleted' }.</p><p><strong>5. Hard Delete:</strong> DELETE /api/v1/files/:id?hard=true permanently removes: database record (DELETE FROM file_attachments WHERE id = $1 AND tenant_id = $2), physical file from storage via storageProvider.delete(file_path), thumbnail if exists via storageProvider.delete(thumbnail_path). Returns 404 on subsequent GET. Irreversible. Quota adjusts via DELETE trigger.</p><h2>5.2 Versioning:</h2><p><strong>Minor Versioning:</strong> Default behavior. Every POST /api/v1/files/:id/versions with new file (multipart) performs: fetches current record by ID and tenant_id, marks it as is_current_version=false via UPDATE, inserts new record with version = previous_version + 1, is_current_version=true, previous_version_id = old record ID. SHA-256 checksum is calculated for the new file. Both old and new records remain in database --- version history is immutable and never deleted.</p><p><strong>Version History Retrieval:</strong> GET /api/v1/files/:id/versions uses a recursive CTE (Common Table Expression) that traverses the previous_version_id chain in both directions (forward via self-join on previous_version_id, backward via matching previous_version_id = id). Returns complete version lineage with: id, version, file_size, checksum, original_name, uploaded_by_email (resolved via LEFT JOIN users), created_at for each version. Ordered by version number ascending.</p><p><strong>Version Chain Structure:</strong> file_attachments.previous_version_id creates a self-referencing linked list. Version 1 has previous_version_id=null. Version 2 points to Version 1. Version 3 points to Version 2. Only the latest version has is_current_version=true. Standard queries (list, get by entity) only return current versions (WHERE is_current_version = true AND deleted_at IS NULL).</p><h2>5.3 Read/Search Path:</h2><p><strong>Get by ID:</strong> GET /api/v1/files/:id --- Redis cached (300s TTL). Returns full file object with uploaded_by_email resolved via LEFT JOIN users. Filters by tenant_id for isolation.</p><p><strong>List Files:</strong> GET /api/v1/files?limit=50&amp;offset=0 --- returns a JSON array of file objects. Supports limit and offset query parameters for pagination. Supports filters: source_module, mime_type, entity_type, category, search (ILIKE on original_name), sort_by (created_at|file_size|original_name), sort_order (ASC|DESC), created_after, created_before. Only returns active files (deleted_at IS NULL, is_current_version = true).</p><p><strong>Get by Entity:</strong> GET /api/v1/files/entity/:entity_type/:entity_id --- returns a JSON array of all files linked to a specific entity. Used by Document Service, CAPA module, Training module, etc. to retrieve their linked files.</p><p><strong>Storage Stats:</strong> GET /api/v1/files/stats --- returns four sections: quota (max_storage, used_storage, available, usage_percent), files (total, images, pdfs, documents counts), by_entity (count + size per entity_type), and by_module (count + size per source_module e.g., 'platform', 'coc').</p><p><strong>Storage Quota:</strong> GET /api/v1/files/quota --- returns current quota status: max_storage_bytes, used_storage_bytes, file_count, max_file_size_bytes, allowed_mime_types, blocked_extensions, usage_percentage.</p><h2>5.4 File Sharing Path:</h2><p><strong>Create Share:</strong> POST /api/v1/files/:id/share with body: share_type ('link'|'user'), permissions ('view'|'download'), expires_at (ISO timestamp) or expires_in_hours (number, auto-converted), max_downloads (integer), shared_with_user_id, shared_with_email. Service generates 64-char hex token via crypto.randomBytes(32). Returns share object with share_token, share_url, expires_at, max_downloads. Redis cached (3600s TTL).</p><p><strong>Access Shared File:</strong> GET /api/v1/files/shared/:token --- PUBLIC route (no authentication). Service: looks up share by token (Redis cached), validates expiry (expires_at &gt; NOW()), validates download count (current_downloads &lt; max_downloads), increments current_downloads, resolves file path via storageProvider.getFullPath(), serves file with correct Content-Type and Content-Disposition headers. Returns 404 if share not found, expired, or download limit reached.</p><p><strong>Revoke Share:</strong> DELETE /api/v1/files/shares/:shareId --- sets is_active=false. Invalidates Redis cache for the share token.</p><h2>5.5 Retention/Deletion:</h2><p>Phase 1: No automated retention policy enforcement. Soft-deleted files remain in database with deleted_at timestamp set. Hard delete permanently removes file and database record.</p><p><strong>Quota Recovery:</strong> Soft delete triggers automatic quota decrement via PostgreSQL trigger. Restoring a soft-deleted file (clearing deleted_at) would trigger quota increment.</p><p><strong>Planned Phase 2:</strong> Configurable retention periods per entity_type. Scheduled cleanup job to hard-delete files past retention period. Archived storage tier for long-term retention at reduced cost.</p><h2>5.6 Audit Trail:</h2><p>file_access_logs records (inserted on download, preview, and any file access): file_id, tenant_id, user_id, action ('download'|'preview'|'access'), ip_address (from req.ip), user_agent (from req.headers), created_at.</p><p>Storage quota changes tracked via PostgreSQL trigger --- every INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE on file_attachments automatically adjusts used_storage_bytes and file_count.</p><p>Share access tracked via current_downloads counter and last_accessed_at timestamp on file_shares.</p><p><strong>Authentication mode impacts audit attribution:</strong> In combined JWT + API Key mode, uploaded_by and file_access_logs.user_id record the real user identity (from JWT), while source_module records the originating module (from API key). This provides GxP-compliant traceability --- auditors can see both who performed the action and which module facilitated it. In API Key-only mode, uploaded_by records the module service account and source_module records the module code. In JWT-only mode, uploaded_by records the platform user and source_module is set to 'platform'.</p><h1>6. PostgreSQL DB --- MODEL</h1><p>The File Attachment Service uses 4 PostgreSQL tables: file_attachments (master record with binary file metadata, entity linking, versioning, and storage path), storage_quotas (per-tenant storage limits with trigger-maintained usage counters), file_shares (token-based file sharing with expiry and download limits), and file_access_logs (audit trail for file access events). All tables enforce multi-tenant isolation via tenant_id foreign key with ON DELETE CASCADE. The file_attachments table serves as the central entity. A PostgreSQL trigger function (update_storage_usage) automatically maintains used_storage_bytes and file_count in storage_quotas on INSERT, UPDATE (soft-delete/restore), and DELETE operations.</p><h2>6.1 ER Diagram:</h2><p><img src="data:image/png;base64," /></p><p><em>Figure 6.1: Entity Relationship Diagram --- File Attachment Service</em></p><p>file_attachments.tenant_id → tenants.id (ON DELETE CASCADE)</p><p>file_attachments.uploaded_by → users.id</p><p>file_attachments.previous_version_id → file_attachments.id (self-referencing version chain)</p><p>storage_quotas.tenant_id → tenants.id (UNIQUE --- one quota per tenant)</p><p>file_shares.tenant_id → tenants.id, file_shares.file_id → file_attachments.id</p><p>file_access_logs.file_id → file_attachments.id, file_access_logs.user_id → users.id</p><h2>6.2 Entities &amp; Attributes:</h2><h3>6.2.1 Entity: file_attachments (32 columns)</h3><table><thead><tr><th><p><strong>Attribute</strong></p></th><th><p><strong>Data Type</strong></p></th><th><p><strong>Constraint / FK</strong></p></th><th><p><strong>Description</strong></p></th><th><p><strong>Example</strong></p></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><p><strong>id</strong></p></td><td><p>UUID</p></td><td><p>PK, DEFAULT gen_random_uuid()</p></td><td><p>Unique file identifier</p></td><td><p>daf3c8ad-...</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>tenant_id</strong></p></td><td><p>UUID</p></td><td><p>NOT NULL, FK → tenants(id) ON DELETE CASCADE, INDEX</p></td><td><p>Tenant isolation</p></td><td><p>c0000000-...</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>original_name</strong></p></td><td><p>VARCHAR(500)</p></td><td><p>NOT NULL</p></td><td><p>User-uploaded filename</p></td><td><p>test-upload.txt</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>stored_name</strong></p></td><td><p>VARCHAR(500)</p></td><td><p>NOT NULL</p></td><td><p>UUID-based stored name</p></td><td><p>daf3c8ad_test-upload.txt</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>file_path</strong></p></td><td><p>VARCHAR(1000)</p></td><td><p>NOT NULL</p></td><td><p>Relative storage path</p></td><td><p>c000.../documents/2026/02/daf3...txt</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>mime_type</strong></p></td><td><p>VARCHAR(255)</p></td><td><p>NOT NULL</p></td><td><p>MIME type</p></td><td><p>text/plain</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>file_size</strong></p></td><td><p>BIGINT</p></td><td><p>NOT NULL</p></td><td><p>File size in bytes</p></td><td><p>18</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>checksum</strong></p></td><td><p>VARCHAR(128)</p></td><td><p>Nullable</p></td><td><p>SHA-256 hash</p></td><td><p>a591a6d40...</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>storage_provider</strong></p></td><td><p>VARCHAR(50)</p></td><td><p>DEFAULT 'local'</p></td><td><p>Storage backend</p></td><td><p>local</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>storage_bucket</strong></p></td><td><p>VARCHAR(255)</p></td><td><p>Nullable</p></td><td><p>S3 bucket (Phase 2)</p></td><td><p>null</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>storage_region</strong></p></td><td><p>VARCHAR(100)</p></td><td><p>Nullable</p></td><td><p>S3 region (Phase 2)</p></td><td><p>null</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>entity_type</strong></p></td><td><p>VARCHAR(100)</p></td><td><p>NOT NULL, INDEX</p></td><td><p>Parent entity type</p></td><td><p>documents</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>entity_id</strong></p></td><td><p>UUID</p></td><td><p>NOT NULL, INDEX</p></td><td><p>Parent entity ID</p></td><td><p>UUID</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>category</strong></p></td><td><p>VARCHAR(100)</p></td><td><p>Nullable</p></td><td><p>File category</p></td><td><p>specification</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>description</strong></p></td><td><p>TEXT</p></td><td><p>Nullable</p></td><td><p>File description</p></td><td><p>Test file upload</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>tags</strong></p></td><td><p>TEXT[]</p></td><td><p>Nullable</p></td><td><p>PostgreSQL text array</p></td><td><p>{sop,quality}</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>version</strong></p></td><td><p>INTEGER</p></td><td><p>DEFAULT 1</p></td><td><p>Version number</p></td><td><p>1</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>is_current_version</strong></p></td><td><p>BOOLEAN</p></td><td><p>DEFAULT true, INDEX (partial)</p></td><td><p>Active version flag</p></td><td><p>true</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>previous_version_id</strong></p></td><td><p>UUID</p></td><td><p>FK → file_attachments(id), Nullable</p></td><td><p>Version chain link</p></td><td><p>UUID or null</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>is_public</strong></p></td><td><p>BOOLEAN</p></td><td><p>DEFAULT false</p></td><td><p>Public access flag</p></td><td><p>false</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>access_level</strong></p></td><td><p>VARCHAR(50)</p></td><td><p>DEFAULT 'private'</p></td><td><p>Access level</p></td><td><p>private</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>download_count</strong></p></td><td><p>INTEGER</p></td><td><p>DEFAULT 0</p></td><td><p>Total downloads</p></td><td><p>5</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>has_thumbnail</strong></p></td><td><p>BOOLEAN</p></td><td><p>DEFAULT false</p></td><td><p>Thumbnail exists</p></td><td><p>false</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>thumbnail_path</strong></p></td><td><p>VARCHAR(1000)</p></td><td><p>Nullable</p></td><td><p>Thumbnail storage path</p></td><td><p>null</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>metadata</strong></p></td><td><p>JSONB</p></td><td><p>DEFAULT '{}'</p></td><td><p>Extensible metadata</p></td><td><p>{}</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>scan_status</strong></p></td><td><p>VARCHAR(50)</p></td><td><p>Nullable</p></td><td><p>Virus scan status (Phase 2)</p></td><td><p>null</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>scanned_at</strong></p></td><td><p>TIMESTAMPTZ</p></td><td><p>Nullable</p></td><td><p>Scan timestamp (Phase 2)</p></td><td><p>null</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>uploaded_by</strong></p></td><td><p>UUID</p></td><td><p>FK → users(id), Nullable</p></td><td><p>Uploader user ID</p></td><td><p>UUID</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>source_module</strong></p></td><td><p>VARCHAR(100)</p></td><td><p>DEFAULT 'platform', INDEX</p></td><td><p>Origin module</p></td><td><p>platform</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>created_at</strong></p></td><td><p>TIMESTAMPTZ</p></td><td><p>DEFAULT NOW()</p></td><td><p>Created timestamp</p></td><td><p>ISO datetime</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>updated_at</strong></p></td><td><p>TIMESTAMPTZ</p></td><td><p>DEFAULT NOW()</p></td><td><p>Updated timestamp</p></td><td><p>ISO datetime</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>deleted_at</strong></p></td><td><p>TIMESTAMPTZ</p></td><td><p>Nullable, INDEX</p></td><td><p>Soft delete timestamp</p></td><td><p>null</p></td></tr></tbody></table><p><em>NOTES: tags is TEXT[] not JSONB. file_size is BIGINT (returns as string from node-pg, parsed to integer in _formatAttachment). is_current_version partial index filters WHERE deleted_at IS NULL AND is_current_version = TRUE. previous_version_id creates a self-referencing linked list for version chains. source_module is auto-derived from auth context, never user-supplied.</em></p><h3>6.2.2 Entity: storage_quotas (10 columns)</h3><table><thead><tr><th><p><strong>Attribute</strong></p></th><th><p><strong>Data Type</strong></p></th><th><p><strong>Constraint / FK</strong></p></th><th><p><strong>Description</strong></p></th><th><p><strong>Example</strong></p></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><p><strong>id</strong></p></td><td><p>UUID</p></td><td><p>PK, DEFAULT gen_random_uuid()</p></td><td><p>Quota record ID</p></td><td><p>UUID</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>tenant_id</strong></p></td><td><p>UUID</p></td><td><p>NOT NULL, FK → tenants(id), UNIQUE</p></td><td><p>Tenant isolation</p></td><td><p>c0000000-...</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>max_storage_bytes</strong></p></td><td><p>BIGINT</p></td><td><p>DEFAULT 10737418240 (10GB)</p></td><td><p>Max total storage</p></td><td><p>10737418240</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>used_storage_bytes</strong></p></td><td><p>BIGINT</p></td><td><p>DEFAULT 0</p></td><td><p>Current usage (trigger-maintained)</p></td><td><p>1536</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>file_count</strong></p></td><td><p>INTEGER</p></td><td><p>DEFAULT 0</p></td><td><p>Active file count (trigger-maintained)</p></td><td><p>15</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>max_file_size_bytes</strong></p></td><td><p>BIGINT</p></td><td><p>DEFAULT 104857600 (100MB)</p></td><td><p>Max single file size</p></td><td><p>104857600</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>allowed_mime_types</strong></p></td><td><p>TEXT[]</p></td><td><p>Nullable</p></td><td><p>Whitelist (null = all allowed)</p></td><td><p>null</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>blocked_extensions</strong></p></td><td><p>TEXT[]</p></td><td><p>Nullable</p></td><td><p>Blacklist</p></td><td><p>{.exe,.bat}</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>created_at</strong></p></td><td><p>TIMESTAMPTZ</p></td><td><p>DEFAULT NOW()</p></td><td><p>Created timestamp</p></td><td><p>ISO datetime</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>updated_at</strong></p></td><td><p>TIMESTAMPTZ</p></td><td><p>DEFAULT NOW()</p></td><td><p>Updated timestamp</p></td><td><p>ISO datetime</p></td></tr></tbody></table><p><em>NOTES: max_storage_bytes and used_storage_bytes are BIGINT. PostgreSQL trigger update_storage_usage() maintains used_storage_bytes and file_count automatically on file_attachments INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE. The trigger uses GREATEST(..., 0) to prevent negative values. Quota auto-creates via service when first upload occurs for a tenant.</em></p><h3>PostgreSQL Trigger Function:</h3><p>CREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION update_storage_usage() --- Handles INSERT (increment), DELETE (decrement), UPDATE with NEW.deleted_at IS NOT NULL AND OLD.deleted_at IS NULL (soft-delete decrement), UPDATE with NEW.deleted_at IS NULL AND OLD.deleted_at IS NOT NULL (restore increment). Uses GREATEST(used_storage_bytes - file_size, 0) and GREATEST(file_count - 1, 0) to prevent negative values.</p><h3>6.2.3 Entity: file_shares (15 columns)</h3><table><thead><tr><th><p><strong>Attribute</strong></p></th><th><p><strong>Data Type</strong></p></th><th><p><strong>Constraint / FK</strong></p></th><th><p><strong>Description</strong></p></th><th><p><strong>Example</strong></p></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><p><strong>id</strong></p></td><td><p>UUID</p></td><td><p>PK, DEFAULT gen_random_uuid()</p></td><td><p>Share record ID</p></td><td><p>UUID</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>tenant_id</strong></p></td><td><p>UUID</p></td><td><p>NOT NULL, FK → tenants(id) ON DELETE CASCADE</p></td><td><p>Tenant isolation</p></td><td><p>c0000000-...</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>file_id</strong></p></td><td><p>UUID</p></td><td><p>NOT NULL, FK → file_attachments(id) ON DELETE CASCADE</p></td><td><p>Shared file</p></td><td><p>UUID</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>share_token</strong></p></td><td><p>VARCHAR(255)</p></td><td><p>NOT NULL, UNIQUE</p></td><td><p>64-char hex token</p></td><td><p>a3f8c2d1...</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>share_type</strong></p></td><td><p>VARCHAR(50)</p></td><td><p>DEFAULT 'link'</p></td><td><p>Share type</p></td><td><p>link</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>shared_with_user_id</strong></p></td><td><p>UUID</p></td><td><p>FK → users(id), Nullable</p></td><td><p>Target user (for 'user' type)</p></td><td><p>null</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>shared_with_email</strong></p></td><td><p>VARCHAR(255)</p></td><td><p>Nullable</p></td><td><p>Target email</p></td><td><p>null</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>permissions</strong></p></td><td><p>VARCHAR(50)</p></td><td><p>DEFAULT 'view'</p></td><td><p>Access level</p></td><td><p>view/download</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>expires_at</strong></p></td><td><p>TIMESTAMPTZ</p></td><td><p>Nullable</p></td><td><p>Expiry timestamp</p></td><td><p>ISO datetime</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>max_downloads</strong></p></td><td><p>INTEGER</p></td><td><p>Nullable</p></td><td><p>Download cap (null = unlimited)</p></td><td><p>5</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>current_downloads</strong></p></td><td><p>INTEGER</p></td><td><p>DEFAULT 0</p></td><td><p>Downloads consumed</p></td><td><p>2</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>is_active</strong></p></td><td><p>BOOLEAN</p></td><td><p>DEFAULT true</p></td><td><p>Active flag (false = revoked)</p></td><td><p>true</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>last_accessed_at</strong></p></td><td><p>TIMESTAMPTZ</p></td><td><p>Nullable</p></td><td><p>Last access time</p></td><td><p>ISO datetime</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>created_by</strong></p></td><td><p>UUID</p></td><td><p>FK → users(id), Nullable</p></td><td><p>Share creator</p></td><td><p>UUID</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>created_at</strong></p></td><td><p>TIMESTAMPTZ</p></td><td><p>DEFAULT NOW()</p></td><td><p>Created timestamp</p></td><td><p>ISO datetime</p></td></tr></tbody></table><p><em>NOTES: share_token is UNIQUE. expires_at can be set directly or auto-calculated from expires_in_hours (service converts: new Date(Date.now() + hours * 3600000).toISOString()). max_downloads null means unlimited. current_downloads is incremented atomically on each access. is_active=false means share has been revoked.</em></p><h3>6.2.4 Entity: file_access_logs (8 columns)</h3><table><thead><tr><th><p><strong>Attribute</strong></p></th><th><p><strong>Data Type</strong></p></th><th><p><strong>Constraint / FK</strong></p></th><th><p><strong>Description</strong></p></th><th><p><strong>Example</strong></p></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><p><strong>id</strong></p></td><td><p>UUID</p></td><td><p>PK, DEFAULT gen_random_uuid()</p></td><td><p>Log record ID</p></td><td><p>UUID</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>tenant_id</strong></p></td><td><p>UUID</p></td><td><p>NOT NULL, FK → tenants(id) ON DELETE CASCADE</p></td><td><p>Tenant isolation</p></td><td><p>UUID</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>file_id</strong></p></td><td><p>UUID</p></td><td><p>NOT NULL, FK → file_attachments(id) ON DELETE CASCADE</p></td><td><p>File accessed</p></td><td><p>UUID</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>user_id</strong></p></td><td><p>UUID</p></td><td><p>NOT NULL, FK → users(id)</p></td><td><p>Who accessed</p></td><td><p>UUID</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>action</strong></p></td><td><p>VARCHAR(50)</p></td><td><p>NOT NULL</p></td><td><p>Action type</p></td><td><p>download/preview/access</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>ip_address</strong></p></td><td><p>INET</p></td><td><p>Nullable</p></td><td><p>Client IP</p></td><td><p>192.168.1.1</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>user_agent</strong></p></td><td><p>TEXT</p></td><td><p>Nullable</p></td><td><p>Browser UA string</p></td><td><p>Mozilla/5.0...</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>created_at</strong></p></td><td><p>TIMESTAMPTZ</p></td><td><p>DEFAULT NOW()</p></td><td><p>When accessed</p></td><td><p>ISO datetime</p></td></tr></tbody></table><p><em>NOTES: Records are insert-only (append-only audit log). ip_address and user_agent are captured from Express request object when available. action values include 'download', 'preview', 'access' for share access.</em></p><h1>7. Qdrant (Vector DB)</h1><p><strong>Phase 1 Status: NOT USED by File Attachment Service.</strong></p><p>Binary file content is stored on the filesystem via LocalStorageProvider. File metadata is stored in PostgreSQL (file_attachments table). No vector embeddings of file content are generated in Phase 1.</p><h2>Planned for Phase 2:</h2><p>Collection Name: file_content_vec_v1</p><p>Vector Dimension: 1536 (OpenAI text-embedding-3-small) or 768 (sentence-transformers)</p><p>Payload Fields: file_id (string), tenant_id (string --- mandatory filter), original_name (string), entity_type (string), entity_id (string), mime_type (string), category (string), source_module (string), created_at (ISO string)</p><p>Payload Indexes: tenant_id, entity_type, mime_type, source_module, created_at</p><p>Use Case: Semantic search across extracted file content (PDF text, document text), similar file discovery, AI-powered content recommendations, duplicate detection via vector similarity rather than checksum matching.</p><p>Prerequisite: Content extraction pipeline (PDF text extraction, OCR for images, document parsing) must be implemented first to generate text from binary files before vector embedding.</p><h1>8. Object Storage</h1><h2>Folder Structure Hierarchy:</h2><p>Root: UPLOAD_DIR (default: ./uploads/, configurable via environment variable)</p><p>Pattern: {UPLOAD_DIR}/{tenant_id}/{entity_type}/{YYYY}/{MM}/{uuid}_{sanitized_name}.ext</p><p><strong>Example hierarchy:</strong></p><p>uploads/</p><p>  c0000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001/   (Tenant: Pharma Corp)</p><p>    documents/   (Entity type)</p><p>      2026/   (Year)</p><p>        02/   (Month)</p><p>          daf3c8ad-3660-4c13-8b57-0daf3cc9aaf0_test-upload.txt</p><p>          d7cbcb77-a8f1-4e22-b901-3dc77f8e22a1_report-v2.pdf</p><p>    capa/</p><p>      2026/</p><p>        02/</p><p>          f1a2b3c4-..._corrective-action-photo.jpg</p><p>    training/</p><p>      2026/</p><p>        01/</p><p>          a9b8c7d6-..._sop-training-material.pdf</p><p>  d0000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000002/   (Tenant: Another Org)</p><p>    documents/</p><p>    suppliers/</p><p>    temp/   (Temporary storage)</p><p>    thumbnails/   (Thumbnail storage)</p><h3>Storage Provider Abstraction:</h3><p>LocalStorageProvider (Phase 1 --- current):</p><p>save(relativePath, buffer) --- Creates directory tree via fs.mkdirSync({recursive: true}), writes buffer via fs.writeFileSync.</p><p>delete(relativePath) --- Removes file via fs.unlinkSync. Silently ignores if file doesn't exist.</p><p>exists(relativePath) --- Checks via fs.existsSync.</p><p>getFullPath(relativePath) --- Joins UPLOAD_DIR + relativePath. Controller uses path.resolve() for absolute path.</p><h3>S3 Migration Path (Phase 2):</h3><p>Implement S3StorageProvider with identical interface (save, delete, exists, getFullPath). S3 key structure maps 1:1 to local folder hierarchy: s3://{bucket}/{tenant_id}/{entity_type}/{YYYY}/{MM}/{uuid}_{name}.ext. No changes to service or controller code required --- swap provider via configuration.</p><h1>SECTION 10 --- REDIS CACHE DETAILS</h1><p>File Attachment Service uses 4 Redis cache key patterns with graceful degradation. The service operates normally if Redis is unavailable --- all cache operations are wrapped in try/catch and failures are logged but never block the main operation.</p><table><thead><tr><th><p><strong>Cache Key Pattern</strong></p></th><th><p><strong>Purpose</strong></p></th><th><p><strong>TTL</strong></p></th><th><p><strong>Invalidation</strong></p></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><p>file:{tenant_id}:{file_id}</p></td><td><p>Single file metadata cache</p></td><td><p>300s (5 min)</p></td><td><p>On update, delete</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>quota:{tenant_id}</p></td><td><p>Tenant storage quota</p></td><td><p>60s (1 min)</p></td><td><p>On upload, delete, quota update</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>share:{share_token}</p></td><td><p>Share token lookup</p></td><td><p>3600s (1 hr)</p></td><td><p>On revoke</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>entity_files:{entity_type}:{entity_id}</p></td><td><p>Files by entity list</p></td><td><p>120s (2 min)</p></td><td><p>On upload, delete to entity</p></td></tr></tbody></table><h3>Redis Service Integration:</h3><p>File: src/infrastructure/cache/redis.service.js --- singleton instance exported as module.exports = new RedisService().</p><p>Methods used: get(key, parseJson=true), set(key, value, ttlSeconds), delete(key). Note: The Redis service uses delete() method, NOT del().</p><p>Cache invalidation method: _invalidateCache(tenantId, fileId, entityType, entityId) --- deletes all relevant cache keys after any write operation (upload, update, delete, share revoke).</p><p>Lazy initialization: getRedis() function returns singleton Redis instance on first call, returns null if Redis is unavailable. Every cache operation checks for null before proceeding.</p><h3>Platform-level Redis usage that benefits File Attachment Service indirectly:</h3><p><strong>Session caching:</strong> JWT session data cached by AuthService for fast auth validation on every request.</p><p><strong>Permission caching:</strong> User permissions cached by PermissionService, used by authorization middleware for files:read/files:write/files:admin checks.</p><h3>Planned Phase 2 enhancements:</h3><p><strong>File content cache:</strong> Large frequently-accessed files cached in Redis for sub-millisecond retrieval.</p><p><strong>Share token bloom filter:</strong> Probabilistic check before DB lookup for invalid/revoked tokens.</p><p><strong>Upload rate limiting:</strong> Per-tenant upload rate tracking via Redis INCR + EXPIRE.</p>	Initial draft	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	4ccf68ad-485c-495a-935b-10d74b173a52	2026-03-27 14:33:43.591372+00	platform	\N
